window.MARLOW_BOOK={"note": "The Marlow Method. First draft. Every passage, poem, example sentence, and drill in this book was written for it; where a public-domain work is quoted, the author and year are named.", "words": 57969,"chapters":[
{"title": "Introduction: the 2.49", "part": "Front matter", "html": "<p>I got an 800 on the verbal section of the SAT in high school. I thought that meant I was a good reader. Then I went to West Point and finished my first year with a 2.49.<\/p>\n<p>Nothing about my reading had changed. What changed was the load. In high school I had time, and when I had time I did something on every hard passage that I could not have named. At West Point I had no time, and the thing I could not name stopped happening. I read the page, I understood the words, and I could not tell you what the page had argued.<\/p>\n<p>What fixed it was not effort. It was noticing, slowly and then all at once, what I had been doing when it worked. On every hard sentence I was finding the one word doing most of the work. On every passage I was finding the one sentence the rest depended on. And before I decided what a sentence said, I was asking a question I had never put into words: how far does this go? Does it say the thing happened, or that it probably happened, or that someone else thinks it happened?<\/p>\n<p>Once I could see the moves, I could do them on purpose, under load, at two in the morning. I made the Dean's List every semester after that. The Academy made me a writing tutor, and I found that the moves could be taught in an afternoon and learned in a season. Later, in the Army, I taught them to drill sergeants who had been out of school for a decade and to soldiers leaving the service for college. Every one of them who enrolled made the Dean's List. The method was never mine. It was just written down.<\/p>\n<p>This book is the method. Part One is the three moves, in twelve chapters. Part Two is what actually moves a reading score, in seven, because knowing the moves is not the same as training them. Part Three applies the moves to data, to the GRE and GMAT, and to the essay. Part Four steps back to the whole passage: how it is built, why each detail is there, and who is telling the story. Part Five reads by domain: the science passage, the social science passage, the historical document, and the poem. Part Six is for parents and Part Seven is for tutors. Part Eight takes the digital SAT closely: reading on a screen, transitions, rhetorical synthesis, and the grammar of sentence boundaries. Part Nine is the long reads: how to read a whole hard book, and a guide to six worth reading. The appendix holds the method on one page, the answer key, two practice sections, thirty sentences to unpack, a twelve-week plan, every question family by test, the full root table, the word banks, and a glossary.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The three moves<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Find the sentence that matters.<\/li>\n<li>Ask how far it goes.<\/li>\n<li>Write down why.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Every chapter teaches one thing that makes one of these moves harder: a qualifier, a negation, a reporting verb, a cause that is only a sequence, a writer who does not mean it. Each chapter has a passage written for it, a walk through what a fast reader does and what the move does instead, and a drill of five items. The answers are in the appendix. Do the drill before you look.<\/p>\n<p><strong>How to use the book<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>If you are a student, read one chapter a week and do the drill twice: once the day you read the chapter, once a week later with the chapter closed. The second time is the one that counts.<\/p>\n<p>If you are a parent, read chapters 1, 2, and 5, which are the three most students are missing, and then read the Friday report with those chapters in mind. You will recognize what the tutor is saying.<\/p>\n<p>If you are a tutor, know all twelve cold. The lesson library in the workspace is built on them, and the eight errors in the appendix are the names you will use with every student.<\/p>\n<p>One warning. The method is simple and it is not fast. The first few weeks it will slow you down, because you are doing on purpose what you used to do by accident or not at all. That is the point. Speed comes back on its own once the moves are automatic, and it comes back with the score attached.<\/p>"},
{"title": "1. The sentence that matters", "part": "Part One: The moves", "html": "<p>Strong readers guess. That is the whole problem, and it is worth saying plainly because nobody tells strong readers this. A student who decodes perfectly, reads fast, and has a good vocabulary will, on a demanding passage, choose the answer that sounds most like the passage. Most of the time it is right. On a test built by people who know this, it is right most of the time and wrong on exactly the questions the test uses to separate the 600s from the 700s.<\/p>\n<p>The habit that stops it costs about four seconds a question. Before you choose an answer, find the sentence that would have to be true for the answer to be right. Put your finger on it. Then choose.<\/p>\n<p><strong>A passage<\/strong><\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Most of the bridges in the county were built in the same decade, by the same crew, from the same plans. For fifty years that uniformity was an advantage: a part that fit one bridge fit all of them. It has become the opposite. The bridges are aging together, and the county's budget cannot replace twelve at once. The engineer's report recommends a schedule that repairs the most-traveled bridges first and lets two of the least-traveled close, a choice that is cheaper on paper and harder in the towns that lose them.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p><strong>The question<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Which statement best describes the passage's main point?<\/p>\n<p>A. The county's bridges were poorly built. B. An advantage of uniform construction has become a liability as the bridges age at once. C. The engineer's schedule is unfair to small towns. D. The county should raise its budget to replace all twelve bridges.<\/p>\n<p><strong>What a fast reader does<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Reads the passage, feels that it is about bridges and money and small towns, and looks at the choices. C sounds like the last sentence. D sounds like a reasonable thing to think. B is long. A fast reader picks C because \"harder in the towns that lose them\" is the most emotional phrase in the passage and it is the last thing they read.<\/p>\n<p><strong>What the move does<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Ask: which sentence would have to be true for each choice to be right? For A, a sentence saying the bridges were badly built. There is none; \"same plans\" is not \"bad plans.\" For C, a sentence saying the schedule is unfair. The passage says \"harder,\" which is a cost, not a judgment. For D, a sentence recommending a bigger budget. None. For B, sentences 2 and 3: the uniformity was an advantage; it has become the opposite. Finger on sentence 3. Choose B.<\/p>\n<p>Notice what happened. The right answer is the one you can point to. The wrong answers are the ones you can only feel.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Why it works<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>A passage on a reading test is built around one or two sentences that carry the claim, surrounded by sentences that set it up, qualify it, or give an example. The question writer knows which sentence carries the claim and writes the right answer from it. The wrong answers are written from the other sentences, which is why they sound right: they are in the passage too. They are just not the sentence the question is about.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>For each item, write the number of the sentence that the correct answer depends on. Do not answer the question; find the sentence.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>(Bridges passage) The passage suggests that the county's problem is one of A. engineering. B. timing. C. politics. D. corruption.<\/li>\n<li>(Bridges passage) The word \"opposite\" in sentence 3 most directly refers to A. a bridge that fails. B. the uniformity becoming a disadvantage. C. the budget running out. D. towns losing bridges.<\/li>\n<li>(Bridges passage) The engineer's report would most likely be opposed by A. the crew that built the bridges. B. residents of the least-traveled towns. C. the county's accountants. D. the most-traveled towns.<\/li>\n<li>(Bridges passage) The phrase \"cheaper on paper\" implies that A. the report contains an arithmetic error. B. the real costs include something the budget does not count. C. the schedule will save no money. D. the engineer is lying.<\/li>\n<li>Write one sentence stating the passage's main point in your own words, and then write the number of the sentence you would cite for it.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Watch for<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The error this chapter fixes is <em>evidence that does not support the answer<\/em>: a correct choice selected for the wrong reason, or a sentence highlighted that establishes something else. It is the most common pattern in our diagnostic, and it is invisible to the student, because the answer was right. The workspace catches it by asking for the sentence every time.<\/p>"},
{"title": "2. Small words, large effects", "part": "Part One: The moves", "html": "<p>Every hard sentence has one word doing most of the work. Usually it is a small one: nearly, only, unless, rather than, alone, most, at least. Find that word and the sentence opens. Miss it and you will answer a question about a sentence that was never written.<\/p>\n<p><strong>A sentence<\/strong><\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The district found that nearly every school that adopted the later start saw attendance improve, although in most cases the improvement could not be separated from other changes made the same year.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>Read it once, the way you would on a test. Most students come away with \"later start, better attendance.\" That is what the first half says, and the first half is where attention goes.<\/p>\n<p>But the sentence has three small words doing most of the work. <em>Nearly<\/em> tells you some schools did not improve. <em>Although<\/em> tells you the second half is about to take something back. <em>Most<\/em> tells you that in the typical case, the district itself could not credit the later start. So the sentence does not say the later start improved attendance. It says attendance improved, and that nobody knows why.<\/p>\n<p>A test question about this sentence will offer \"the later start improved attendance\" as a choice, because that is what a fast reader believes the sentence says. The right answer will be the one that sounds weaker.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The qualifiers, sorted by what they do<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Kind<\/th>\n<th>Words<\/th>\n<th>What they do to the claim<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Quantity<\/td>\n<td>some, many, most, nearly all, all; few, several, a handful<\/td>\n<td>Set how many. \"Most\" is not \"all,\" and the gap between them is where wrong answers live<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Degree<\/td>\n<td>nearly, almost, largely, partly, somewhat, roughly, about<\/td>\n<td>Set how much. \"Nearly vanished\" means a little remained<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Limit<\/td>\n<td>only, alone, merely, just, solely<\/td>\n<td>Shrink the claim to one thing. \"The bell alone\" rules out the bell as the sole cause, not the bell as a cause<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Condition<\/td>\n<td>unless, if, provided, as long as, except<\/td>\n<td>Set a default and one exception. \"Unless the holds problem worsens\" means the policy continues by default<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Concession<\/td>\n<td>although, though, while, even if, despite<\/td>\n<td>Take something back. Whatever follows is the part that limits the claim<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Contrast<\/td>\n<td>but, however, yet, rather than, instead<\/td>\n<td>Turn the sentence against what came before. \"Rather than\" sets two things against each other and picks one<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Time<\/td>\n<td>for now, so far, still, no longer, yet<\/td>\n<td>Make the claim provisional. \"For now\" means it may not hold<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>A passage<\/strong><\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Two years ago the Marlow Public Library stopped charging late fees on children's books, and last spring it extended the policy to almost everything else. The director expected a modest rise in overdue items and got one: returns now arrive, on average, four days later than before. What she did not expect was the change in who came through the door. Families who had let their cards lapse over fines of only a few dollars began returning, and in the neighborhoods nearest the branch, card registrations rose by at least a third. Not every number moved the right way. A small set of popular titles now spends most of the year checked out, and the holds list for them has roughly doubled. The board has said it will keep the policy unless the holds problem worsens, though it has not said what worsening would mean. For now, the director says, a library that is used more and returned to later is a trade she will take.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p><strong>What the move does<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Circle the qualifiers. You should have at least ten: almost, modest, on average, only, nearest, at least, not every, small, most, roughly, unless, though, for now. Now ask of each one what it takes away.<\/p>\n<p><em>Almost everything else<\/em>: some items still carry fees. <em>Only a few dollars<\/em>: the fines were small, which is the point of dropping them. <em>Nearest the branch<\/em>: the registration number applies to the closest neighborhoods, not the town. <em>At least a third<\/em>: a floor, not a measurement. <em>Unless<\/em>: the policy continues by default; one named condition would end it. <em>Though<\/em>: the board did not define the condition. <em>For now<\/em>: the director's judgment is provisional.<\/p>\n<p>A claim that the policy \"worked\" is too strong. A claim that it \"brought families back near the branch, at a cost in late returns and holds, and the board has not decided what would make it stop\" is exactly as strong as the passage.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>For each sentence, circle the qualifier, write what it removes, and rewrite the sentence without it so you can feel the difference.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>The survey found that most residents who responded supported the lane, although fewer than a third of residents responded.<\/li>\n<li>The medication reduced symptoms in nearly all patients, but only for about six hours.<\/li>\n<li>The company has not yet reported a loss, which is not the same as having reported a profit.<\/li>\n<li>Unless the river rises above the second mark, the bridge stays open.<\/li>\n<li>The play was, for the most part, well received, except by the critics who had seen the original.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Watch for<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The error this chapter fixes is <em>overlooking qualifying language<\/em>: reading \"nearly\" as \"completely,\" \"most\" as \"all,\" \"unless\" as \"because.\" On our diagnostic it is the single most common pattern among fluent readers with stalled scores, and it is the fastest to fix, because the habit is mechanical: circle the small word, ask what it takes away.<\/p>"},
{"title": "3. No, not, never", "part": "Part One: The moves", "html": "<p>Negation is where fast readers lose the thread without noticing. A sentence that says what did not happen reads, at speed, like a sentence about what happened, because the eye lands on the nouns and verbs and skips the \"not.\" Reference words are the other half of the problem: <em>this<\/em>, <em>that<\/em>, <em>which<\/em>, <em>it<\/em>, <em>the former<\/em>, <em>such a reading<\/em> all point at something earlier, and if you do not stop to find what they point at, you are reading a sentence with a hole in it.<\/p>\n<p><strong>A passage<\/strong><\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The committee did not reject the proposal, which is not to say it approved it. It declined to vote, citing a procedural question that no member could later explain. Supporters took the delay as a sign of weakness in the opposition; opponents took it as proof that the proposal could not survive a vote. Neither reading is obviously wrong, and that is precisely the problem with a decision not to decide: it confirms whatever the reader brought to it.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p><strong>What a fast reader does<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Comes away with \"the committee rejected the proposal\" or \"the committee approved the proposal,\" depending on which clause they landed on. Then reads \"neither reading is obviously wrong\" as the author agreeing with someone.<\/p>\n<p><strong>What the move does<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>For every <em>not<\/em>, <em>no<\/em>, <em>never<\/em>, <em>neither<\/em>, <em>declined<\/em>, <em>failed to<\/em>, write down in plain words what did happen. Sentence 1: it did not reject, and it did not approve. Sentence 2: it did not vote. So what happened is nothing, on a procedural question nobody could explain.<\/p>\n<p>For every reference word, draw the arrow. <em>Which<\/em> (sentence 1) points at \"did not reject.\" <em>It<\/em> (sentence 2) is the committee. <em>The delay<\/em> (sentence 3) is the decision not to vote. <em>Neither reading<\/em> (sentence 4) is the supporters' reading and the opponents' reading. <em>That<\/em> (sentence 4) is the fact that neither is obviously wrong. <em>It<\/em> (end of sentence 4) is the decision not to decide.<\/p>\n<p>Now the last sentence is readable: a non-decision confirms whatever the reader already believed. That is the author's own point, and it is critical of both sides, not supportive of either.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Double negation<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Tests love it because fast readers cancel the wrong pair. \"Not uncommon\" means common. \"Not without merit\" means has some merit, and the hedge matters: it is weaker than \"has merit.\" \"It is not the case that none of the schools improved\" means at least one improved. Rule: count the negatives, resolve them one at a time, and then ask whether the result is as strong as the plain version. It usually is not; a writer who says \"not without merit\" is choosing to say less than \"has merit.\"<\/p>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Rewrite each sentence as a plain statement of what is true, with every reference word replaced by what it refers to.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>The study did not find that the program failed; it found that the data could not show whether it succeeded.<\/li>\n<li>Neither the engineer's count nor the merchants' estimate was wrong, but neither answered the question the council had asked, which was about Saturdays.<\/li>\n<li>It is not that the river never floods the lower road; it is that it has not done so since the culvert was widened, a change the report credits for this.<\/li>\n<li>Few of the objections were new, and none that were new were serious.<\/li>\n<li>The author does not deny that trees cool the street by day; what she denies is that this is the whole story, and the former claim is the one the officials quoted.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Watch for<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>There is no single named error for this chapter; negation and reference failures show up as <em>overclaiming<\/em> (the student states the positive version of a negated claim) and as <em>confusing the author's view with a reported one<\/em> (the student attaches a reference word to the wrong speaker). The fix is the same in both cases: slow down at every <em>not<\/em> and every <em>this<\/em>, and write the plain version in the margin.<\/p>"},
{"title": "4. After is not because", "part": "Part One: The moves", "html": "<p>The most expensive mistake a reader makes is to turn a sequence into a cause. A passage says that one thing happened and then another thing happened. The reader writes that the first thing caused the second. Test writers build a wrong answer out of exactly that move on nearly every passage that reports a study, a policy, or a change.<\/p>\n<p><strong>A passage<\/strong><\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>In the fall of 2019, Harlow High School moved its first bell from 7:20 to 8:30. Administrators hoped the change would reduce the chronic absences that had troubled the school for a decade. By spring, absences had fallen by nearly a fifth. During the same semester, however, the district also launched an attendance outreach program that assigned a counselor to call the family of any student who missed two consecutive days. The program reached about four hundred families in its first year. Several teachers credited the calls, rather than the later bell, for the improvement. The district's own report declined to choose between the two explanations. It noted that the two changes began within three weeks of each other and that no school in the district had adopted one without the other. Any claim that the later bell alone improved attendance, the report concluded, goes beyond what the evidence can show.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p><strong>What a fast reader does<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Reads sentences 1 to 3 and has the story: later bell, fewer absences, done. The rest of the passage is detail. Asked why absences fell, writes \"the later start time caused attendance to improve because students got more sleep.\" Sleep appears nowhere in the passage.<\/p>\n<p><strong>What the move does<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Three questions, in order.<\/p>\n<p><em>What happened?<\/em> Absences fell by nearly a fifth. That is a fact in the passage, and the only one about the outcome.<\/p>\n<p><em>What else changed at the same time?<\/em> The outreach program, within three weeks. Sentence 4 begins with <em>however<\/em>, which is the passage telling you the story just got complicated.<\/p>\n<p><em>Is there a comparison?<\/em> No. Sentence 8: no school adopted one change without the other. Without a school that got the bell and not the calls, or the calls and not the bell, nothing in the passage can separate them. The report says so in sentence 9, and it says it precisely: a bell-alone claim goes <em>beyond<\/em> the evidence. It does not say the bell did nothing. It says the evidence cannot show it did.<\/p>\n<p>The calibrated claim: absences fell after the later bell, but the passage cannot show the bell caused it, because a second change began at the same time and no school had one without the other.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The three shapes of a causal passage<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Shape<\/th>\n<th>What it looks like<\/th>\n<th>What you may claim<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Sequence<\/td>\n<td>A happened, then B happened<\/td>\n<td>A came before B. Nothing more<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Correlation<\/td>\n<td>Places or people with more A tend to have more B<\/td>\n<td>A and B travel together; either could cause the other, or a third thing could cause both<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Comparison<\/td>\n<td>Places with A and without A, otherwise alike, differ in B<\/td>\n<td>A is the better explanation for B, within the limits the passage states<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Most test passages are the first two shapes dressed as the third. The question is always: where is the group that did not get A?<\/p>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>For each, write the strongest claim about cause the passage allows, in one sentence.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>The town replaced its streetlights with dimmer ones and residents of those blocks reported sleeping better; residents of unchanged blocks, who read the same news coverage, reported no change.<\/li>\n<li>Students who ate breakfast scored higher on the morning test than students who did not.<\/li>\n<li>After the library dropped late fees, registrations near the branch rose by a third; the branch also extended its weekend hours that spring.<\/li>\n<li>In the forty neighborhoods studied, tree cover and daytime temperature moved in opposite directions; the authors note that the leafiest neighborhoods were also the wealthiest.<\/li>\n<li>The clinic's wait times fell after it hired a second receptionist and, the same month, stopped accepting walk-ins.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Watch for<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The error this chapter fixes is <em>inference goes beyond the evidence<\/em>, the overclaim. Its signature in a student's writing is the word <em>caused<\/em> with no comparison group in sight, and its cousin is <em>outside knowledge substituted for the passage<\/em>: the sleep that was never mentioned. The coach flags both by name.<\/p>"},
{"title": "5. Whose view is this?", "part": "Part One: The moves", "html": "<p>A passage is full of voices. The author reports what officials said, what a study found, what critics argued, what supporters claimed, and somewhere in the middle of all that, usually in one sentence, says what the author thinks. Fast readers hear one voice. They attribute everything to \"the passage,\" and then a question asks what the author believes, and they answer with what a merchant said in sentence 2.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The reporting verbs<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The verb that introduces a claim tells you who owns it and often what the author thinks of it.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Verb<\/th>\n<th>Who owns the claim<\/th>\n<th>What it tells you<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>said, stated, noted<\/td>\n<td>The speaker<\/td>\n<td>Neutral. The author is reporting, not endorsing<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>argued, contended, insisted, maintained<\/td>\n<td>The speaker<\/td>\n<td>A position in a dispute; expect an answer from someone<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>claimed, asserted, alleged<\/td>\n<td>The speaker<\/td>\n<td>The author is marking some distance; the claim is unproven<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>warned, predicted, feared<\/td>\n<td>The speaker<\/td>\n<td>A forecast with a stance; not a fact<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>suggested, proposed, speculated<\/td>\n<td>The speaker<\/td>\n<td>Tentative; weaker than argued<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>conceded, admitted, acknowledged<\/td>\n<td>The speaker, against their own side<\/td>\n<td>The speaker is giving ground. Look for what their argument loses<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>countered, replied, objected<\/td>\n<td>The speaker, against someone else<\/td>\n<td>A dispute is under way; the two sentences are in tension<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>credited, blamed, attributed<\/td>\n<td>The speaker<\/td>\n<td>A causal judgment that is theirs, not the author's<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>found, showed, demonstrated<\/td>\n<td>A study or the evidence<\/td>\n<td>Strong; the author is treating it as established<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>(no verb; a plain statement)<\/td>\n<td>The author<\/td>\n<td>The author's own voice. This is the sentence the question is about<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>A passage<\/strong><\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>At Tuesday's meeting the Fenwick town council heard three hours of comment on a plan to remove parking from one side of Market Street to make room for a protected bike lane. Merchants along the street warned that customers would go elsewhere, and one bakery owner estimated that half her weekday trade arrives by car. A traffic engineer hired by the town countered that most of those spaces sit empty before ten in the morning and after six at night. Supporters of the lane pointed to a neighboring town where a similar change was followed by a rise in foot traffic, though the councilwoman who raised it conceded that the two streets are not much alike. The mayor, who had not taken a position, said he found the engineer's numbers persuasive but the merchants' fears reasonable. It is tempting to describe the evening as a contest between data and anecdote. It was not quite that; the engineer's count said nothing about who parks in the spaces that are full, and the merchants' estimates were not counts at all. The council will vote in March. Whatever it decides, Market Street will be the first place in Fenwick where the town has chosen between two groups of people who both, by any fair reading, had a point.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p><strong>What the move does<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Underline every reporting verb and write the speaker above it. <em>Warned<\/em>: merchants. <em>Estimated<\/em>: the bakery owner. <em>Countered<\/em>: the engineer. <em>Pointed to<\/em>: supporters. <em>Conceded<\/em>: the councilwoman. <em>Said<\/em>: the mayor. Then find the sentences with no reporting verb and no named speaker. Sentences 6, 7, and 9. That is the author.<\/p>\n<p>Now the author's view is readable, and it is not on either side. Sentence 7 says the engineer's count has a hole in it (who parks in the full spaces) and the merchants' numbers are guesses. Sentence 9 says both sides had a point. A question that asks what the author believes about the engineer's data has one right answer: it is useful and incomplete. A fast reader who heard the engineer's voice as the passage's voice will answer that the data settled the question.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The concession<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><em>Conceded<\/em> is the most valuable verb on the list because it tells you a speaker gave ground on their own side. The councilwoman offered the neighboring town as evidence and then conceded the streets are not alike. Her comparison is now weaker, by her own admission. A reader who catches the concession knows the supporters' best evidence has a crack in it; a reader who misses it thinks the supporters won the exchange.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>For each sentence, name who owns the claim and whether the author endorses it, distances from it, or stays neutral.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Critics of the program have alleged that its costs were understated.<\/li>\n<li>The study found that attendance rose, and the superintendent attributed the rise to the later start.<\/li>\n<li>It is often said that the river is cleaner than it was; the data from the last three summers do not bear this out.<\/li>\n<li>The engineer conceded that the count did not cover Saturdays.<\/li>\n<li>The board insists the policy is working. Perhaps. The holds list has doubled.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Watch for<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The error this chapter fixes is <em>confusing the author's view with a view the author reports<\/em>. It is the hardest of the eight to fix because the student is reading accurately; they are just assigning the claim to the wrong person. The cure is mechanical: the verb, then the speaker, then the sentences with no speaker at all.<\/p>"},
{"title": "6. Irony and distance", "part": "Part One: The moves", "html": "<p>Sometimes the writer does not mean it. A sentence says one thing and the passage around it says the writer believes the opposite, or believes it less, or is holding the statement at arm's length to look at it. Tests call this tone, and they test it constantly, because it is the one thing a fast reader cannot do at speed: you have to hear the sentence, not just decode it.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Three kinds of distance<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><em>Verbal irony<\/em>: the words say the opposite of what is meant. \"The envelope was thin and polite\" describes a rejection letter; <em>polite<\/em> is doing the work of <em>cold<\/em>.<\/p>\n<p><em>Understatement<\/em>: the words say less than is meant, and the gap is the point. \"Not every number moved the right way\" introduces a holds list that doubled.<\/p>\n<p><em>Reported distance<\/em>: the writer quotes or paraphrases a position in order to examine it, not to hold it. \"It is tempting to describe the evening as a contest between data and anecdote\" is a sentence the author writes in order to reject it in the next one. <em>Tempting<\/em> is the signal: the writer is about to resist.<\/p>\n<p><strong>A passage<\/strong><\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Nadia told everyone she had not entered the essay contest for the prize, and for most of the spring she believed it. She had entered, she said, because her teacher had asked, and because the topic, what a town owes its river, was one she had opinions about. When the first envelope came, thin and polite, she read it twice on the porch and then went inside and reorganized the spice drawer. Her brother, who had never been fooled by her, asked whether the spices had done something to deserve it. She said that she was simply glad to have written the thing, which was true, and that she did not care about a committee's opinion, which was less true than she would have liked.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p><strong>What a fast reader does<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Takes Nadia at her word. She did not enter for the prize, she is glad to have written it, she does not care. Asked how Nadia feels about the rejection, answers \"relieved\" or \"indifferent,\" because those are the words on the page.<\/p>\n<p><strong>What the move does<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Ask of every statement: does the passage around it agree? Sentence 1 says she believed it <em>for most of the spring<\/em>, which means not all of it. Sentence 3 shows her reorganizing a spice drawer after reading a letter twice, which is not what indifference does. Sentence 4 has her brother, <em>who had never been fooled by her<\/em>, making a joke whose whole point is that she is upset. Sentence 5 says outright that one claim was true and the other <em>less true than she would have liked<\/em>.<\/p>\n<p>So the narrator is telling you, through action and through the brother and finally in plain words, that Nadia cares and will not say so. The tone is dry, affectionate, and a little amused at her. A question about her feelings has one supported answer: more disappointed than she admits. The words \"relieved\" and \"indifferent\" are what she says, not what the passage shows.<\/p>\n<p><strong>How to hear it<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Three signals that a writer is at a distance from a statement: a qualifier on the belief itself (\"for most of the spring she believed it\"); an action that contradicts the statement (the spice drawer); and a second character or the narrator stepping in to correct it (the brother, \"less true than she would have liked\"). When two of the three are present, read the statement as ironic. When none are, read it straight. Most test errors on tone come from reading an ironic line straight; a few come from reading a straight line as ironic because the student has been told to look for irony. The passage decides, not the instruction.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>For each, say whether the quoted line is meant straight, ironic, or understated, and name the signal in the surrounding sentences that tells you.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>\"The meeting was productive,\" the chair said afterward, to a room that had agreed on nothing and adjourned an hour early.<\/li>\n<li>The report calls the shortfall \"manageable.\" The next paragraph proposes closing two schools.<\/li>\n<li>She said the river was the best thing about the town, and she said it every morning from the bridge, in all weathers, for eleven years.<\/li>\n<li>It would be unfair to say the plan has no supporters. It has three, and two of them wrote it.<\/li>\n<li>The engineer's numbers were, the mayor said, persuasive. He then voted against the lane.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Watch for<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Tone errors show up in the diagnostic as <em>inference goes beyond the evidence<\/em> in reverse: the student stops at the words and never gets to what the words are doing. The fix is to read the sentence aloud, which is why the workspace has a listen button. Irony is easier to hear than to see.<\/p>"},
{"title": "7. Claim, evidence, warrant", "part": "Part One: The moves", "html": "<p>Every argument has three parts, and most readers can name two. The claim is what the writer wants you to accept. The evidence is what the writer offers in support. The warrant is the reason the evidence supports the claim, and it is almost never stated. It is also where every argument is weak, because a warrant is a claim about the world that the evidence does not itself prove.<\/p>\n<p>The move in this chapter is to write the warrant down. If you cannot, the argument is a guess with a quotation attached.<\/p>\n<p><strong>A passage<\/strong><\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A town replaced the sodium streetlights on six residential blocks with dimmer, warmer LEDs and left the rest unchanged. Residents of the six blocks reported sleeping better in a survey the following spring. A researcher argues that the change in lighting, rather than the publicity around it, explains the reports, because residents of the unchanged blocks, who read the same news coverage, reported no change in their sleep.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p><strong>The three parts<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><em>Claim<\/em>: lighting, not publicity, explains the sleep reports.<\/p>\n<p><em>Evidence<\/em>: residents of the unchanged blocks, with the same publicity and no new lights, reported no change.<\/p>\n<p><em>Warrant<\/em>: if publicity were the cause, the unchanged blocks would show the effect too; they do not, so what differs between the groups (the lights) is the better explanation.<\/p>\n<p>Now the argument can be tested. Is the warrant true? Mostly. It assumes the two groups are alike except for the lights, which a test question can probe: what if the six blocks were chosen because residents had complained about sleep? Then the groups differ in more than lighting, and the warrant weakens. The passage does not say how the blocks were chosen. A good reader notices the gap; a question writer builds an answer choice on it.<\/p>\n<p><strong>How far the evidence reaches<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Evidence has a reach, and a claim that extends past it is an overclaim. The survey here reaches as far as: self-reported sleep, one spring, six blocks, compared with unchanged blocks. It does not reach: measured sleep, other towns, the following year, or health. A claim that \"warmer streetlights improve health\" is three steps past the evidence. A claim that \"on these blocks, in this survey, the lights are a better explanation than the publicity\" is exactly as far as it reaches.<\/p>\n<p>The calibration ladder in the appendix is the tool. <em>Suggests<\/em> is one rung; <em>shows<\/em> is higher; <em>proves<\/em> is the top, and almost no passage on a test earns it. Match the verb in your claim to the reach of the evidence.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Writing it<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The sentence shape that always works: <em>Claim, because evidence, which matters because warrant.<\/em> \"The lights are the better explanation, because the unchanged blocks with the same publicity reported no change, which matters because publicity alone would have reached them too.\" Three clauses. If the third clause will not come, you do not have an argument yet.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>For each item, write the claim, the evidence, and the warrant in three short lines, then say how far the evidence reaches.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>The library's registrations near the branch rose by a third after fees were dropped; the director says the policy brought families back.<\/li>\n<li>Students who used the workspace three times a week gained more than students who used it once; the company says practice volume drives the gain.<\/li>\n<li>The neighboring town's foot traffic rose after its bike lane; supporters say Market Street's would too.<\/li>\n<li>Tree cover and daytime temperature move in opposite directions across forty neighborhoods; the authors say trees cool streets by day.<\/li>\n<li>The guild's members paid more tax after 1640 than before; the historian says the guild did not decline.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Watch for<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The error this chapter fixes is the quiet one: <em>a quotation that is relevant but does not establish the conclusion<\/em>. The student has evidence and a claim and no warrant, so the quotation sits beside the claim without touching it. The coach's line for this is \"your quotation supports this part of your claim; what supports the rest?\"<\/p>"},
{"title": "8. The strongest objection", "part": "Part One: The moves", "html": "<p>An argument that ignores the best case against it is not an argument; it is a speech. Good writers raise the objection themselves, grant what must be granted, and answer the rest. Good readers find that moment, because it is where the writer's real position lives, and it is where tests ask their hardest questions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Three moves a writer makes with an objection<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><em>Counterevidence<\/em>: a fact that cuts against the claim, stated plainly. \"Not every number moved the right way.\"<\/p>\n<p><em>Concession<\/em>: granting a point to the other side. \"The two streets are not much alike.\" Watch what the argument loses when the point is granted; usually it is the strength of one piece of evidence, not the whole claim.<\/p>\n<p><em>Rebuttal<\/em>: answering the objection. \"The engineer's count said nothing about who parks in the spaces that are full.\" A rebuttal narrows the objection rather than denying it; the strongest rebuttals say \"true, and here is why it does not decide the question.\"<\/p>\n<p><strong>A passage<\/strong><\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The case for charging for downtown parking is simple: free spaces fill with employees who arrive at eight and leave at five, and the customers the merchants want cannot find a spot. Charge a dollar an hour and the employees move to the lot behind the courthouse, where parking is free, and the spaces turn over. Merchants object that customers will go to the mall, where parking costs nothing. They are right that some will. But the mall has always had free parking, and the customers who were going to choose it on that basis chose it years ago. The ones still downtown are there for something the mall does not have, and a dollar an hour will not change that. What would change it is continuing to let them circle the block looking for a space that an employee took at eight.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p><strong>What a fast reader does<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Reads \"merchants object that customers will go to the mall\" and \"they are right that some will,\" and concludes the author is unsure, or is on the merchants' side, or thinks the plan is risky. Asked for the author's position, hedges.<\/p>\n<p><strong>What the move does<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Map it. Claim: charge for parking so spaces turn over. Evidence: employees fill free spaces all day. Objection (raised by the author, in the merchants' voice): customers will go to the mall. Concession: some will. Rebuttal: the ones who would leave over parking already left; the ones who remain are there for something else; the real risk is the status quo.<\/p>\n<p>The author's position is not hedged at all. It is stronger for the concession, because the concession is small and the rebuttal turns the objection around: the objection's own logic (customers care about parking) is used to argue that circling the block is worse than paying a dollar. A question asking why the author grants that \"some will\" leave has one right answer: to narrow the objection before answering it. A fast reader answers that the author is admitting doubt.<\/p>\n<p><strong>How to read a concession<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Ask three things. What exactly is granted? (Some customers will leave.) What is kept? (Most will not, and the ones who leave were already lost.) What does the rebuttal do with the objection? (Turns it into evidence for the claim.) A concession that grants a little and keeps a lot is a sign of a confident writer. A concession that grants the main point and keeps a detail is a writer losing the argument while sounding reasonable, and tests love to ask which is which.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>For each, find the objection, say what is conceded and what is kept, and state whether the rebuttal narrows the objection, denies it, or turns it.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Critics say the later bell costs families an hour of childcare in the morning. It does, for families with a working parent and no older sibling, which the district estimates at one household in six. For the other five, the bell is free. For the one, the district has proposed a supervised early room, which costs less than the absences the bell prevents.<\/li>\n<li>The holds list doubling is a real cost, and the board is right to watch it. It is also a list of people who want to read, which was the point.<\/li>\n<li>Supporters pointed to a neighboring town. The councilwoman conceded the streets are not alike. She did not withdraw the comparison; she said the question was whether the differences cut for or against the lane, and nobody at the meeting could say.<\/li>\n<li>It is true that the survey measured only what residents said about their sleep. But what residents say about their sleep is what the lighting was meant to change.<\/li>\n<li>Yes, the guild's records thin out after 1640. So do everyone's; the courthouse burned in 1652.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Watch for<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The error this chapter fixes is <em>the response ignores counterevidence<\/em>: a claim written as if the objection in the passage did not exist. In the workspace, a claim task on a passage with a concession will not pass unless the claim accounts for it. In writing, the fix is to put the objection in your own second sentence, before anyone else does.<\/p>"},
{"title": "9. Reading literature as argument", "part": "Part One: The moves", "html": "<p>Students who are good at nonfiction often go to pieces on a poem or a story, because they have been told literature is about feelings and interpretation is free. It is not. A story or a poem makes a claim the way an essay does; it just makes it with a tone shift instead of a thesis, a motive instead of a reason, an image instead of a statistic. The same three moves apply. Find the line that matters. Ask how far it goes. Write down why.<\/p>\n<p><strong>A poem<\/strong><\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>River, Late<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>All spring I kept my ledger of the river: the days it rose, the days it gave back ground, the trash it carried, every inch I owed it for the slow brown water passing through the town.<\/p>\n<p>And then one evening, walking with no notebook, I watched it go the way it always went, and could not think of one thing it had promised, or one thing, in the end, that I had meant.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p><strong>The line that matters<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>In a poem of two stanzas, the line that matters is almost always the first line of the second, because that is where the turn is. <em>And then one evening, walking with no notebook.<\/em> Everything before it is one state; everything after is another. Find the turn and you have the poem's argument: the thing that changes between stanza one and stanza two.<\/p>\n<p><strong>What changes<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Stanza one is bookkeeping. <em>Ledger<\/em>, <em>owed<\/em>, <em>every inch<\/em>: the speaker treats the river as a debt to be tracked. Stanza two removes the notebook, and with it the debt. The river <em>promised<\/em> nothing; the speaker <em>meant<\/em> nothing by the ledger either. The poem's claim, if you had to write it as a sentence: the accounting was mine, not the river's, and letting it go cost me the meaning I had put there.<\/p>\n<p>Notice how far that goes and no further. The poem does not say the speaker is at peace; the last line admits a loss. It does not say the river is indifferent; it says the speaker could not think of one thing it had promised, which is about the speaker's expectation. A reading that says \"the speaker learns to accept nature\" is an overclaim in the same way \"the bell improved attendance\" is. The text does not reach it.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Competing readings<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Literature allows more than one supported reading, and tests know it. The question is never which reading is right; it is which reading the text supports more. Two readings of this poem: (a) release from a self-imposed burden; (b) loss of a meaning the speaker had made. Both are supported. Which is stronger? Count the lines. <em>Could not think of one thing it had promised<\/em> supports (a). <em>Or one thing, in the end, that I had meant<\/em> supports (b), and it is the last line, which is where a poem puts its weight. (b) is stronger, and (a) is not wrong. A good answer says both of those things.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Tone, motive, image: the three handles<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><em>Tone shift<\/em>: find the line where the temperature changes. In the Nadia story it is the spice drawer; in the poem it is the notebook. The shift is the argument.<\/p>\n<p><em>Motive<\/em>: what a character wants, as shown by what they do, not what they say. Nadia says she does not care and reorganizes a drawer. The action is the evidence; the statement is the claim to be tested.<\/p>\n<p><em>Image as claim<\/em>: a description doing the work of a statement. <em>The slow brown water passing through the town<\/em> is not scenery. It is the river declining to be anything other than what it is, which is the poem's point before the poem makes it.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>(River, Late) Which single word in stanza one most establishes the speaker's attitude toward the river? Say why.<\/li>\n<li>(River, Late) State the two competing readings in one sentence each and name the line that supports each.<\/li>\n<li>(Nadia) The brother is a foil. In one sentence, what does his presence let the reader see that Nadia's own words hide?<\/li>\n<li>Write one sentence describing the tone of \"thin and polite\" and name the second word in the passage that confirms it.<\/li>\n<li>(River, Late) A student writes: \"The poem shows that nature teaches us to let go of material concerns.\" Name the two ways this overclaims.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Watch for<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>On literature, the two errors that dominate are <em>inference beyond the evidence<\/em> (the moral the poem never states) and <em>outside knowledge substituted for the passage<\/em> (what rivers usually symbolize, imported from another class). The fix is the same as in chapter 1: put your finger on the line.<\/p>"},
{"title": "10. Two passages, one question", "part": "Part One: The moves", "html": "<p>Paired passages are two voices on one subject, and the questions about them test whether you can hold both voices apart. The trap is to merge them into \"the passages say,\" which is a sentence no test ever rewards. The move is to build a small table before you read a single question: what each author claims, what each offers as evidence, and where they agree, disagree, and talk past each other.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Two passages<\/strong><\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Passage A.<\/strong> The dimming of streetlights after midnight has been sold as a savings measure, and it is one: the utility bill fell by a fifth. What it has not been sold as is a safety measure, and the silence is telling. The town has not published the accident figures for the dimmed hours.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Passage B.<\/strong> Critics of the dimming program want accident data before they will accept it, which is reasonable. What they overlook is that the town never claimed the program was about safety. It is a budget decision, and the budget numbers are public.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p><strong>The table<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><\/th>\n<th>Author A<\/th>\n<th>Author B<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Claim<\/td>\n<td>The town's silence on safety is suspicious<\/td>\n<td>The program is a budget decision and should be judged as one<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evidence<\/td>\n<td>Accident figures for the dimmed hours are unpublished<\/td>\n<td>The town never claimed safety; the budget numbers are public<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Concedes<\/td>\n<td>The savings are real (a fifth)<\/td>\n<td>Wanting data is reasonable<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reporting<\/td>\n<td>Describes how the program was \"sold\"<\/td>\n<td>Describes what critics \"want\" and \"overlook\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>Agree, disagree, talk past<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><em>Agree<\/em>: the program was not presented as a safety measure. A says it \"has not been sold as\" one; B says the town \"never claimed\" it was. Two authors who disagree about everything else state this fact in their own words. That is the overlap, and overlaps are almost always facts, not arguments.<\/p>\n<p><em>Disagree<\/em>: whether the silence matters. A thinks unpublished accident data is a warning sign. B thinks safety data is beside the point for a budget decision.<\/p>\n<p><em>Talk past<\/em>: A is asking what the program does; B is asking what the program is for. Neither answers the other's question. A test question that asks how B would respond to A's last sentence has an answer in that gap: B would say the figures are irrelevant to the decision as made, not that the figures are fine.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The questions a pair always asks<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>What would both authors agree on? (The fact in the overlap, never a judgment.)<\/li>\n<li>How would Author B respond to a specific sentence in A? (From B's claim, applied to that sentence, not from B's general mood.)<\/li>\n<li>What is the main difference? (The question each is answering, more often than the answer each gives.)<\/li>\n<li>Which author uses which kind of evidence? (A: an absence. B: a definition of the decision.)<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Using the Fenwick passage from chapter 5 as Passage A and the parking passage from chapter 8 as Passage B:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>On what single point would both authors agree about merchants' objections?<\/li>\n<li>How would the author of B respond to A's sentence that the merchants' estimates \"were not counts at all\"?<\/li>\n<li>Which author treats the status quo as a risk, and in which sentence?<\/li>\n<li>State the question each author is answering, in one sentence each.<\/li>\n<li>A student writes that \"both passages argue that merchants exaggerate.\" Say which passage supports that and which does not, with the sentence.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Watch for<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Paired-passage errors are <em>reported-view<\/em> errors in a new shape: the student assigns a claim to the wrong author, or to both. The table is the fix. Build it in the margin before the first question, every time, even when it feels slow. It is faster than rereading both passages for each of six questions.<\/p>"},
{"title": "11. The plausible wrong answer", "part": "Part One: The moves", "html": "<p>Test writers do not write wrong answers at random. Every distractor is built from something in the passage, which is why it feels right, and every distractor has a seam: the exact word or move that makes it wrong. Once you know the five ways a wrong answer is built, you can find the seam in a few seconds, and the question stops being a feeling and becomes a search.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The five builds<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Build<\/th>\n<th>How it is made<\/th>\n<th>The seam<\/th>\n<th>Chapter<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Too strong<\/td>\n<td>The right idea with the qualifier removed or the verb promoted: \"nearly\" becomes \"completely,\" \"suggests\" becomes \"proves\"<\/td>\n<td>The word that is one rung too high on the ladder<\/td>\n<td>2, 7<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wrong speaker<\/td>\n<td>A claim someone in the passage made, presented as the author's or the passage's<\/td>\n<td>The reporting verb back in the passage<\/td>\n<td>5<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>True but off<\/td>\n<td>A sentence from the passage that is accurate and does not answer the question asked<\/td>\n<td>The question's verb: it asked for the main point, and this is a detail<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Outside<\/td>\n<td>A reasonable belief about the world that the passage never states<\/td>\n<td>No sentence to point to<\/td>\n<td>4<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reversed<\/td>\n<td>The passage's claim with the cause and effect, or the before and after, swapped<\/td>\n<td>A sequence word: after, because, led to<\/td>\n<td>4<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>A passage<\/strong><\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Early accounts of the coastal fort described its walls as impregnable, but the engineer's 1790 survey, written after two seasons of storms, is more measured: it notes that the seaward wall had been rebuilt twice and would need rebuilding again. Later historians, relying on the early accounts, called the fort the strongest on the coast. The survey, which sat unread in a county archive until 1961, suggests they were describing the fort its builders had intended rather than the one that stood.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p><strong>The question<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The author's main point is that<\/p>\n<p>A. the fort was poorly designed. B. later historians were careless. C. the engineer's survey shows the fort was weaker than the early accounts claimed. D. the fort's reputation rested on accounts that the surviving evidence does not support.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Finding the seams<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>A is <em>too strong<\/em> and <em>outside<\/em>: the passage says the seaward wall needed rebuilding, not that the design was poor, and offers no sentence about design. B is <em>too strong<\/em> and <em>wrong speaker<\/em>: the author says the historians relied on early accounts, which is an explanation, not a charge of carelessness; \"careless\" is the reader's word. C is <em>true but off<\/em>: it is accurate about the survey and stops there, when the last sentence of the passage is about the reputation, not the wall. D has no seam. It matches the last sentence, which carries the author's own voice (\"suggests they were describing\"), and it is calibrated: \"does not support,\" not \"disproves.\"<\/p>\n<p><strong>The method, in order<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Read the question's verb and object. <em>Main point<\/em>, not <em>a detail<\/em>; <em>the author<\/em>, not <em>the historians<\/em>.<\/li>\n<li>Find the sentence the right answer depends on before looking at the choices. Here, the last sentence.<\/li>\n<li>Read each choice looking for its seam, not its appeal. Name the build.<\/li>\n<li>The choice with no seam is the answer. If two have no seam, the weaker one is.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Words in context: predict before you look<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>One question family is built almost entirely out of plausible wrong answers, and it deserves its own rule. <em>As used in line 12, \"sound\" most nearly means.<\/em> Every choice is a real meaning of the word. The distractors are the meanings you know best, which is why strong vocabularies miss these items: the reader recognizes a definition and stops.<\/p>\n<p>The rule: cover the choices. Read the sentence, and the one before it, and put a plain word of your own in the blank. Only then uncover the choices and pick the one closest to your word.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The committee's reasoning was sound, but its timing was not; the report arrived a week after the vote it was meant to inform.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>Before looking: the sentence contrasts the reasoning with the timing, and the timing was bad, so the reasoning was good. Your word: <em>solid<\/em>. Now the choices: A. audible. B. well founded. C. deep, as of sleep. D. healthy. B matches <em>solid<\/em>. A and C are the dictionary's most common meanings, and both are seams of the same kind: a real meaning that the sentence cannot hold. D is the near miss, a positive meaning that fits bodies, not arguments.<\/p>\n<p>Three things make the prediction reliable. First, the contrast or agreement word in the sentence (<em>but<\/em>, <em>and<\/em>, <em>although<\/em>) tells you the direction before you know the word. Second, the sentence after often restates the idea in plainer language; read one sentence past the line number. Third, if two choices both fit your word, substitute each into the sentence and read it aloud. One will sound like the passage. On the GRE and GMAT this is the whole of text completion, and chapter 21 builds on it.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>For each wrong answer in the question above, and then for the four below, name the build and the seam.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>(Fort passage) \"The survey proves the early accounts were fabricated.\"<\/li>\n<li>(Bridges passage, chapter 1) \"The engineer recommends closing the least-traveled bridges because small towns have less political power.\"<\/li>\n<li>(Library passage, chapter 2) \"Dropping late fees caused registrations across the town to rise by a third.\"<\/li>\n<li>(Fenwick passage, chapter 5) \"The author believes the engineer's data settled the question.\"<\/li>\n<li>(Streetlights, chapter 7) \"Residents of the six blocks slept better because warmer light is healthier.\"<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Watch for<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>This chapter does not fix one error; it is the test-craft layer over all eight. The signature of a student who has not learned it is <em>high confidence on missed items<\/em>: certain, and wrong, because the distractor felt like the passage. The signature of a student who has learned it is a slower first read of the choices and a faster, calmer decision.<\/p>"},
{"title": "12. The unfamiliar text", "part": "Part One: The moves", "html": "<p>Everything in this book has been practiced on passages you have now read twice. The test will hand you one you have never seen, with a clock running, and nobody beside you. This chapter is about doing it alone.<\/p>\n<p><strong>What transfers and what does not<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>What transfers is the habit: the finger on the sentence, the circle around the small word, the arrow from the reference word, the speaker above the verb, the seam in the distractor. What does not transfer is familiarity with any particular passage, which is why the program measures you only on texts you have never seen and why nothing in the lesson library is ever reused as a checkpoint. If your checkpoint scores are lower than your lesson scores, that gap is the most useful number you have. It says how much of your performance was the method and how much was recognition.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The week before<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><em>Seven days out<\/em>: one full timed section on unfamiliar passages. Score it. For every miss, name the build of the distractor you chose and the chapter it belongs to. That list is your week.<\/p>\n<p><em>Six to three days out<\/em>: twenty minutes a day. Reread the two chapters your list points to. Do their drills with the chapter closed. One oral reading checkpoint.<\/p>\n<p><em>Two days out<\/em>: one more timed section. Compare it with the first. You are looking for the same builds to have stopped working on you, not for a score.<\/p>\n<p><em>The day before<\/em>: vocabulary due, flip cards, the watch-again moments from your recordings. Nothing new. Pack. Sleep is a study method.<\/p>\n<p><em>The morning of<\/em>: the first two questions slowly and correctly. A clean start sets the pace; a rushed miss sets the mood.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Under time<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>You will not have time to do everything in this book on every question. You will have time to do the one thing each question needs, and the question's verb tells you which. <em>Main point<\/em>: find the author's own sentence (chapter 5). <em>Most directly supports<\/em>: find the warrant (chapter 7). <em>The word most nearly means<\/em>: predict before you look (chapter 11). <em>Both authors would agree<\/em>: the overlap, a fact (chapter 10). <em>The author's attitude<\/em>: the line where the temperature changes (chapters 6 and 9). When you are between two answers, find the seam in each; if one has none, it is right; if both do, the weaker one is, and move on.<\/p>\n<p><strong>What deserves full attention<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Skimming is sold as reading faster. It is not; eyes have a ceiling, and chapter 16 says where it is. What a skilled reader does under time is decide, sentence by sentence, how much attention each one needs, and the decision follows the moves of Part One. Every sentence is read once. These get read twice:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>The sentence with the small word in it: the <em>nearly<\/em>, the <em>only<\/em>, the <em>unless<\/em>. That is where the question will be.<\/li>\n<li>The sentence where the voice changes: a reporting verb, a <em>but<\/em>, a quotation the author steps away from.<\/li>\n<li>The first and last sentences of each paragraph, because the job of the paragraph is usually in one of them.<\/li>\n<li>Any sentence that states a cause, a number, or a comparison.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>These get read once and not again unless a question sends you back: the second and third examples in a list of examples, the details of a method you have already labeled, the description that sets a scene. The difference between a reader who finishes the section and one who does not is rarely eye speed. It is that the first reader spends the second read where the questions are and the second reader spends it everywhere.<\/p>\n<p>Bank the last two questions. A question skipped at full speed is worth more than one ground out at half.<\/p>\n<p><strong>A final passage<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Read it once, as you would on the test, and answer the question before you read the walkthrough.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>When the regional airline dropped its morning flight, the town's newspaper predicted the end of the business park on the airport road. Three years later the park is fuller than it was, though not with the companies the newspaper had in mind. The engineering firms that needed the flight left. The warehouses and the call center that replaced them needed the road, not the runway. Whether the town is better off depends on which jobs one counts, and the newspaper has not yet said which it prefers.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>The author's main point is that<\/p>\n<p>A. the newspaper's prediction was wrong. B. the business park recovered because of the road. C. the prediction failed on its own terms while the question it raised remains open. D. the town is better off without the morning flight.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The walkthrough<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The author's own sentence is the last one: the question of whether the town is better off is unanswered, and the newspaper has not said what it would count. A is <em>too strong<\/em> and <em>true but off<\/em>: the park did not end, so the prediction missed, but the passage's point is what the miss leaves unresolved, and <em>though not with the companies the newspaper had in mind<\/em> is the author declining to call the newspaper simply wrong. B is <em>true but off<\/em>, and slightly <em>reversed<\/em>: the road explains who came, not the author's point. D is <em>outside<\/em>: no sentence says the town is better off; the last sentence says that depends. C has no seam. It is calibrated to the last sentence, and it accounts for the concession in sentence 2.<\/p>\n<p>If you got C, and you can say why in those terms, you have the method. If you got A, reread chapter 2 and chapter 8 this week; the seam you missed was the concession. Either way, you did it on a text you had never seen, which is the only kind that counts.<\/p>"},
{"title": "13. What actually moves a reading score", "part": "Part Two: What moves the needle", "html": "<p>Part One taught the moves. This part is about training, and it begins with an honest ranking, because most of what students and parents spend time and money on does not move a reading score, and a few things move it a great deal.<\/p>\n<p>The ranking below is for one kind of reader: fluent, decoding automatic, a good vocabulary for their age, stuck. It is not for a beginning reader, where decoding dominates everything. The order comes from decades of reading research as it applies to that reader, from the diagnostic data we collect, and from what I watched work on drill sergeants and cadets. Where the research and my experience disagree, I say so.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The levers, ranked<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Rank<\/th>\n<th>Lever<\/th>\n<th>What it is<\/th>\n<th>Effect for the stuck fluent reader<\/th>\n<th>How fast<\/th>\n<th>Chapter<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>Sentence-level reasoning<\/td>\n<td>The three moves: the sentence that matters, how far it goes, whose it is, written down<\/td>\n<td>Large, and the reason scores move in weeks rather than years. The reader already has the words; this is what to do with them<\/td>\n<td>Weeks<\/td>\n<td>1 to 12<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2<\/td>\n<td>Knowledge in coherent domains<\/td>\n<td>Reading several texts on one subject so the schema is loaded and vocabulary recurs<\/td>\n<td>Large, and it never saturates. The single most under-used lever in school and in test prep<\/td>\n<td>Months<\/td>\n<td>15<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3<\/td>\n<td>Vocabulary by morphology and context<\/td>\n<td>Roots and affixes, words met in context, retrieved on a schedule<\/td>\n<td>Large, slow, compounding. About six in ten academic words are built from parts you can learn<\/td>\n<td>Months<\/td>\n<td>14<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4<\/td>\n<td>Writing about reading<\/td>\n<td>Closed-book summaries, claims with evidence, explaining aloud<\/td>\n<td>Large. One of the strongest effects in the literature, because it forces you to resolve what you only half understood<\/td>\n<td>Weeks<\/td>\n<td>17<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5<\/td>\n<td>Volume<\/td>\n<td>Whole books and long articles, at the edge of comfort, 150 minutes a week<\/td>\n<td>Large and slow; the lever that separates the top readers from the rest by millions of words a year<\/td>\n<td>Months to years<\/td>\n<td>16<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6<\/td>\n<td>Automaticity<\/td>\n<td>Oral reading speed and accuracy on grade-level text<\/td>\n<td>Foundational: large if it is missing, zero if it is present. The diagnostic tells you which<\/td>\n<td>Weeks<\/td>\n<td>16<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7<\/td>\n<td>Spacing and interleaving<\/td>\n<td>Practice spread over days and mixed across skills instead of blocked<\/td>\n<td>Medium, and it multiplies everything above it<\/td>\n<td>Immediate<\/td>\n<td>17<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8<\/td>\n<td>Calibration<\/td>\n<td>Knowing how sure you are, and matching the strength of your answer to the strength of the text<\/td>\n<td>Medium; converts knowledge you already have into points you are currently losing<\/td>\n<td>Weeks<\/td>\n<td>18<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9<\/td>\n<td>Test craft<\/td>\n<td>The seams in wrong answers, the question's verb, pacing<\/td>\n<td>Medium, fast, and it caps out. Worth two sessions, not twenty<\/td>\n<td>Days<\/td>\n<td>11, 12<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10<\/td>\n<td>Sleep<\/td>\n<td>Memory consolidates during it<\/td>\n<td>Medium and nearly free; the cheapest lever on the list<\/td>\n<td>Nights<\/td>\n<td>17<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>What does not move it<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>These are the things that feel like work and produce nothing, listed because every family has already spent money on at least two of them.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Speed reading.<\/em> The eye takes in a handful of letters per fixation and moves four or five times a second. That caps real reading near 300 to 400 words a minute. Everything faster is skimming, which is a different skill and is taught in chapter 12 as deciding what deserves full attention, never as reading.<\/li>\n<li><em>Rereading and highlighting.<\/em> The weakest study methods with the strongest feeling of competence. The second pass through a passage feels like learning because the words are familiar; familiarity is not understanding, and the test will not ask you to recognize the passage.<\/li>\n<li><em>Comprehension strategy worksheets.<\/em> Summarizing, predicting, questioning. They help, and their effect saturates in a few weeks. A program that is still teaching strategies in month three is charging for something the student already has.<\/li>\n<li><em>Practice tests without error analysis.<\/em> A score with no list of which builds the distractors used and which chapter each belongs to is a number, not practice. One analyzed section is worth five taken.<\/li>\n<li><em>Vocabulary lists of unrelated words.<\/em> Twenty words with no shared root, no context, no neighbor to distinguish them from, and no retrieval schedule. They are gone in a week. Chapter 14 is the alternative.<\/li>\n<li><em>Reading more at comfort level.<\/em> Volume matters, and volume below the edge maintains rather than builds. The book has to be slightly too hard.<\/li>\n<li><em>Apps that count streaks and minutes.<\/em> Time on task is not learning. A student who finishes the routine in fourteen minutes with no hints did more than one who spent forty.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>The sequence<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The levers are not done all at once. The program orders them because the order matters.<\/p>\n<p><em>Base<\/em> (the first third of the plan): automaticity checked and, if needed, drilled; the first unit of moves; the word bank started; the first long read begun. Nothing timed.<\/p>\n<p><em>Build<\/em> (the middle third): the harder moves (cause, voice, argument); knowledge units in sequence; writing about reading on every passage; the first practice section at the midpoint.<\/p>\n<p><em>Peak<\/em> (the last quarter): unfamiliar texts under time; the seams; calibration; two timed sections a week.<\/p>\n<p><em>Taper<\/em> (the last week): retrieval only. Nothing new. Sleep.<\/p>\n<p>The reason for the order is simple. The moves produce the fastest gain, so they go first and keep the student in the program. Knowledge and vocabulary are slow, so they start early and run underneath everything. Test craft caps out, so it comes last and takes two sessions. Sleep is in every phase.<\/p>\n<p><strong>One more thing that moves it<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>A student who can name their own error. By week three, every student in the program should be able to say \"I overlooked a qualifier\" or \"I gave the author a view the merchants had\" without a tutor prompting it. The research on this is less tidy than the research on vocabulary, and I believe it anyway, because I watched it: a reader who can name the mistake stops making it far faster than one who is told the right answer. The eight errors in the appendix exist for that reason.<\/p>"},
{"title": "14. The word bank", "part": "Part Two: What moves the needle", "html": "<p>A skilled adult reader knows tens of thousands of words, and almost none of them came from a list. They came from reading, from meeting a word in a sentence that made its meaning guessable, and meeting it again a week later in a different sentence. The job of vocabulary work is to make that happen on purpose and faster. Two things do it: morphology, so that an unfamiliar word half-explains itself, and a schedule, so that a word met once is met again before it is gone.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Why lists fail<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>A list of twenty unrelated words gives you twenty things to remember with nothing connecting them. The brain stores words as a network: this word is built from that root, sits near that neighbor, appeared in that sentence. A word with no connections has no address, and it is gone in a week. A word met in a passage about a library, built from a root you know, distinguished from a neighbor you already have, and retrieved on day one, three, seven, and fourteen has four addresses, and it stays.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The five-step encounter<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Every word in the program's banks is learned the same way, and the order is the point.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Meet it in context.<\/em> A sentence from a passage you read, not a definition. \"Families who had let their cards lapse.\" You can already half-guess it.<\/li>\n<li><em>Learn its parts.<\/em> Lapse: Latin <em>lapsus<\/em>, a slip. Collapse, elapse, relapse share it. Now the word has relatives.<\/li>\n<li><em>Set it against its neighbor.<\/em> Lapse is not cancel. A card lapses by itself; a person cancels it. The neighbor is where test questions live, because the wrong answer is usually the near word.<\/li>\n<li><em>Use it.<\/em> One sentence of your own, about something that is not the passage.<\/li>\n<li><em>Retrieve it later.<\/em> Day one, three, seven, fourteen, thirty. Before you look, say the meaning. If you cannot, back to day one.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>A word is learned when step five works on day fourteen, not when step two is read.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Words in context, on the test<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The test rarely asks what a word means. It asks what a word means <em>here<\/em>, and the four options are all real meanings of real words. The move is from chapter 11: predict before you look. Cover the choices, read the sentence, write your own word for the blank from the sentence's logic, then find the option closest to it. A student who reads the choices first will find one that is a real meaning of the word and wrong in the sentence, because that is how the distractor was built.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The parts that matter most<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Roughly six in ten academic words in English are built from Latin and Greek parts. Learn the most productive twenty prefixes and suffixes and the most productive fifty roots and you have a key to thousands of words you have never seen. The tables below are the program's spine. Learn them a row at a time, and for each, say three words that use it before you read the examples.<\/p>\n<p><em>Twenty affixes<\/em><\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Affix<\/th>\n<th>Means<\/th>\n<th>Examples<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>un-, in-, im-, il-, ir-<\/td>\n<td>not<\/td>\n<td>unequal, inevitable, impartial, illegible, irrelevant<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>re-<\/td>\n<td>again, back<\/td>\n<td>revise, recede, retract<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>dis-<\/td>\n<td>apart, not<\/td>\n<td>discrepancy, dismiss, disparity<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>pre-<\/td>\n<td>before<\/td>\n<td>precedent, predict, preclude<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sub-<\/td>\n<td>under<\/td>\n<td>substantiate, subtle, subvert<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>inter-<\/td>\n<td>between<\/td>\n<td>intermittent, interpret, intervene<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>trans-<\/td>\n<td>across<\/td>\n<td>transfer, transient, transcribe<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>con-, com-, col-<\/td>\n<td>with, together<\/td>\n<td>concede, compel, collaborate<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ex-, e-<\/td>\n<td>out<\/td>\n<td>exacerbate, elicit, evoke<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>de-<\/td>\n<td>down, away<\/td>\n<td>decline, deride, deduce<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>pro-<\/td>\n<td>forward, for<\/td>\n<td>provisional, proponent, prosaic<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ambi-, amphi-<\/td>\n<td>both<\/td>\n<td>ambivalent, ambiguous<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-tion, -sion<\/td>\n<td>the act or state of<\/td>\n<td>attribution, concession, allusion<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-ity<\/td>\n<td>quality of<\/td>\n<td>ambiguity, equanimity, uniformity<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-ous, -ious<\/td>\n<td>full of<\/td>\n<td>meticulous, garrulous, conspicuous<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-ate<\/td>\n<td>to make, to do<\/td>\n<td>mitigate, corroborate, vacillate<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-ive<\/td>\n<td>tending to<\/td>\n<td>persuasive, decisive, tentative<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-able, -ible<\/td>\n<td>can be<\/td>\n<td>feasible, viable, impregnable<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-ize<\/td>\n<td>to make<\/td>\n<td>scrutinize, emphasize, synthesize<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-ment, -ance, -ence<\/td>\n<td>result or state of<\/td>\n<td>argument, precedence, prevalence<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><em>Fifty roots<\/em><\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Root<\/th>\n<th>Means<\/th>\n<th>Words<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>ced, cess<\/td>\n<td>go, yield<\/td>\n<td>concede, precedent, recede, accessible<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>dict<\/td>\n<td>say<\/td>\n<td>diction, contradict, verdict, predict<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>duc, duct<\/td>\n<td>lead<\/td>\n<td>deduce, conduct, induce, reduce<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>fid<\/td>\n<td>trust<\/td>\n<td>confide, fidelity, diffident<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ject<\/td>\n<td>throw<\/td>\n<td>reject, conjecture, objective<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>loqu, locu<\/td>\n<td>speak<\/td>\n<td>eloquent, colloquial, loquacious<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>mit, miss<\/td>\n<td>send<\/td>\n<td>submit, omission, intermittent<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>pel, puls<\/td>\n<td>push<\/td>\n<td>compel, repel, impulse<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>plic<\/td>\n<td>fold<\/td>\n<td>implicit, explicit, replicate<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>spec, spic<\/td>\n<td>look<\/td>\n<td>speculate, conspicuous, perspective<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>tract<\/td>\n<td>pull<\/td>\n<td>abstract, detract, protracted<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>vert, vers<\/td>\n<td>turn<\/td>\n<td>subvert, averse, versatile<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>voc, vok<\/td>\n<td>call<\/td>\n<td>equivocal, advocate, evoke<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>val<\/td>\n<td>strength, worth<\/td>\n<td>prevalent, ambivalent, valid<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>equ<\/td>\n<td>equal<\/td>\n<td>equivocal, equanimity, equitable<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>cred<\/td>\n<td>believe<\/td>\n<td>credit, credible, incredulous<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>scrib, script<\/td>\n<td>write<\/td>\n<td>describe, transcribe, inscription<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ten, tin, tain<\/td>\n<td>hold<\/td>\n<td>tenuous, pertinent, sustain<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>tend, tens<\/td>\n<td>stretch<\/td>\n<td>contend, tentative, extend<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>pos, pon<\/td>\n<td>place<\/td>\n<td>juxtaposition, proponent, impose<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>fer<\/td>\n<td>carry<\/td>\n<td>infer, transfer, defer<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>cap, cept<\/td>\n<td>take<\/td>\n<td>anticipate, perception, capable<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>grad, gress<\/td>\n<td>step<\/td>\n<td>gradual, regress, digress<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>clin<\/td>\n<td>lean<\/td>\n<td>decline, incline, recline<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>curr, curs<\/td>\n<td>run<\/td>\n<td>current, recur, cursory<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>fac, fect<\/td>\n<td>make<\/td>\n<td>facilitate, effect, superficial<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>gen<\/td>\n<td>birth, kind<\/td>\n<td>generate, genre, engender<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>log<\/td>\n<td>word, reason<\/td>\n<td>logos, analogy, eulogy<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>mod<\/td>\n<td>measure<\/td>\n<td>modest, moderate, mode<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>nov<\/td>\n<td>new<\/td>\n<td>innovative, novel, renovate<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>path<\/td>\n<td>feeling<\/td>\n<td>pathos, apathy, empathy<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>port<\/td>\n<td>carry<\/td>\n<td>report, purport, import<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>rupt<\/td>\n<td>break<\/td>\n<td>disrupt, abrupt, rupture<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sent, sens<\/td>\n<td>feel<\/td>\n<td>consensus, sentiment, dissent<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sequ, secu<\/td>\n<td>follow<\/td>\n<td>sequence, consequence, obsequious<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sol<\/td>\n<td>alone<\/td>\n<td>solitary, desolate, soliloquy<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sta, stat<\/td>\n<td>stand<\/td>\n<td>substantiate, static, status<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>struct<\/td>\n<td>build<\/td>\n<td>construe, obstruct, structure<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>tempor<\/td>\n<td>time<\/td>\n<td>temporary, contemporary, extemporaneous<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ver<\/td>\n<td>true<\/td>\n<td>verify, verdict, veracity<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>vid, vis<\/td>\n<td>see<\/td>\n<td>evident, provisional, revise<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>viv, vit<\/td>\n<td>live<\/td>\n<td>vivid, viable, revive<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>chron<\/td>\n<td>time<\/td>\n<td>chronic, chronology, synchronize<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>graph, gram<\/td>\n<td>write<\/td>\n<td>paragraph, epigram, demographic<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>morph<\/td>\n<td>form<\/td>\n<td>morphology, amorphous, metamorphosis<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>phil<\/td>\n<td>love<\/td>\n<td>philosophy, bibliophile<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>phon<\/td>\n<td>sound<\/td>\n<td>euphony, cacophony, phonics<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>scop<\/td>\n<td>see<\/td>\n<td>scope, microscope, periscope<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>anthrop<\/td>\n<td>human<\/td>\n<td>anthropology, misanthrope, philanthropy<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>crat, cracy<\/td>\n<td>rule<\/td>\n<td>aristocrat, bureaucracy, autocratic<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Take any five words from a passage you read this week. For each, write the five steps: the sentence it came from, its parts, its neighbor and how it differs, a sentence of your own, and the date you will retrieve it. Then, for one root from the table, write three words that use it which are not in the examples. If you can, the root is yours.<\/p>"},
{"title": "15. Knowledge is the skill", "part": "Part Two: What moves the needle", "html": "<p>Here is the finding that should change how every reading program is built, and mostly has not. In a well-known study from the 1980s, researchers gave students a passage describing half an inning of a baseball game and asked them to show what happened. The students who knew baseball understood the passage better than the students who did not, and it did not matter much who the better readers were. Weak readers who knew the game out-comprehended strong readers who did not.<\/p>\n<p>The lesson is not about baseball. It is that past the point where decoding is automatic, reading comprehension is mostly a function of what you already know about the subject. The strong reader without the knowledge has no schema to hang the sentences on, no way to infer what the passage leaves unsaid, and no familiarity with the vocabulary the passage assumes. The weak reader with the knowledge has all three.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Why this is good news<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Strategies saturate. Teach summarizing and predicting for three weeks and the student has them; teach them for three months and nothing more happens. Knowledge does not saturate. Every passage you read about a subject makes the next passage about that subject easier, and the effect compounds for the rest of your life. A reader who has read five texts about how cities manage heat will read a sixth at a speed and depth that a stranger to the subject cannot reach by any strategy.<\/p>\n<p>This is why the program's lessons are written in units, several passages on one subject, instead of a new topic every day. The standard curriculum rotates topics weekly to practice skills, and in doing so starves the knowledge term. We do the opposite on purpose.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The four domains tests draw from<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Reading tests are not random. Their passages come from a small number of domains, and a student who has built knowledge in those domains is reading on home ground.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Domain<\/th>\n<th>What the passages look like<\/th>\n<th>What to read to build it<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Natural science<\/td>\n<td>A study, an experiment, a finding and its limits; the vocabulary of method (sample, control, correlation, mechanism)<\/td>\n<td>Science journalism about a single question, several pieces in a row; one popular science book per year<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Social science<\/td>\n<td>A survey, a policy, a town or a school that changed something; the vocabulary of cause and comparison<\/td>\n<td>Local reporting on a civic decision followed over months; a book about one institution<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>History and civics<\/td>\n<td>Founding documents, speeches, letters, a historian weighing evidence; 18th- and 19th-century prose rhythm<\/td>\n<td>Public-domain primary documents (the program's long reads), read whole<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Literature<\/td>\n<td>19th-century fiction, modern short fiction, poems with a turn; tone, motive, image<\/td>\n<td>One novel from before 1929 and one from after, per year, read for what each argues<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>A student who has read two public-domain speeches will not be slowed by the syntax of a third. A student who has followed one school-board decision in the news will recognize the shape of the later-bell passage before the second paragraph. That is knowledge doing the work the moves cannot do alone.<\/p>\n<p><strong>How to build it on purpose<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Cluster.<\/em> Three to five texts on one subject in a row, never one. The second is easier than the first; the third is easier still.<\/li>\n<li><em>Keep a knowledge ledger.<\/em> One page per subject: what you now know, in your own words, and which words recurred. Add to it after each text. It is the closed-book summary at the scale of a subject.<\/li>\n<li><em>Read the hard primary text, not the summary.<\/em> The textbook paragraph about a speech is comprehension; the speech is knowledge. The program's long reads are primary texts for this reason.<\/li>\n<li><em>Ask the subject's questions.<\/em> Science: what was the comparison? Social science: what else changed? History: who is speaking and to whom? Literature: where does the tone turn? These are the three moves, specialized by domain.<\/li>\n<li><em>Let vocabulary come from the cluster.<\/em> The words that recur across three texts on one subject are the words worth the five-step encounter. The ones that appear once are not.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Pick one subject you know nothing about and one you know well. Find a passage on each of about the same difficulty, and time yourself reading both, then write a two-sentence summary of each with the text closed. The gap between the two summaries is the knowledge effect, felt from the inside. Then spend a week reading three more texts on the subject you did not know, and do it again.<\/p>"},
{"title": "16. Volume and automaticity", "part": "Part Two: What moves the needle", "html": "<p>Two levers that look old-fashioned and are not: read a great deal, and read aloud. The first builds the word bank that every other chapter draws on. The second keeps the foundation under it from quietly giving way.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Volume: the gap that compounds<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>A word becomes yours after you have met it successfully a handful of times. That means the size of your word bank is set, more than by anything else, by how many words pass in front of you. The spread is enormous. A student in the top tenth of readers in middle school meets well over a million more words a year outside school than a student in the bottom tenth, and the gap compounds: more words, more knowledge, easier reading, more reading. By 10th grade the two students are not reading the same language.<\/p>\n<p>The program's number is 150 minutes a week of reading outside school, of whole things: a book, a long essay, a primary document. Not passages. Passages train the moves; whole things build the bank and the stamina, and the stamina is what the 45th minute of a test is measured on.<\/p>\n<p>Three rules for the 150 minutes.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>At the edge, not in comfort.<\/em> The right book has a few words per page you do not know and sentences you have to read twice. Comfortable reading maintains; edge reading builds. If there is nothing to look up, the book is too easy.<\/li>\n<li><em>Whole, and without a question set.<\/em> Read it the way a reader does. The knowledge ledger from chapter 15 is the only output.<\/li>\n<li><em>Logged.<\/em> Minutes and pages, weekly. Not because the number is the point, but because the number is honest and memory is not.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Automaticity: the foundation you cannot see<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Decoding, for a fluent reader, is invisible, which is why it can fail without anyone noticing. Working memory is small. If any of it is going to the words, less is left for the meaning, and the reader experiences this not as a decoding problem but as a comprehension problem: \"I read it and I don't know what it said.\" The diagnosis is a one-minute read-aloud, and the program does one every week.<\/p>\n<p>The bars for the readers this book is for, on grade-level nonfiction: about 150 words per minute, 97 percent accuracy, and phrasing in meaningful chunks. A reader who clears them spends the whole session on meaning. A reader who is accurate but slow spends ten minutes a session on the drills below until the bar is met. A reader below 90 percent accuracy is practicing errors, and the honest answer is a structured-literacy specialist, not this program; a decoding problem is real and fixable and it is not what we teach.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The three drills<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><em>Repeated reading.<\/em> The same paragraph aloud, timed, three times in a row. The third read is usually 20 to 30 percent faster than the first, and most of that speed transfers to new text. It is the highest-yield fluency drill there is, and it costs four minutes.<\/p>\n<p><em>Phrase-cued reading.<\/em> A passage marked where a fluent reader pauses: at phrase and clause boundaries, not at random. Read it aloud with the marks, then without. This builds prosody, which is comprehension made audible; a reader who pauses in the right places is parsing the sentence correctly, and one who does not is usually not.<\/p>\n<p><em>Multisyllabic word attack.<\/em> Older readers do not stall on short words. They stall on <em>corroborate<\/em>, <em>equanimity<\/em>, <em>juxtaposition<\/em>, long academic words that arrive at test density. The drill: say the word by its chunks, stress the marked syllable, name the parts you already know from chapter 14, then say it whole three times. Thirty words, a few a week, until each is automatic.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The speed ceiling, once more<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Real reading caps near 300 to 400 words a minute, because that is as fast as the eye can move and still take in the words. Nobody who comprehends is reading faster than that. If a program promises more, it is teaching skimming, which chapter 12 covers as a decision about what deserves full attention. The goal of the drills above is not speed past the ceiling; it is reaching the ceiling without effort, so that all the effort can go to meaning.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>This week: one read-aloud checkpoint, timed, with errors counted and phrasing rated. Three repeated readings of one paragraph from a lesson. One hundred fifty minutes of a whole book at the edge, logged. Next week, the same. The checkpoint number is the one to watch; when it crosses the bar, the drills stop and the time goes to reading.<\/p>"},
{"title": "17. Retrieve, do not reread", "part": "Part Two: What moves the needle", "html": "<p>The most reliable finding in the science of learning is also the one students resist most: pulling something out of memory strengthens it far more than putting it in again. Rereading feels productive because the second pass is smooth. The smoothness is recognition, and recognition is not what the test measures. The test measures whether you can produce the idea with the page closed. So that is what practice has to be.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Writing about reading<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Of everything a reader can do after a passage, writing about it has one of the largest effects in the research, and it is easy to see why. Writing forces you to resolve what you only half understood. You cannot write \"the passage says attendance improved because of the bell\" without noticing, as the sentence forms, that the passage did not say that. The program has three forms of it, and every session uses at least two.<\/p>\n<p><em>The closed-book summary.<\/em> Two sentences, passage hidden: what it showed, what it could not show. Fifteen to sixty words. The second sentence is the one students skip and the one that matters, because every passage has a limit and naming it is the whole method in miniature.<\/p>\n<p><em>The claim with evidence.<\/em> One sentence that takes a position, one sentence that points to the text, and the warrant between them, from chapter 7. The coach names one error; you revise once. Revision, not production, is where the learning is.<\/p>\n<p><em>Explaining it aloud.<\/em> Forty-five seconds, no notes: the argument, the strongest evidence, what a critic would say, what is uncertain. Speech is faster than writing and less forgiving; if you cannot say the argument, you do not have it yet. In the program this is done to a tutor; alone, to a timer, and then rated against four questions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Spacing<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>A word or an idea reviewed once, the day you learned it, is mostly gone in a week. The same word reviewed on day one, three, seven, fourteen, and thirty is yours for years, for about the same total minutes. The schedule works because each review arrives just as the memory is fading, and the effort of pulling it back is what strengthens it. The program's review notebook runs the schedule automatically; the discipline is to retrieve before you look. A review in which you read the definition first is rereading with extra steps.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Interleaving<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Ten qualifier tasks in a row feel efficient and teach less than three qualifier tasks mixed with three whose-view tasks and three claim tasks. Blocked practice lets you stop thinking about which move the item needs, and on the test, deciding which move the item needs is most of the work. Every lesson in the program has four different task types for this reason, and every practice section is mixed. It feels harder. It is supposed to.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The illusion of fluency, and how to beat it<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Students judge how well they know something by how easy it feels, and easy is the wrong signal. Rereading, highlighting, and watching an explanation all feel easy and produce little; retrieval feels hard and produces a lot. The fix is to stop trusting the feeling and trust the test: close the book, say it, write it, and let what comes out be the measure. The confidence rating in chapter 18 is the formal version of this.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Sleep<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Memories from the day are consolidated during sleep, which means the hours after a session are when the session becomes permanent, and they only count if you are asleep. A student who trades an hour of sleep for an hour of late review the week of a test is almost always making a bad trade. The program's taper week has no new material for this reason, and the parent guide says so in plain words.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The 20-minute routine is a retrieval engine<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Look at the routine in the appendix again with this chapter in mind. Read once; answer with the sentence; write why; claim and revise; close the book and summarize; retrieve vocabulary. Every step after the first is production, and none is rereading. That is not an accident, and it is the reason the routine is twenty minutes: retrieval is tiring, and twenty minutes of it is worth an hour of the other thing.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Take the last passage you read in Part One. Without looking, write the two-sentence summary. Then open the chapter and check it against the passage. Mark every place your summary said more than the passage did. That gap, measured honestly, is the only study method in this book that cannot be faked.<\/p>"},
{"title": "18. Calibration", "part": "Part Two: What moves the needle", "html": "<p>There are two ways to lose a point you could have had. One is not knowing. The other is knowing and not trusting it, or not knowing and being sure. Calibration is the match between how confident you are and how often you are right, and for a fluent reader stuck at a 640, fixing it is worth more points than any single chapter in Part One.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Why the diagnostic asks how sure you are<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Every item in the program's diagnostic asks for a confidence rating after the answer: guess, unsure, fairly sure, certain. The ratings are not for the student's feelings. They are the fastest way to find two patterns the score alone hides.<\/p>\n<p><em>Certain and wrong.<\/em> The student chose a distractor and was sure of it, which means the distractor felt like the passage. This is the reader who needs chapter 11 more than anything: the seams are invisible to them, and the fix is to make the seams visible before working on anything else.<\/p>\n<p><em>Guess and right, with the right sentence.<\/em> The student found the evidence, chose correctly, and did not believe it. The skill is there and the trust is not. This reader second-guesses right answers into wrong ones under time, and the fix is not more content; it is a record, kept for two weeks, of how often the guesses were right.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The ladders<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Calibration applies to the text as well as to yourself. A writer who says <em>suggests<\/em> has not said <em>proves<\/em>, and an answer that says <em>proves<\/em> is wrong even if the idea is right. The three ladders, weakest to strongest:<\/p>\n<p><em>How sure is the writer?<\/em> suggests &lt; indicates &lt; shows &lt; demonstrates &lt; proves<\/p>\n<p><em>How many?<\/em> some &lt; many &lt; most &lt; nearly all &lt; all<\/p>\n<p><em>How likely?<\/em> possible &lt; plausible &lt; probable &lt; likely &lt; certain<\/p>\n<p>The rule that follows from them is the single most useful rule for choosing between two answers: when both seem right, the one that claims less usually is. Tests punish overclaiming far more often than underclaiming, because a passage that earns <em>proves<\/em> is rare and a passage that earns <em>suggests<\/em> is every passage.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Matching your claim to the text<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>When you write, put your claim on the ladder and put the passage's evidence on the ladder, and make them match. The later-bell passage earns <em>absences fell; the cause cannot be separated<\/em>. A claim at <em>the bell probably helped<\/em> is one rung too high. A claim at <em>the bell proved effective<\/em> is three rungs too high and will be marked by the coach as an overclaim. A claim at <em>nothing can be known<\/em> is a rung too low; the passage knows that absences fell.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The certain-and-wrong audit<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Once a week, from the log: list every item marked certain that was wrong. For each, name the build of the distractor (too strong, wrong speaker, true but off, outside, reversed) and the chapter. Two or more of the same build in a week is the week's lesson. This audit is the most efficient use of twenty minutes in the program, because it targets the exact places where confidence is covering for a gap.<\/p>\n<p>Then, once a week, the other list: every item marked guess or unsure that was right with the right sentence. If it is long, the problem is trust, and the cure is to look at the list.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The four confidence levels, and what to do with each on the test<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>You feel<\/th>\n<th>Do<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Certain<\/td>\n<td>Find the sentence anyway. Certainty is where the distractors live. Four seconds.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fairly sure<\/td>\n<td>Check the seam in the answer you did not pick. If it has one, move on.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unsure<\/td>\n<td>Find the sentence that would have to be true for each of the two remaining answers. One will not have it.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Guess<\/td>\n<td>Pick the weaker claim, mark the item, bank the time. Return only if the bank allows.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Take any ten items you have done in the program, with their confidence ratings and results. Sort them into four boxes: certain-right, certain-wrong, unsure-right, unsure-wrong. A calibrated reader has almost everything in the first and last boxes. Count what is in the middle two. That number, more than the score, is what to work on this month, and the two boxes tell you which chapter.<\/p>"},
{"title": "19. Train like a team", "part": "Part Two: What moves the needle", "html": "<p>Everything in Part Two is a lever. This chapter is about the machine that pulls them, because a lever nobody pulls on Tuesday moves nothing. I learned the machine in three places that do not look alike and run the same way: a track team, a plebe year, and an artillery battery. Elite teams do not perform well because their people are talented. They perform well because the intent is clear, the checklist is written, the drills are counted, the scrimmage is at game speed, the season is planned, the tape is watched, and the review is honest. Reading can be trained the same way, and when it is, the score stops being a mystery.<\/p>\n<p><strong>1. Intent<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Soldiers call it the commander's intent: the one sentence that says what success looks like, so that every decision underneath it can be made without asking. A season has a date and a time to beat. In reading, the target is the stated band on the stated date, and under it, this week's pattern. Every session opens by saying both aloud. A session that begins with \"let's do some reading\" has no intent and produces no training effect.<\/p>\n<p><strong>2. Checklist<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>A team has a standard for everything that recurs, and the standard is on paper, not in someone's head. A battery has a checklist for emplacing a gun; a sprinter has a warm-up that does not change on meet day. In this program the standards are the 20-minute routine, the three bars for oral reading, the eight errors, and the rule that evidence comes before the answer. A student who knows the standard can hold themselves to it on a Tuesday night with no tutor in the room, which is most nights.<\/p>\n<p><strong>3. Drills<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>A battle drill is an action rehearsed so many times that it happens under stress without a decision: contact left, everyone knows where to go. A hurdler does not think about the lead leg. The three moves are battle drills. Finger on the sentence; circle the small word; speaker above the verb. They are drilled in reps, hundreds of them across a season, on easy passages first, because a reflex is built on easy material and spent on hard material. By the peak phase the moves should take four seconds a question and no thought, which is the whole point: thought is reserved for the question.<\/p>\n<p><strong>4. Scrimmage at game speed<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>No team scrimmages under conditions easier than the game. The program's checkpoints are unfamiliar texts, timed, with hints disabled, because that is the test. Lessons can be easier; checkpoints cannot. The gap between lesson performance and checkpoint performance is reported every week, and it is the number that tells you whether the training is transferring or merely familiar.<\/p>\n<p><strong>5. Season<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>No athlete trains at race intensity for twelve weeks; they build a base, add load, sharpen, and taper. The program's plan is the same shape: base (fluency and sentences, nothing timed), build (the harder moves, knowledge units, the midpoint practice test), peak (timed sections on unfamiliar texts), taper (retrieval only, nothing new, sleep). Two rules from the track that transfer directly: never peak early, and the hard session is only as good as the recovery after it. The routine is capped at twenty minutes for this reason. Longer is not more; it is slower recovery.<\/p>\n<p><strong>6. Watch the tape<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Every team reviews film, and the review is more valuable than the game, because the game happens once and the film can be stopped. Every session in the program is recorded, and the student tags the moment the tutor named the error in their own sentence. Those tagged moments are the \"watch again\" list the week before a test. Watching yourself overlook a qualifier at minute fourteen does more than being told you tend to.<\/p>\n<p><strong>7. Run the after-action review, without blame<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>After every operation the Army asks four questions in a fixed order: what was supposed to happen, what happened, why, what changes. The review is blameless, which is what makes it honest; nobody hides a mistake they will not be punished for naming. The student's version is the weekly audit from chapter 18: the certain-and-wrong list, the build of each distractor, the chapter it points to, one change for next week. One change, not five. The tutor's version runs after every cohort and rewrites the playbook.<\/p>\n<p><strong>8. You know it when you can teach it<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The Army trains in three steps: document, demonstrate, duplicate. Write it down, show it, have them do it until it matches. The last step is where learning happens, and the strongest form of it is teaching. In a cohort, students explain passages to each other and find the seams in each other's claims. Alone, the explain-it-aloud drill is the same thing to a timer. A student who can teach the move to the person next to them has it in a way that a student who can recognize it does not.<\/p>\n<p><strong>9. Measure against yourself, on a few numbers<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>A sprinter knows their splits to the hundredth and does not know, or care, what the sprinter in the next lane ran in practice. A battery tracks the time from fire mission to rounds out. The program reports five numbers every Friday, and two of them matter: the independent score on an unfamiliar text, and hints per task. Both are compared with the student's own last week. No percentiles, no other students, no streaks. A team that measures the wrong thing trains for the wrong thing, and most reading programs measure minutes.<\/p>\n<p><strong>10. The team holds the standard<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Standards are what a group tolerates. On a good team, the standard is enforced sideways, by peers, before a coach has to say anything. A cohort of six does this: when one student answers before pointing to the sentence, another one asks for the sentence. The tutor's role is coach, not answer key, and the coach's standard is enforced the same way, by two reviewed recordings a month against a checklist. Nothing is tolerated twice.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Compounding: what a season adds up to<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The reason to run the machine is arithmetic. Three 20-minute sessions a week for twelve weeks is 36 sessions. In those sessions a student reads about a hundred passages, selects evidence on about 200 questions, writes about 70 claims and 36 closed-book summaries, retrieves roughly 150 vocabulary words on schedule, and completes 12 oral reading checkpoints. None of those numbers is large. All of them are reps, and reps compound: the hundredth passage is read with a schema the first did not have, a word bank the tenth did not have, and three moves that no longer cost attention. A weekend of cramming produces none of that, and feels like more.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Phase<\/th>\n<th>Weeks<\/th>\n<th>Workspace sessions<\/th>\n<th>Passages<\/th>\n<th>Claims written<\/th>\n<th>Checkpoints<\/th>\n<th>Long read<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Base<\/td>\n<td>1 to 4<\/td>\n<td>12<\/td>\n<td>30<\/td>\n<td>20<\/td>\n<td>4<\/td>\n<td>Begun<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Build<\/td>\n<td>5 to 8<\/td>\n<td>12<\/td>\n<td>36<\/td>\n<td>26<\/td>\n<td>4, plus practice test<\/td>\n<td>Continued<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Peak<\/td>\n<td>9 to 11<\/td>\n<td>9<\/td>\n<td>30, timed<\/td>\n<td>20<\/td>\n<td>3, hints off<\/td>\n<td>Finished<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Taper<\/td>\n<td>12<\/td>\n<td>3, short<\/td>\n<td>4<\/td>\n<td>4<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>None<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Season<\/td>\n<td>12<\/td>\n<td>36<\/td>\n<td>100<\/td>\n<td>70<\/td>\n<td>12<\/td>\n<td>One whole book<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>A training week<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Day<\/th>\n<th>What<\/th>\n<th>Minutes<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Monday<\/td>\n<td>Workspace session 1 (routine)<\/td>\n<td>20<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tuesday<\/td>\n<td>Long read, logged<\/td>\n<td>40<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wednesday<\/td>\n<td>Workspace session 2 (routine)<\/td>\n<td>20<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thursday<\/td>\n<td>Tutor session: checkpoint review, demonstration, guided tasks, explain aloud, the Friday line<\/td>\n<td>60<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Friday<\/td>\n<td>Read the report; retrieve vocabulary due<\/td>\n<td>10<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Saturday<\/td>\n<td>Workspace session 3 plus the oral reading checkpoint<\/td>\n<td>25<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sunday<\/td>\n<td>Long read, logged; the certain-and-wrong audit<\/td>\n<td>50<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>About three and a half hours a week, none of it over an hour at a stretch, with one day that is only reading. It is less time than most students spend on a prep class, and it is all reps.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Write your own training week on one page, with the days, the minutes, and the one number you will check on Friday. Put it where you will see it. Then run it for two weeks before you change anything. The first change you want to make will be to skip the oral reading; do not. The second will be to add time; do not. Teams get good by running the standard, not by improving it every week.<\/p>"},
{"title": "20. Data with text", "part": "Part Three: Applications", "html": "<p>The SAT puts a small table or graph beside a short passage and asks which data would support a claim. The GMAT builds a whole section on it. The ACT has done it for years in science. Students treat these as math questions and lose points on them for the same reason they lose points everywhere else: the sentence beside the data says less than the data seems to, or more, and nobody checked.<\/p>\n<p>The move is the same as chapter 1 with one addition. Find the sentence that matters. Then find the cell or the bar that the sentence is about, and ask whether they agree.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The three mismatches<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Every data-with-text question is built on one of three gaps between the numbers and the words.<\/p>\n<p><em>The sentence claims more than the data show.<\/em> The table shows two years; the sentence says \"every year.\" The graph shows a rise in one city; the sentence says \"cities.\" The data are a sample; the sentence is a law. This is chapter 2's overclaim, with a table attached.<\/p>\n<p><em>The sentence is about a different column.<\/em> The question asks about cost per student; the student reads the total-cost column. The question asks about the change; the student reads the level. Column headers are qualifiers. Read them the way you read \"nearly.\"<\/p>\n<p><em>The data show a sequence; the sentence claims a cause.<\/em> Two lines on a graph move together; the sentence says one moved the other. This is chapter 4 with axes.<\/p>\n<p><strong>A worked example<\/strong><\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A town reports the following for its two library branches after the North branch dropped late fees in 2024 and the South branch kept them.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Branch<\/th>\n<th>Card registrations, 2023<\/th>\n<th>Card registrations, 2025<\/th>\n<th>Overdue items per 100 loans, 2023<\/th>\n<th>Overdue items per 100 loans, 2025<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>North (fees dropped)<\/td>\n<td>1,200<\/td>\n<td>1,640<\/td>\n<td>6<\/td>\n<td>9<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>South (fees kept)<\/td>\n<td>1,150<\/td>\n<td>1,210<\/td>\n<td>6<\/td>\n<td>6<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The passage states: \"Dropping fees at the North branch brought in new readers, at a cost in overdue items that the South branch, which kept its fees, did not pay.\"<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>Which choice best describes how the table supports the passage's claim?<\/p>\n<p>A. It shows that dropping fees increased registrations at both branches. B. It shows that North's registrations rose far more than South's while North's overdue rate rose and South's did not, which is consistent with the claim. C. It proves that late fees cause lower registration. D. It shows that overdue items rose at both branches.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Walkthrough<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The sentence that matters is the passage's claim, and it has two parts: new readers, and a cost South did not pay. Find the cells. Registrations: North 1,200 to 1,640, up about 37 percent; South 1,150 to 1,210, up about 5 percent. Overdue: North 6 to 9; South 6 to 6. Both parts of the claim have a cell behind them.<\/p>\n<p>Now the seams. A is wrong on the column: South's registrations barely moved, and South did not drop fees. D is wrong on the column: South's overdue rate did not rise. C is <em>too strong<\/em>: two branches in one town is a comparison, and a useful one, but \"proves\" and \"cause\" are three rungs past what two branches can carry; other things may differ between North and South. B says what the cells say and uses <em>consistent with<\/em>, which is the right rung. B.<\/p>\n<p>Notice that the table does something the passage alone could not: it gives a comparison group. South is the branch that did not get the change. That is why the claim in this passage is stronger than the claim in the later-bell passage, where no school had one change without the other. When a table gives you a comparison group, say so; when it does not, the claim is a sequence.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The routine for any data item<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Read the sentence first, not the table. Underline its claim and its qualifiers.<\/li>\n<li>Find the exact row and column the claim is about. Say the header aloud.<\/li>\n<li>Compute the one number the claim depends on: a difference, a ratio, a direction. Not more.<\/li>\n<li>Ask whether the table has a comparison. If yes, the claim can reach <em>consistent with<\/em> or <em>supports<\/em>. If no, it can reach <em>rose after<\/em>.<\/li>\n<li>Read the choices for seams: wrong column, too strong, cause from sequence.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Using the table above:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Write the strongest claim about fees and registrations the table allows, with the rung from the ladder.<\/li>\n<li>A student writes: \"Dropping fees raised registrations by 37 percent.\" Name the seam.<\/li>\n<li>What would the table need to show for the claim \"late fees do not affect overdue rates\" to be supported?<\/li>\n<li>Which single cell would most weaken the passage's claim if it changed, and to what?<\/li>\n<li>Rewrite the passage's claim so that it reaches exactly as far as the table and no further.<\/li>\n<\/ol>"},
{"title": "21. The adult candidate", "part": "Part Three: Applications", "html": "<p>If you are reading this for the GRE or the GMAT, the method is the same and the stakes are compressed. The GMAT's verbal section is 23 questions in 45 minutes, all reading comprehension and critical reasoning; sentence correction is gone. The GRE's two verbal sections are 12 and 15 questions of reading, text completion, and sentence equivalence, with harder vocabulary. Neither test rewards grammar drills anymore. Both reward the three moves at speed, and both have a few question families that deserve their own chapter.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Critical reasoning is chapter 7 with a stopwatch<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Every critical reasoning item is an argument: a claim, evidence, and an unstated warrant. The question asks you to do one of five things to it, and each one is a question about the warrant.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Family<\/th>\n<th>The question asks<\/th>\n<th>What you do<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Strengthen<\/td>\n<td>Which choice most supports the argument<\/td>\n<td>Find the warrant; pick the choice that makes it more likely to be true<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weaken<\/td>\n<td>Which choice most undermines it<\/td>\n<td>Find the warrant; pick the choice that breaks it, usually by supplying an alternative cause or a missing comparison<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assumption<\/td>\n<td>Which choice must be true for the argument to hold<\/td>\n<td>State the warrant; the answer is the warrant, or something it requires<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluate<\/td>\n<td>What would help determine whether the argument is sound<\/td>\n<td>Name the fact that would settle the warrant one way or the other<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inference<\/td>\n<td>What must be true given the passage<\/td>\n<td>The weakest claim the evidence forces; never a rung higher<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>The method, every time: read the argument, write the warrant in six words, then read the choices against the warrant and nothing else. A choice that is true, relevant, and interesting but does not touch the warrant is the test's favorite wrong answer.<\/p>\n<p><em>An example.<\/em> \"The city's new express bus has cut average commute times on the north corridor by 11 minutes since it opened. The council concludes the bus should be extended to the east corridor.\" Warrant: what cut north-corridor times would cut east-corridor times too. Weaken: the east corridor's delays come from a bridge the bus cannot avoid. Strengthen: the two corridors have similar traffic and distance. Assumption: nothing else changed on the north corridor when the bus opened. All three answers are the warrant, from different sides.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Text completion and sentence equivalence are chapter 2 with a blank<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The sentence's logic decides the answer, and the vocabulary only matters once you know what the blank must mean. Read the sentence without the choices. Find the turn words (<em>although<\/em>, <em>despite<\/em>, <em>yet<\/em>) and the agreement words (<em>and<\/em>, <em>indeed<\/em>, <em>because<\/em>). Predict the blank in plain English: \"a word meaning careful.\" Then look. On sentence equivalence, the two right answers mean the same thing; a choice with no partner is wrong however well it fits.<\/p>\n<p>Two blanks or three: fill the one with the most surrounding evidence first, usually the last, then use it to constrain the others.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Reading at test density<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>GRE and GMAT passages are denser than anything on the SAT: one sentence can carry a reported view, a concession, and the author's own judgment. Two adjustments to the moves.<\/p>\n<p><em>Map the voices before the questions.<\/em> In a passage with a historian, a critic, and the author, write three letters in the margin as each speaks. The whose-view questions are the majority on these tests.<\/p>\n<p><em>The author's own line is usually short and late.<\/em> \"It was not quite that.\" \"The second reading has been preferred for no better reason than that it was proposed first.\" Find it before the questions; most of them point at it.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The non-native tax, and how to stop paying it<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>If English is not your first language, you lose verbal points in four places, and none of them is vocabulary size.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Qualifiers and quantifiers.<\/em> <em>Some<\/em>, <em>most<\/em>, <em>nearly all<\/em> are different claims in English and the difference is tested constantly. Chapter 2's table is worth memorizing.<\/li>\n<li><em>Reasoning verbs.<\/em> <em>Suggests<\/em>, <em>concedes<\/em>, <em>counters<\/em>, <em>credits<\/em> each carry a stance. Chapter 5's table is the second thing to memorize.<\/li>\n<li><em>Idiom at the sentence level.<\/em> \"Not without merit,\" \"it is tempting to,\" \"less true than she would have liked.\" These are the understatements and distances of chapter 6, and they are learned by reading at the edge, not by lists.<\/li>\n<li><em>Stamina.<\/em> The 45th minute. Chapter 16's drills and the whole-book reading are the only cure, and the oral reading checkpoint shows the parsing giving way before you feel it.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>The word banks address the vocabulary itself by root, so that an unfamiliar word on test day half-explains itself. But the points are mostly in the four items above, and three of them are not vocabulary at all.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Write the warrant, in six words, for the express-bus argument. Then write one strengthen, one weaken, and one assumption answer of your own.<\/li>\n<li>Take any dense paragraph from Part One and mark each sentence with the letter of its speaker. Circle the author's own line.<\/li>\n<li>Find three sentences in this book that use <em>although<\/em> or <em>despite<\/em> and predict the second half of each from the first before reading it.<\/li>\n<li>Read one passage at test density aloud, timed. Note where your phrasing broke. That is where the syntax beat you, and it is the next lesson.<\/li>\n<\/ol>"},
{"title": "22. From the claim to the essay", "part": "Part Three: Applications", "html": "<p>Everything in this book about reading is also about writing, because the two-sentence claim from chapter 7 is an essay in miniature. An essay is the claim, the evidence, and the warrant, repeated three or four times under one bigger claim, with the strongest objection answered. The AP exams, the GRE's analytical writing task, and the college essay are all scored on the same three things, and all three have names.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The three things every scorer is looking for<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><em>A defensible claim, stated first.<\/em> Not a topic, not a question, not \"there are many factors.\" One sentence that takes a position someone could disagree with, in the first paragraph, ideally the first line. Soldiers call it bottom line up front; the AP rubric calls it a thesis; the GRE rubric calls it a clear position. It is the same thing, and most essays that fail fail here.<\/p>\n<p><em>A line of reasoning.<\/em> The order of the paragraphs is itself an argument: this, therefore this, therefore this. A reader should be able to list your paragraph claims in order and see the big claim arrive. If the paragraphs could be shuffled without loss, there is no line of reasoning; there is a list.<\/p>\n<p><em>Commentary.<\/em> The sentence after the quotation that says what the quotation does. This is where the score lives, and it is the part students skip. Evidence without commentary is the chapter 7 error, a quotation relevant but not establishing the conclusion, at paragraph scale. The test: for every piece of evidence, is there a sentence in your own words that begins, in effect, \"this matters because\"?<\/p>\n<p><strong>The BLUF paragraph<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The first paragraph does four things in four sentences, and no more.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>The claim, as a sentence someone could dispute.<\/li>\n<li>The reason, in one line: \"because\" and the warrant.<\/li>\n<li>The strongest objection, named, so the reader knows you have seen it.<\/li>\n<li>The line of reasoning, as a list: \"first, then, finally.\"<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>For a rhetorical-analysis prompt on the commencement address in the worked examples: \"The speaker turns 'safety school' from an insult into evidence of care, because each repetition of 'assume' names someone who refused to take the graduates for granted. The move risks sentimentality; it avoids it by including the students among those who did not assume. First the reframing of 'safe,' then the anaphora, then the final turn to the graduates themselves.\" Four sentences, and the grader knows the whole essay.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The body paragraph, as a unit of the method<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Part<\/th>\n<th>What it is<\/th>\n<th>The chapter it comes from<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Paragraph claim<\/td>\n<td>One sentence, one step of the line of reasoning<\/td>\n<td>7<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evidence<\/td>\n<td>A quotation or a specific, as short as it can be and still carry the point<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Commentary<\/td>\n<td>What the evidence does; how far it reaches; whose view it is<\/td>\n<td>2, 5, 7<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Limit or objection<\/td>\n<td>What the evidence does not show, or what a critic would say, in one sentence<\/td>\n<td>8<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Link<\/td>\n<td>How this step leads to the next<\/td>\n<td>10<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Five moves, five sentences, give or take. A body paragraph that is twice as long is usually two paragraphs or one with the commentary missing.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Three tests, one method<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><em>AP Language.<\/em> Rhetorical analysis: the claim is about what the writer does and why; the evidence is the device in the text; the commentary names the effect on the audience. Argument: the claim is yours; the evidence comes from your own reading and knowledge, which is why chapter 15 matters here. Synthesis: chapter 10's table, with the sources as the voices.<\/p>\n<p><em>AP Literature.<\/em> The claim is an interpretation, the evidence is the line, and the commentary says what the line does. Chapter 9's competing readings become a strength: name the other reading, say why yours is better supported, and the \"sophistication\" point is in reach.<\/p>\n<p><em>GRE analytical writing.<\/em> One task, thirty minutes, a position on an issue. The BLUF paragraph, three body paragraphs, the objection handled in the third, a two-sentence close. Calibrate the claim to the ladder: a position that uses <em>in most cases<\/em> and names its exception reads as thought, not as hedging.<\/p>\n<p><em>The college essay.<\/em> Not an argument on its face, and scored on the same three things underneath: one claim about who you are, told through one specific thing you did, with commentary that says what it meant. The two-sentence summary from chapter 17 is the drill: what it showed, what it could not show. An essay that knows its own limit sounds like an adult.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a BLUF paragraph, four sentences, for the parking passage in chapter 8, arguing either side.<\/li>\n<li>Take any body paragraph you have written for school and label its five parts. Mark the missing ones.<\/li>\n<li>Rewrite one paragraph of commentary so that every sentence after the quotation begins with what the quotation does, not what it says.<\/li>\n<li>For the River, Late poem, write the first paragraph of an AP Literature response that names both readings and commits to one.<\/li>\n<li>In thirty minutes, timed, write a GRE-style response to this prompt: \"A town should never make a decision that benefits most residents at a serious cost to a few.\" Then run the chapter 18 audit on your own claim: which rung did you put it on, and does the body earn it?<\/li>\n<\/ol>"},
{"title": "23. How a passage is built", "part": "Part Four: Structure and voice", "html": "<p>Parts One to Three were about sentences: which one matters, how far it goes, what it proves. This part steps back to the whole passage. Every test asks some version of the same two questions about structure: <em>what does this paragraph do<\/em>, and <em>how is the whole thing put together<\/em>. Students who read sentence by sentence answer these by summarizing, and summary is the wrong tool. A paragraph's content is what it says. Its function is what it does for the argument. The test asks about function.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Paragraphs have jobs<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>A paragraph in an argument or an explanation does one of a small number of jobs. Learn the list and you can name any paragraph in a few seconds.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Job<\/th>\n<th>What it does<\/th>\n<th>Signals<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Sets up<\/td>\n<td>Gives background, a common view, or the problem the passage will take on<\/td>\n<td><em>For years, it was assumed; most people; traditionally<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Claims<\/td>\n<td>States the position the passage will defend or test<\/td>\n<td><em>I will argue; the truth is; she suspected<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Supports<\/td>\n<td>Gives evidence, data, or reasons for a claim<\/td>\n<td><em>found; showed; recorded; for instance<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Illustrates<\/td>\n<td>Makes a general point concrete with one case<\/td>\n<td><em>consider; for example; take the case of<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Qualifies<\/td>\n<td>Limits a claim or the evidence for it<\/td>\n<td><em>only; in one; it is not clear; the study did not<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Concedes<\/td>\n<td>Grants a point to the other side<\/td>\n<td><em>to be sure; admittedly; granted; critics are right that<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rebuts<\/td>\n<td>Answers the concession or an objection<\/td>\n<td><em>but; still; even so; yet<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shifts<\/td>\n<td>Turns from one view, period, or subject to another<\/td>\n<td><em>however; by contrast; in 2023; this changed when<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lands<\/td>\n<td>States the conclusion, often narrower than the claim<\/td>\n<td><em>so; the lesson; what this means; she concluded<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Two cautions. First, the job is usually in the first sentence of a paragraph, but the turn often arrives in the second or third: \"To be sure, X. But Y.\" is a rebuttal paragraph, not a concession paragraph, because the paragraph ends on the author's side. Read to the end of the paragraph before you name it. Second, a paragraph can do two jobs, and the test asks for the <em>primary<\/em> one: the job the rest of the paragraph serves.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The one-line map<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The single most useful habit for structure questions takes ten seconds per paragraph. After each paragraph, write a note in the margin of six words or fewer that starts with a verb. Not a summary of the content; a name for the job.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>[1]<\/strong> For most of a century, a museum painting came with a card: the artist, the year, the medium, and, more and more often, a paragraph telling the visitor what to notice. Museums added the paragraphs for a good reason. Visitors surveyed at exits said they wanted to understand what they had seen, and a paragraph seemed to deliver understanding at the cost of a few seconds.<\/p>\n<p><strong>[2]<\/strong> In 2023 the Hallam Museum, a mid-sized regional gallery, removed every card from one of its twelve rooms for six months. Its curator, Ines Adeyemi, suspected that the paragraphs were doing something other than what visitors believed: that people were reading the card instead of the painting.<\/p>\n<p><strong>[3]<\/strong> The museum's guards, asked to note how long visitors stood before each work, recorded a median of 11 seconds per painting in the labeled rooms and 19 seconds in the unlabeled one. Visitors leaving the unlabeled room were also more likely, when asked, to describe a specific detail of a painting: a hand, a color, a figure at the edge.<\/p>\n<p><strong>[4]<\/strong> The experiment had obvious limits. The guards were not trained observers, and they knew which room was which. The unlabeled room held the museum's landscapes, which may simply invite longer looking. And about one visitor in five complained, some angrily, that they had been left to guess.<\/p>\n<p><strong>[5]<\/strong> Adeyemi did not conclude that labels should go. She concluded that the museum did not know what its labels were for, and that it should find out before writing more of them. The cards went back up in the autumn, shorter.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>The map:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Sets up the practice and its case.<\/li>\n<li>States the curator's hunch.<\/li>\n<li>Gives the counts.<\/li>\n<li>Admits the limits.<\/li>\n<li>Lands a narrower conclusion.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Read down the map and the shape of the whole passage is visible in five lines: a practice, a test of it, the test's weaknesses, a conclusion smaller than the hunch. That shape is the answer to the \"overall structure\" question before the question is asked.<\/p>\n<p><strong>A worked example<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The fourth paragraph primarily serves to<\/p>\n<p>A. reject the curator's hypothesis. B. acknowledge weaknesses in the evidence presented in the third paragraph. C. explain why the museum placed its landscapes in the unlabeled room. D. describe visitors' preference for labeled paintings.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Walkthrough<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The map says <em>admits the limits<\/em>, so the answer will have a verb in the qualifies-or-concedes family and an object that is the evidence. B. Now the seams. A is <em>too strong<\/em>: limits weaken evidence; they do not reject a hypothesis, and paragraph 5 shows the curator kept a version of hers. C is <em>off<\/em>: the paragraph mentions the landscapes as a possible cause of longer looking but never explains why they were placed there. D is <em>partial<\/em>: the complaints are one sentence of four, and the question asks for the primary job. A partial answer names one sentence's content; the right answer names the whole paragraph's function.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Six shapes<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Most passages on the SAT, ACT, AP, GRE, and GMAT follow one of six overall shapes. Name the shape from your map and the structure question is matching, not reading.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Shape<\/th>\n<th>The map reads like<\/th>\n<th>Typical structure answer<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Old view, new view<\/td>\n<td>Sets up a belief; shifts; supports the new one<\/td>\n<td><em>describes a traditional view and then presents evidence that challenges it<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Problem, solution<\/td>\n<td>Sets up a problem; proposes; supports; qualifies<\/td>\n<td><em>identifies a problem and evaluates a proposed response<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Claim, objection, reply<\/td>\n<td>Claims; concedes; rebuts; lands<\/td>\n<td><em>advances a position, considers an objection, and responds to it<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Puzzle, explanations<\/td>\n<td>Sets up a puzzle; offers two or three causes; picks one<\/td>\n<td><em>presents a phenomenon and weighs competing explanations for it<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Test and limits<\/td>\n<td>Sets up; claims a hunch; supports; qualifies; lands narrower<\/td>\n<td><em>reports an investigation and the limits of its findings<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Story, reflection<\/td>\n<td>Recounts; shifts to what it meant<\/td>\n<td><em>recounts an experience and reflects on its significance<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>The museum passage is <em>test and limits<\/em>. A choice that says \"presents a theory and then proves it\" has the right shape with the wrong rung, which is the most common way structure answers are built to fail.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The routine for any structure question<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Map every paragraph as you read, six words, verb first. Do not wait for the question.<\/li>\n<li>For a function question, find the paragraph's line on your map and read the choices for the verb family first, the object second.<\/li>\n<li>For an overall structure question, read your map top to bottom and name the shape. Eliminate choices whose order is wrong before you weigh their wording.<\/li>\n<li>Check the rung. \"Proves,\" \"refutes,\" and \"dismisses\" are rarely the job of a paragraph that qualifies.<\/li>\n<li>Check for partial: a choice that names one sentence's content is answering a different question.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Using the museum passage:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Without looking at the map above, write your own one-line map. Compare the verbs.<\/li>\n<li>Name the job of the second sentence of paragraph 1 (\"Museums added the paragraphs for a good reason\"). Why would the author include it?<\/li>\n<li>What is the function of the words \"some angrily\" in paragraph 4?<\/li>\n<li>A classmate says the passage argues that labels harm understanding. Which paragraph corrects her, and with which words?<\/li>\n<li>Write the overall structure answer in one sentence of twenty words or fewer.<\/li>\n<\/ol>"},
{"title": "24. Why is this here?", "part": "Part Four: Structure and voice", "html": "<p>Chapter 23 named what whole paragraphs do. This chapter goes down a level, to the detail, the example, the quotation, the number. The questions sound like this: <em>The author mentions the hospital riders primarily to. The reference to the guards serves mainly to. The main purpose of the passage is to.<\/em> They are purpose questions, and they have a reliable trick: the answer is never what the detail says. It is what the detail does to the sentence or claim around it.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The delete test<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The fastest way to find a detail's purpose is to take it out. Cover the detail with a finger, reread the paragraph without it, and ask: <em>what did the paragraph just lose?<\/em> Whatever was lost is the purpose.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Transit planners like to say that riders will walk a quarter mile to a bus and no farther. The number appears in manuals, in grant applications, and in the maps that decide where stops go. When the city of Corland surveyed 2,000 riders in 2022, it found that most did walk about that far. It also found that riders over 70 walked, on average, less than half that distance, and that riders heading to a hospital walked farther than anyone. One planner, asked what she took from the survey, said that the quarter mile was a fine description of the average rider and a poor one of almost every actual rider.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>Cover \"riders heading to a hospital walked farther than anyone.\" What is lost? Without it, the only variation in the paragraph runs one way: some riders walk <em>less<\/em>. The rule would look like an overestimate, fixable by moving stops closer. With it, variation runs both ways, and the quarter mile is shown to be an average sitting over people who walk very different distances for very different reasons. That is exactly what the planner's last line needs. The detail's purpose is to make the variation two-sided, so the conclusion about averages can land.<\/p>\n<p><strong>A worked example<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The author mentions riders heading to a hospital primarily to<\/p>\n<p>A. show that the quarter-mile rule underestimates how far all riders walk. B. give a second case in which walking distance depends on who the rider is and why they travel, extending the point made about older riders. C. suggest that hospitals should be built closer to bus stops. D. criticize planners for ignoring riders' medical needs.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Walkthrough<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The delete test says <em>makes the variation two-sided<\/em>. B. A is <em>too strong<\/em>: \"all riders\" contradicts the sentence before it about older riders and the one before that about most riders. C is <em>outside<\/em>: the passage proposes nothing about where to build hospitals. D is <em>wrong tone<\/em>: the author reports the survey and quotes a planner; nothing in the passage blames anyone, and \"criticize\" is a verb the evidence cannot carry.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The function verbs<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Purpose answers are built on a small vocabulary of verbs, and the wrong answers are usually the right family with the wrong direction or the wrong strength. Sort the verbs once and you will read the choices in half the time.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Family<\/th>\n<th>Verbs<\/th>\n<th>Direction<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Show it<\/td>\n<td>illustrate, exemplify, make concrete, dramatize<\/td>\n<td>Same direction as the claim, no added proof<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prove it<\/td>\n<td>support, substantiate, corroborate, confirm<\/td>\n<td>Same direction, adds weight<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Limit it<\/td>\n<td>qualify, concede, acknowledge, temper<\/td>\n<td>Same claim, smaller reach<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Push back<\/td>\n<td>challenge, undercut, complicate, rebut, refute<\/td>\n<td>Against a claim; <em>complicate<\/em> is the weakest, <em>refute<\/em> the strongest<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Set up<\/td>\n<td>introduce, provide context, define, frame<\/td>\n<td>Before the claim<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shift<\/td>\n<td>contrast, transition, juxtapose<\/td>\n<td>Between two things<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Land<\/td>\n<td>conclude, emphasize, summarize, underscore<\/td>\n<td>After the claim<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Then check the strength inside the family, using the ladder from chapter 18. <em>Complicate<\/em> is to <em>refute<\/em> what <em>suggests<\/em> is to <em>proves<\/em>. On the purpose of the Corland passage as a whole, \"refute the quarter-mile rule\" is too strong (the survey confirms the rule for the average rider), \"defend the rule\" is wrong direction, and \"complicate a planning rule by showing how it misdescribes individual riders\" is the right family at the right rung.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Attitude verbs<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Some purpose answers carry an attitude: the author mentions X to <em>mock<\/em>, <em>praise<\/em>, <em>lament<\/em>, <em>celebrate<\/em>, <em>warn<\/em>. These are the most often wrong, because attitude needs a signal in the words, and most passages are written in a level voice. Before you pick an attitude verb, find the word that carries the attitude. If you cannot point to it, the choice is too strong. Chapter 6's irony signals apply: a gap between the words and the situation, a quotation the author distances from, an understatement.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Purpose of the whole passage<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The main purpose question is a structure question in disguise. Read your one-line map and look at the last line. A passage that lands on a recommendation was written to <em>argue<\/em>. One that lands on an open question was written to <em>raise<\/em> or <em>explore<\/em>. One that lands on a narrower conclusion than it began with was written to <em>qualify<\/em> or <em>complicate<\/em>. One that lands on a summary of facts was written to <em>explain<\/em> or <em>describe<\/em>.<\/p>\n<p>Two wrong answers appear on almost every main purpose question. The <em>too narrow<\/em> answer is the purpose of one paragraph offered as the purpose of the passage. The <em>too broad<\/em> answer is a theme the passage gestures at but never takes on: \"to examine the relationship between cities and the elderly\" for a five-sentence paragraph about one survey.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The routine for any purpose question<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Find the detail. Read the sentence before it and the sentence after it.<\/li>\n<li>Cover it and ask what is lost. Write the loss in five words.<\/li>\n<li>Pick the verb family that matches the loss, then the strength inside the family.<\/li>\n<li>For an attitude verb, point to the word that carries the attitude, or eliminate it.<\/li>\n<li>For main purpose, read the last line of your map; eliminate too narrow and too broad first.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>In the Corland passage, why does the author mention that the number appears \"in manuals, in grant applications, and in the maps that decide where stops go\"?<\/li>\n<li>What is the function of the planner's quotation at the end?<\/li>\n<li>Apply the delete test to \"it found that most did walk about that far.\" What would the passage seem to argue without it?<\/li>\n<li>Return to the museum passage in chapter 23. Why does the author mention that the unlabeled room held the landscapes?<\/li>\n<li>Choose the verb and defend it in one sentence: the primary purpose of the Corland passage is to <em>refute<\/em>, <em>complicate<\/em>, or <em>defend<\/em> a planning rule.<\/li>\n<\/ol>"},
{"title": "25. Who is telling the story?", "part": "Part Four: Structure and voice", "html": "<p>Chapter 5 asked <em>whose view is this<\/em> about arguments: the author, a critic, a study. Fiction asks the same question with more layers. There is the author, who wrote every word. There is the narrator, who tells the story and may be a character in it. There are the characters, whose thoughts the narrator may report, quote, or quietly borrow. A literary passage question that asks what someone believes, feels, or suggests is almost always a question about which of these three is speaking in a given sentence. Students who read fiction as the author's direct statement miss these items at a rate that does not improve with more reading. They improve when they learn to hear the voices.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Four points of view<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Point of view<\/th>\n<th>Signals<\/th>\n<th>What the narrator can know<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>First person<\/td>\n<td><em>I, we<\/em>; the narrator is in the story<\/td>\n<td>Only what the narrator saw, heard, felt, or was told; often looking back from later<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Third person limited<\/td>\n<td><em>he, she, they<\/em>; one character's thoughts reported<\/td>\n<td>One character's inner life; everyone else from the outside<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Third person omniscient<\/td>\n<td><em>he, she, they<\/em>; many characters' thoughts, or facts no character knows<\/td>\n<td>Everything, including what is coming<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Second person<\/td>\n<td><em>you<\/em> as the main character<\/td>\n<td>Rare; often makes a reader complicit or recalls a habit (\"you learn not to ask\")<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>The first question on any literary passage is which of these you are in. It takes one paragraph to tell, and it decides how much you can trust what follows.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The narrator who borrows a character's words<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The hardest and most tested move in fiction is the one critics call <em>free indirect discourse<\/em>: a third-person narrator who slips into a character's way of thinking without quotation marks or \"he thought.\" The sentence is grammatically the narrator's. The judgment in it is the character's.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Teodor had decided, on the train, that he would not mention the money. His sister had always been careless with money, and it would only embarrass her, and anyway it was not so much. When she met him at the station she was wearing a new coat. Of course she was. He kissed her on both cheeks and asked about the children, and she told him about the children for the whole length of the drive, which was what she did when she did not want to be asked anything else.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>\"Of course she was\" is not a fact about the coat. A neutral narrator would not say it. It is Teodor's resentment, spoken in the narration's voice. So is \"anyway it was not so much,\" which is the sound of a man talking himself into something. The signals that a narrator is borrowing:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Words or phrases the character would use and a neutral reporter would not: <em>anyway, of course, naturally, poor thing.<\/em><\/li>\n<li>Exclamations and questions without quotation marks.<\/li>\n<li>Judgments of other characters that the passage does not otherwise support.<\/li>\n<li>A sudden shift in rhythm: a short sentence after long ones, often the character's verdict.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>A worked example<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The sentence \"Of course she was\" most likely reflects<\/p>\n<p>A. the narrator's objective report that the coat was expensive. B. Teodor's resentful judgment, rendered in the narration without quotation marks. C. the sister's pride in her new coat. D. the author's view that the sister is careless.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Walkthrough<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>B. A is wrong on <em>objective<\/em>: nothing in the passage says the coat was expensive, and \"of course\" is a judgment, not a report. C is the <em>wrong speaker<\/em>: the passage never enters the sister's thoughts. D is the trap that catches strong readers, and it is chapter 5's error in fiction: it takes a character's view and gives it to the author. The author has written a man who thinks his sister is careless. Whether she is, the passage does not say.<\/p>\n<p>Now the deeper reading, the kind that separates a 700 from a 780 and a 4 from a 5 on the AP essay. Look at the last sentence: the sister talks about the children \"when she did not want to be asked anything else.\" That is Teodor's reading of her, and the passage hands it to us inside his point of view. But Teodor, in the first sentence, had already decided not to raise a subject himself. Both of them are avoiding something. The narration lets the reader see what Teodor cannot: that the habit he resents in her is one he is practicing at that moment. That gap between what a character sees and what the passage shows is where literary meaning usually lives.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The narrator looking back<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>First-person narrators often tell a story from years later, and the passage carries two selves: the one who lived it and the one who is telling it. The older self knows more and often signals it in a single word.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I was eleven that summer, and I believed every word my uncle said, including the ones about the war.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>\"Including\" does the work. The eleven-year-old believed everything. The adult telling the story is quietly letting you know that the war stories, at least, were not to be believed. Questions about a narrator's attitude toward a younger self (<em>affectionate, rueful, amused, regretful<\/em>) are answered by finding these words: <em>then, at the time, I believed, I did not yet know, it seemed.<\/em><\/p>\n<p><strong>The unreliable narrator<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Some narrators are wrong about their own stories, through innocence, vanity, or self-defense. The rule for reading them is simple and holds on every test: <em>trust the details over the summaries.<\/em> When a narrator says \"we were a happy family\" and the passage shows a dinner where nobody speaks, the passage means the dinner. The tested skill is noticing the gap, and the right answer usually describes it in mild words: the narrator <em>does not fully recognize<\/em>, <em>seems unaware<\/em>, <em>presents a more favorable account than the details support<\/em>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The routine for any literary passage<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Name the point of view in the first paragraph.<\/li>\n<li>For any judgment in the narration, ask: whose word is this? Look for the borrowing signals.<\/li>\n<li>For a first-person narrator, find the then-and-now words. Mark where the older self speaks.<\/li>\n<li>Where the narrator summarizes, check the summary against the details. If they disagree, believe the details.<\/li>\n<li>Never assign a character's view to the author. The author's view, if the question asks for it, comes from the shape of the whole passage, not from any one character's line.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Name the point of view in the Teodor passage, and the sentence that tells you.<\/li>\n<li>Find one phrase besides \"Of course she was\" that sounds like Teodor rather than a neutral narrator.<\/li>\n<li>What do Teodor and his sister have in common in this passage? Which sentences show it?<\/li>\n<li>Rewrite \"Of course she was\" as a neutral third-person sentence. What does the passage lose?<\/li>\n<li>In the uncle sentence, what does the adult narrator now believe, and which word tells you?<\/li>\n<\/ol>"},
{"title": "26. The science passage", "part": "Part Five: Reading by domain", "html": "<p>Students who are strong in English often fear the science passage, and students who are strong in science often rush it. Both lose points the same way. A science passage on a reading test is not testing science. It is testing whether you can keep six things apart: the question, the hypothesis, the method, the result, the interpretation, and the limit. Almost every item lives in the gap between two of them, most often between what was found and what was concluded.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The six parts<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Label them in the margin as you read, one letter each. Most passages give you all six in a few hundred words.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Mark<\/th>\n<th>Part<\/th>\n<th>The question it answers<\/th>\n<th>Signals<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Q<\/td>\n<td>Question<\/td>\n<td>What did they want to know?<\/td>\n<td><em>wondered; wanted to know; whether<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>H<\/td>\n<td>Hypothesis<\/td>\n<td>What did they expect, and why?<\/td>\n<td><em>suspected; predicted; proposed; if X, then Y<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>M<\/td>\n<td>Method<\/td>\n<td>What did they do to find out?<\/td>\n<td><em>raised; measured; compared; divided into groups<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>R<\/td>\n<td>Result<\/td>\n<td>What did they observe?<\/td>\n<td>numbers; <em>found; recorded; were shorter<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I<\/td>\n<td>Interpretation<\/td>\n<td>What do they think it means?<\/td>\n<td><em>concluded; suggests; indicates; accounts for<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>L<\/td>\n<td>Limit<\/td>\n<td>What can the study not show?<\/td>\n<td><em>cautioned; only; one; did not measure; further work<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Gardeners have long claimed that seedlings raised indoors grow tall and weak, and that a few minutes of brushing each day makes them sturdier. Plant biologist Mara Okafor wanted to know whether the effect was real and, if it was, whether it came from the touch itself or from the moving air that brushing stirs up. She raised 240 tomato seedlings in four groups: brushed daily, fanned daily, brushed and fanned, and left alone. After four weeks, the brushed seedlings were on average 18 percent shorter than the untouched ones, with thicker stems; the brushed-and-fanned seedlings looked much like the brushed ones; and the fanned seedlings differed from the untouched ones by less than 3 percent on every measure. Okafor concluded that touch, not moving air, accounts for most of the effect. She cautioned that her seedlings were grown under one light level in one greenhouse, and that she had not measured whether the sturdier plants produce more fruit, which is the outcome gardeners care about.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>The labels: the gardeners' claim and Okafor's two questions (Q, with a hypothesis inside: touch or air), the four groups (M), the three findings (R), \"touch, not moving air\" (I), and the last sentence (L).<\/p>\n<p><strong>Why the groups are the point<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The method is where the reasoning in a science passage lives, and the test knows it. Ask of every design: <em>what is each group for?<\/em> The untouched group is the comparison; without it, nobody knows whether brushed seedlings are short or just seedlings. The fanned group is the clever part: it separates the two possible causes. Brushing a seedling both bends it and moves air past it. If only the brushed group existed, a short seedling could be the work of either. The fanned group gets air without touch, and it barely differed from untouched plants. That single group is what lets Okafor say <em>touch, not air<\/em>. Questions that ask \"why did the researcher include\" a group are answered by naming the cause it rules out.<\/p>\n<p><strong>A worked example<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Which finding, if true, would most weaken Okafor's conclusion?<\/p>\n<p>A. The brushed-and-fanned seedlings were slightly shorter than the brushed seedlings. B. The brush Okafor used shaded the brushed seedlings from the greenhouse lights for part of each day. C. Gardeners in many countries brush their seedlings. D. Shorter tomato plants sometimes produce less fruit than taller ones.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Walkthrough<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The conclusion is that <em>touch<\/em> causes most of the effect. To weaken it, find another difference between the brushed group and the others: chapter 4's alternative cause. B supplies one. If the brushed plants also got less light, shading could explain some of their growth, and touch is no longer the only thing that differed. B. A is <em>consistent<\/em>: a little extra effect from air does not touch \"most.\" C is <em>irrelevant<\/em>: how many people brush seedlings says nothing about why it works. D is <em>right topic, wrong claim<\/em>: it is about fruit, which Okafor already said she did not measure; it would matter to gardeners, but it does not weaken a conclusion about height and stems.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The vocabulary of method<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>A handful of words in science passages mean something narrower than they do in conversation, and the test writes wrong answers on the everyday meaning.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term<\/th>\n<th>In a science passage<\/th>\n<th>The trap<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Hypothesis<\/td>\n<td>A testable guess, stated before the result<\/td>\n<td>Treated as a finding<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Control or comparison group<\/td>\n<td>The group that did not get the change<\/td>\n<td>Missing, so a sequence is read as a cause<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Variable<\/td>\n<td>A thing that differs between groups or over time<\/td>\n<td>Two variables changed at once and one is credited<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sample<\/td>\n<td>Who or what was studied<\/td>\n<td>Generalized to everyone or everything<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Significant<\/td>\n<td>Unlikely to be due to chance alone, in a statistical sense<\/td>\n<td>Read as <em>large<\/em> or <em>important<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Correlated<\/td>\n<td>Tend to move together<\/td>\n<td>Read as <em>one causes the other<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Consistent with<\/td>\n<td>Does not contradict<\/td>\n<td>Read as <em>proves<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Replicate<\/td>\n<td>Repeat the study and get the same result<\/td>\n<td>One study treated as settled<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mechanism<\/td>\n<td>How a cause produces its effect<\/td>\n<td>A result without a known mechanism dismissed, or one with a mechanism treated as proven<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Model<\/td>\n<td>A simplified representation used to predict<\/td>\n<td>Mistaken for the thing itself<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>The graph beside the passage<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>When a science passage comes with a figure, chapter 20's routine applies with one addition: read the axis labels and units before the lines. Most figure errors are axis errors: reading percent change as amount, a log scale as linear, or one group's line as another's. Say the axis aloud, the way you read a column header.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The routine for any science passage<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Label Q, H, M, R, I, L as you read. If a part is missing, note it; missing limits are a question waiting to happen.<\/li>\n<li>For each group in the method, write what it rules out.<\/li>\n<li>Put the result and the interpretation side by side. Is the interpretation the weakest claim the result supports?<\/li>\n<li>For strengthen and weaken items, look for another difference between the groups.<\/li>\n<li>Read technical words in their technical sense; <em>significant<\/em> is not <em>big<\/em>.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Label every sentence of the Okafor passage with Q, H, M, R, I, or L. Some carry two.<\/li>\n<li>Why did Okafor include the fanned-only group? What could she not have concluded without it?<\/li>\n<li>A student writes: \"Okafor proved that brushing makes tomato plants more productive.\" Name both seams.<\/li>\n<li>Okafor's own caution names two limits. Design the next study that would address both.<\/li>\n<li>Write the strongest claim the passage supports, with its rung from the ladder.<\/li>\n<\/ol>"},
{"title": "27. The social science passage", "part": "Part Five: Reading by domain", "html": "<p>Social science passages are about people: what they do, what they say they do, and what happens when an institution tries to change either. They look easier than science passages because the vocabulary is plainer. They are harder, because the evidence is messier and the passage often quotes someone with a stake in the answer. Five questions, asked of every claim, catch nearly every trap the test builds on these passages.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Five questions for any claim about people<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question<\/th>\n<th>The trap it catches<\/th>\n<th>Signals in the passage<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Who was asked?<\/td>\n<td><em>Selection<\/em>: the people studied are not the people the claim is about<\/td>\n<td><em>of those who responded; volunteers; a sample of; users of<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Did they say it or do it?<\/td>\n<td><em>Self-report<\/em>: what people report is read as what they did<\/td>\n<td><em>said; reported; described; felt; would have<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compared to what?<\/td>\n<td><em>No baseline<\/em>: a number with nothing to measure it against<\/td>\n<td><em>rose to; reached; was<\/em>, with no earlier figure or other group<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Percent of what?<\/td>\n<td><em>Base rate<\/em>: a large share of a small group read as a large effect<\/td>\n<td><em>of respondents; of users; among those who<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Who says so, and what do they want?<\/td>\n<td><em>Interested source<\/em>: an institution grading its own program<\/td>\n<td><em>the director; the company; the program's own report<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>These are chapter 2, chapter 4, and chapter 5 applied to people. A passage can be built to fail any one of them, and the best passages fail two at once.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>In 2024 the city of Brenton began giving every student at its community college a free transit pass. A year later, the college reported that 64 percent of students rode the bus at least once a week, up from 41 percent before the program. The college also surveyed students about the pass. Of the 900 who responded, out of about 6,000 enrolled, 71 percent said the pass had made it easier to get to class, and 38 percent said they had attended a class they would otherwise have skipped. The college's president called the program \"the most effective thing we have done for attendance in a decade.\" Attendance records, which the college keeps by course, show that the share of class sessions attended rose from 82 to 85 percent over the same year, a change about the size of the year-to-year swings in the five years before the program.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>Run the five questions. <em>Who was asked?<\/em> About 15 percent of students, and the ones who use and like the pass are likelier to answer a survey about it. <em>Said or did?<\/em> The 71 and 38 percent are reports; the ridership and the attendance records are behavior. <em>Compared to what?<\/em> Ridership has a before figure; attendance has a before figure and, crucially, a range of normal variation. <em>Percent of what?<\/em> Thirty-eight percent of 900 is about 340 students, under 6 percent of the college. <em>Who says so?<\/em> The president, about the college's own program.<\/p>\n<p>The passage now sorts itself. One claim is solid: bus use rose a lot. One is weak: the program's effect on attendance, which the behavior shows to be small and within normal swings. And the president's sentence is the strongest claim in the passage, resting on the weakest evidence.<\/p>\n<p><strong>A worked example<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Which choice best describes the relationship between the survey responses and the attendance records?<\/p>\n<p>A. The records confirm the survey: attendance rose sharply under the program. B. The survey suggests a larger effect on attendance than the records show, and the change in the records is within the range of normal variation. C. The records show that the pass reduced attendance. D. Together they prove the president's claim.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Walkthrough<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>B. A is <em>wrong on the number<\/em>: 82 to 85 is not sharp, and the passage says so with the five-year comparison. C is <em>inverted<\/em>: attendance rose slightly; it did not fall. D is <em>too strong<\/em> and <em>wrong speaker<\/em>: the president's sentence is a claim reported in the passage, and nothing in the passage proves it. Notice what B does: it puts the two kinds of evidence side by side, says which is larger, and uses the passage's own baseline. That is the habit the five questions build.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Averages that hide groups<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>One more trap deserves a name. An average over a whole population can move in one direction while every group inside it moves in another, or while one group moves a lot and the rest not at all. If a passage reports an overall figure and then mentions a subgroup, ask whether the overall figure is carrying the subgroup. The Corland bus survey in chapter 24 is this trap in miniature: a quarter-mile average sitting over older riders who walk half that and hospital riders who walk farther.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Measures that become targets<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>When an institution is judged by a number, people inside it learn to move the number, and the number stops meaning what it did. Economists call a version of this Goodhart's law. A passage about a school rewarded for attendance, a clinic rewarded for short wait times, or a police department rewarded for fewer reported crimes is often building to this point. The question to ask: <em>could the number improve without the thing it measures improving?<\/em><\/p>\n<p><strong>The routine for any social science passage<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>For each claim, run the five questions. Write the answers as one-word margin notes: <em>selected, reported, no baseline, small base, interested.<\/em><\/li>\n<li>Separate behavior from report. Behavior usually carries more weight.<\/li>\n<li>Find the baseline. A number with no comparison is a sequence, not an effect.<\/li>\n<li>Convert percentages of a subgroup into percentages of the whole when the question turns on size.<\/li>\n<li>Note who is quoted and what they gain. Their claim is evidence of their view, not of the fact.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Which claim in the Brenton passage is best supported, and by what?<\/li>\n<li>Why might the 900 respondents differ from all 6,000 students?<\/li>\n<li>What does the sentence about \"year-to-year swings\" do for the reader?<\/li>\n<li>Rewrite the president's sentence so that the passage's evidence supports it.<\/li>\n<li>The 38 percent figure: restate it as a share of all enrolled students, then name the second reason it may overstate the effect.<\/li>\n<\/ol>"},
{"title": "28. The historical document", "part": "Part Five: Reading by domain", "html": "<p>The SAT, the AP Language exam, and the GRE all put old prose in front of students: an address, a letter, an essay from the eighteenth or nineteenth century. Strong modern readers stall on these passages, and the reason is almost never the ideas. It is the sentences. A writer from 1840 will hold the main verb of a sentence back for forty words, stack three clauses in front of it, and use a word that meant something different then. Once the sentence is unpacked, the argument is usually simpler than anything in a modern op-ed.<\/p>\n<p>The passage below was written for this book in the style of an address from the 1840s. It is not a historical text, and no real speaker said it.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>That a people who have entrusted to every man among them a share in their own government should yet leave to chance, or to the charity of a few, the instruction by which alone that share can be wisely exercised, is a contradiction which, however long it has been tolerated, no free state can much longer afford. It will be said that the cost is great; and it is great. But let any man who urges the expense consider what he already pays, in courts and in prisons and in the slow waste of talents that were never awakened, for the want of the very schools he declines to support; and he will find, I think, that the question is not whether he shall pay, but whether he shall pay for the remedy or for the disease.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p><strong>Find the main verb<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The first sentence is sixty words long and has one main verb. Find it and the sentence collapses into something you can hold.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Bracket the openers.<\/em> \"That a people ... should yet leave ... the instruction\" is one long clause, and it is the <em>subject<\/em> of the sentence. Old prose often uses a whole clause as a subject: <em>That he came at all surprised them.<\/em> Bracket it as [the situation].<\/li>\n<li><em>Find the verb that belongs to the bracket.<\/em> Skip everything inside the brackets and the next verb is \"is.\"<\/li>\n<li><em>Find what it is.<\/em> \"a contradiction.\" Everything after \"which\" describes the contradiction.<\/li>\n<li><em>Write it in six words.<\/em> [This situation] is a contradiction we cannot afford.<\/li>\n<li><em>Now fill the bracket in plain words.<\/em> A nation that gives every man a vote but leaves his education to chance is contradicting itself, and cannot keep doing it.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>That is the margin translation, and it is the habit that turns these passages from a slog into a ninety-second read. Write it after every long sentence. Six to fifteen words, modern English, no ornament.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Watch the words that have moved<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The second sentence of the passage has a trap: \"for the want of the very schools.\" <em>Want<\/em> here means <em>lack<\/em>, not <em>desire<\/em>. Words drift, and the test asks about the old meanings because they separate careful readers from fast ones.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word<\/th>\n<th>Older meaning<\/th>\n<th>Example of the old sense<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>want<\/td>\n<td>lack<\/td>\n<td><em>for want of a nail<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>awful<\/td>\n<td>inspiring awe<\/td>\n<td><em>an awful silence in the cathedral<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>candid<\/td>\n<td>fair, impartial<\/td>\n<td><em>a candid judge of the evidence<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>nice<\/td>\n<td>precise, fine<\/td>\n<td><em>a nice distinction<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>presently<\/td>\n<td>at once<\/td>\n<td><em>he will come presently<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>enthusiasm<\/td>\n<td>excessive or unreasoning zeal, often religious; usually a criticism<\/td>\n<td><em>the sober condemned his enthusiasm<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>condescension<\/td>\n<td>gracious courtesy toward someone of lower rank; not an insult<\/td>\n<td><em>the duke's condescension charmed the town<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>interest<\/td>\n<td>a share or stake; a group with a stake<\/td>\n<td><em>the landed interest<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>liberal<\/td>\n<td>generous; fit for a free person<\/td>\n<td><em>a liberal gift; a liberal education<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sensible<\/td>\n<td>aware of<\/td>\n<td><em>sensible of the honor<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>indifferent<\/td>\n<td>impartial; or ordinary, middling<\/td>\n<td><em>an indifferent judge; an indifferent dinner<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>science<\/td>\n<td>knowledge in general<\/td>\n<td><em>the science of government<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>The rule for any \"most nearly means\" item on an old passage: substitute each choice into the sentence and read it aloud. The modern meaning will usually make the sentence nonsense (\"for the <em>desire<\/em> of the very schools he declines to support\"), and that is your signal.<\/p>\n<p><strong>A worked example<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>In context, \"want\" most nearly means<\/p>\n<p>A. desire. B. lack. C. poverty. D. request.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Walkthrough<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>B. A is the modern meaning and makes the sentence say the opposite of what it means. C is the <em>near miss<\/em>: poverty is related to lack, and a hurried reader thinking about prisons and wasted talent will reach for it, but the thing lacking is schools, not money. D has no support. The test builds the near miss on purpose; it is the choice that matches the passage's mood instead of its sentence.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The old moves are the same moves<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Under the long sentences, a historical argument uses exactly the moves of Part One. This passage has three of them.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>The concession and the turn<\/em> (chapter 8): \"It will be said that the cost is great; and it is great. But ...\" The speaker grants the objection in full, which makes the rebuttal more credible. A question on the speaker's attitude toward the cost objection wants something like <em>acknowledges it as valid and then reframes it<\/em>, not <em>dismisses it<\/em>.<\/li>\n<li><em>The reframe<\/em>: \"the question is not whether he shall pay, but whether he shall pay for the remedy or for the disease.\" The speaker changes the question rather than answering it. Cost becomes unavoidable, so the only choice left is what the cost buys.<\/li>\n<li><em>The audience<\/em>: \"any man who urges the expense,\" \"the very schools he declines to support.\" The speaker is addressing people who would have to pay for the schools and do not want to. Audience questions are answered by these pronouns and descriptions.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>The routine for any historical passage<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Read the headnote. Date, speaker, and occasion tell you the audience before the first sentence does.<\/li>\n<li>For every sentence over thirty words, bracket the openers, find the main verb, and write the margin translation.<\/li>\n<li>Flag any common word used strangely. Check it against the older meanings.<\/li>\n<li>Find the concession and the turn; old writers almost always grant before they argue.<\/li>\n<li>Name the audience and what the speaker wants them to do.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Reduce the first sentence to six words, then to fifteen.<\/li>\n<li>In context, what does \"want\" mean, and what would the sentence say if you read it the modern way?<\/li>\n<li>What does the speaker concede, and in which words?<\/li>\n<li>Name the reframe in the final clause in one plain sentence.<\/li>\n<li>Who is the audience, and which words tell you?<\/li>\n<\/ol>"},
{"title": "29. The poem, line by line", "part": "Part Five: Reading by domain", "html": "<p>Chapter 9 treated literature as argument: a poem makes a claim, and the claim needs evidence from the lines. This chapter is about the lines themselves. Students freeze in front of poems because they try to read for meaning and form at once, and a poem gives up neither to a reader who has not first read it as a set of sentences. The order matters: sentences first, speaker second, turn third, form last. Each step makes the next one possible.<\/p>\n<p>The poem below was written for this book.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>The Lake in March<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The ice goes out the way a rumor does,<br>\nnot all at once but everywhere, a gray<br>\nthat yesterday was white and now is less<br>\nthan either. All winter we walked on it,<br>\nthe children dragging sleds across the place (5)<br>\nthe boathouse floats in June, and no one thought<br>\nto ask it what it held. This morning, though,<br>\nI stood at the edge and heard it give,<br>\na sound like someone setting down a load<br>\nthey had not mentioned carrying, and I (10)<br>\nwas sorry, briefly, for the weight of us,<br>\nand then not sorry, since it bore it, since<br>\nit was a lake, and lakes do not keep score.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p><strong>Step 1: read the sentences, not the lines<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Read the poem once aloud, stopping only at punctuation, never at the end of a line. Then count the sentences. This poem has thirteen lines and three sentences, and the sentences are where the meaning is.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>The ice melts gradually and everywhere, so that it is neither white nor gray. (Lines 1 to 4)<\/li>\n<li>All winter people walked on the lake without thinking about what it was holding up. (Lines 4 to 7)<\/li>\n<li>This morning the speaker heard the ice crack, felt briefly sorry for the weight the lake had carried, and then stopped feeling sorry, because a lake does not keep accounts. (Lines 7 to 13)<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>That paraphrase is not the poem. It is the floor under the poem, and every claim you make later has to stand on it. A student who skips this step will write about \"the rumor\" as if the poem were about gossip.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Step 2: find the speaker and the situation<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Who is talking, where, when, to whom? An adult (\"the children,\" \"we\") who knows this lake through the seasons (\"the boathouse floats in June\"), standing at its edge on a March morning, talking to no one in particular. The speaker is not the poet; it is a voice the poet made, and questions about \"the speaker's attitude\" are about that voice.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Step 3: find the turn<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Most poems change direction once, sometimes twice. The turn is often marked by a small word: <em>but, yet, though, still, now<\/em>, or a shift in tense or in who is addressed. Here the first turn is \"This morning, though\" in line 7: from a general past (\"all winter\") to one present moment. The second is \"and then not sorry\" in line 12: from feeling to the release of the feeling. The poem's meaning lives in the second turn, so that is where questions about the ending will point.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Step 4: read the line endings<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Only now look at where the lines break. A line that runs past its end into the next without punctuation is <em>enjambed<\/em>, and enjambment does two things: it gives weight to the last word of the line, and it holds the sentence in suspense for a moment.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"a gray / that yesterday was white\": \"gray\" sits alone at the end of line 2, as if it were the answer, and then line 3 takes it back.<\/li>\n<li>\"and I / was sorry\": line 10 ends on \"I.\" For one breath the speaker is alone at the edge of the line, at the moment the poem turns inward.<\/li>\n<li>\"since it bore it, since / it was a lake\": the repeated \"since\" stacks two reasons, and the line break between them makes the second one, the plain fact, arrive as a small surprise.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Step 5: hear the meter, briefly<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Most lines in this poem have ten syllables and five stresses in a da-DUM pattern: <em>the ICE goes OUT the WAY a RU-mor DOES.<\/em> That pattern is <em>iambic pentameter<\/em>, the most common meter in English verse. You do not need to scan every line. You need to notice when a line breaks the pattern, because poets usually break it on purpose. Line 8, \"I stood at the edge and heard it give,\" is a beat shorter than its neighbors, at the moment the poem stops to listen.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The two similes<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The poem has two comparisons, and they work in opposite directions. The ice goes out \"the way a rumor does\": gradually, everywhere, impossible to locate, a comparison that explains the ice. The sound is \"like someone setting down a load / they had not mentioned carrying\": a comparison that gives the lake a human burden and a human silence about it. The last line takes that second comparison back. A person might keep score; a lake does not. Compare chapter 9's river, which the speaker of that poem treats as a creditor. This speaker starts to make the same move and then declines it.<\/p>\n<p><strong>A worked example<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The final line (\"lakes do not keep score\") primarily serves to<\/p>\n<p>A. reveal that the speaker blames the children for walking on the ice. B. reject the human feelings the speaker has just attributed to the lake, releasing the speaker from guilt. C. suggest that the lake will take revenge when the ice is gone. D. state the poem's lesson that nature is indifferent to all human suffering.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Walkthrough<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>B. A is <em>unsupported<\/em>: the children are part of \"us,\" and nobody is blamed. C is <em>inverted<\/em>: keeping no score is the opposite of revenge. D is the trap for confident readers: it is <em>too broad<\/em>. The poem is about one lake, one winter, and one speaker's brief guilt; \"all human suffering\" is a theme the poem never takes on. Chapter 9's rule holds: the right claim about a poem is the weakest one that accounts for the lines.<\/p>\n<p><strong>A note on form<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Some questions name a form. Know these four.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Form<\/th>\n<th>Shape<\/th>\n<th>Where the turn usually falls<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Petrarchan sonnet<\/td>\n<td>14 lines: an octave of 8, a sestet of 6<\/td>\n<td>At line 9<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shakespearean sonnet<\/td>\n<td>14 lines: three quatrains of 4, a couplet of 2<\/td>\n<td>At the couplet, line 13, sometimes also at line 9<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Villanelle<\/td>\n<td>19 lines; two lines repeat as refrains<\/td>\n<td>The refrains change meaning as the poem goes<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Free verse<\/td>\n<td>No fixed meter or rhyme<\/td>\n<td>Wherever the poem changes direction; look for the small words<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>\"The Lake in March\" is none of these exactly: thirteen lines of loose, unrhymed pentameter, which is called <em>blank verse<\/em>. A poem one line short of a sonnet is a choice a question could ask about, though the safest answer is always the one tied to what the lines do.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The routine for any poem<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Read it aloud once for sound, stopping only at punctuation.<\/li>\n<li>Count the sentences and paraphrase each in plain prose.<\/li>\n<li>Name the speaker and the situation.<\/li>\n<li>Find the turn or turns; mark the small word.<\/li>\n<li>Read the line endings; note any word the break isolates.<\/li>\n<li>Make a claim at the weakest rung that fits, with a line number for every part of it.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Paraphrase sentence 2 (lines 4 to 7) in plain prose.<\/li>\n<li>Where is the poem's first turn, and what word marks it?<\/li>\n<li>Why might the poet end line 10 on \"I\"?<\/li>\n<li>What does the rumor simile say about how the ice melts?<\/li>\n<li>A student writes: \"The poem shows that humans exploit nature.\" Name the seam and write a better claim with line numbers.<\/li>\n<\/ol>"},
{"title": "30. How to help without taking over", "part": "Part Six: For parents", "html": "<p>This chapter is for the adult in the house. You do not need to know the method to help, and you should not try to teach it; that is the tutor's job and, increasingly, your child's. What you control is the environment the method runs in: how much your child reads, what gets talked about at dinner, how a mistake is treated, and whether the habits from sessions survive the week between them. Those four things move a reading score more than any amount of help with homework.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The three questions<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>If you learn nothing else from this book, learn these. They are the three moves turned into questions a parent can ask about anything: a news article, a history assignment, a claim a sibling makes at dinner.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Where does it say that?<\/em> (Find the sentence that matters.)<\/li>\n<li><em>Does it say that, or something a little smaller?<\/em> (Ask how far it goes.)<\/li>\n<li><em>How does that sentence prove it?<\/em> (Write down why.)<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Ask them with curiosity, not as a test. The goal is that by spring your child asks them of you.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Instead of this, try this<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Instead of<\/th>\n<th>Try<\/th>\n<th>Why<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Giving the answer when they are stuck<\/td>\n<td>\"Which sentence are you looking at?\"<\/td>\n<td>The hint ladder starts with the location, never the answer<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"That's wrong\"<\/td>\n<td>\"Show me the line that makes you think so\"<\/td>\n<td>Most wrong answers have a reason; the reason is what gets fixed<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Correcting a misread word mid-sentence<\/td>\n<td>Wait until the end of the sentence; if they did not catch it, point to the word<\/td>\n<td>Interrupting trains them to wait for you; waiting trains them to self-correct<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Praising the score<\/td>\n<td>Praising the evidence: \"That was the right sentence to use\"<\/td>\n<td>Scores swing; habits compound<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Read for twenty minutes\"<\/td>\n<td>\"What are you reading? Tell me one thing that surprised you\"<\/td>\n<td>The conversation builds knowledge and makes the reading count<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vocabulary lists at the kitchen table<\/td>\n<td>Using one hard word at dinner and asking what they think it means<\/td>\n<td>Words learned in context stick; lists fade<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Comparing to a sibling or classmate<\/td>\n<td>Comparing to their own last checkpoint<\/td>\n<td>The only comparison that motivates is with yourself<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>The third row draws on a long-used approach to supporting oral reading sometimes called <em>pause, prompt, praise<\/em>: pause when the reader errs, prompt with a cue if they do not self-correct, praise the fix. It works because it gives the child the first chance to notice.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Volume is the lever you own<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Chapter 16 showed that the readers who improve fastest read the most, and chapter 15 showed that what they know shapes what they understand. Both of these happen at home, not in sessions. Three things help, and none of them is a rule.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Books in reach.<\/em> A shelf in the room where your child spends time, refreshed every few weeks, with some books a little hard and some easy. Easy reading builds speed; hard reading builds stamina.<\/li>\n<li><em>Choice.<\/em> Let them pick most of it. A teenager reading a long series they love is doing more for their reading than one dragging through an assigned classic.<\/li>\n<li><em>Your own reading, visible.<\/em> Children read in houses where adults read. Read the paper at breakfast, mention what you read, argue about it.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Audiobooks count, with one limit. Listening builds vocabulary, background knowledge, and language comprehension, which is half of the Simple View. It does not build decoding or reading speed, which only practice with print does. A child who is accurate and slow needs eyes on the page; a child who is fast and shallow can learn a great deal from listening.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Ten minutes at dinner<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The single highest-value habit a family can build is a short, regular conversation about something someone read. Not every night. Twice a week is enough. The structure is simple:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>One person shares one thing they read: an article, a chapter, a post.<\/li>\n<li>Someone asks one of the three questions.<\/li>\n<li>Someone disagrees, or names what the piece could not show.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Ten minutes, no phones, no grades. It builds background knowledge, oral argument, and calibration at once.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Reading the Friday report<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The program sends a one-page report each week. Read three numbers and ignore the rest unless something changes.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Number<\/th>\n<th>What it tells you<\/th>\n<th>What to watch for<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Evidence rate<\/td>\n<td>How often the cited sentence actually supports the answer<\/td>\n<td>Steady improvement over weeks; it leads the score<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Certain-and-wrong<\/td>\n<td>Misses the student was sure of<\/td>\n<td>It should fall; if it rises, the student is rushing<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Oral reading checkpoint<\/td>\n<td>Speed and accuracy on a fresh passage<\/td>\n<td>Accuracy below about 95 percent, or no gain in speed over six weeks<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>The score on practice sections will move later than these, and it will move unevenly. A flat score with a rising evidence rate is a good month.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When to look further<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>This program is built for fluent readers. If you see any of these, talk to your child's school or a reading specialist about an evaluation, and tell your tutor:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reading aloud is slow and effortful long after classmates have become fluent, with frequent guessing from the first letter or two of a word.<\/li>\n<li>Spelling is far weaker than speaking vocabulary and does not improve with practice.<\/li>\n<li>Your child understands a passage well when it is read to them and poorly when they read it themselves.<\/li>\n<li>Reading causes headaches, avoidance, or distress that does not ease as skill grows.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>None of these is a diagnosis. They are reasons to ask someone qualified, early, when help is most effective.<\/p>\n<p><strong>What not to do<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Do not do the homework, even part of it; the struggle is the training. Do not use reading as punishment or screen time as its reward; it teaches that reading is a cost. Do not add sessions in test week; chapter 13's taper applies to parents too. And do not let the score become the conversation. The conversation is about what they read and what it proved. The score follows that conversation; it never leads it.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The weekly fifteen minutes<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>If you want a single routine, use this one, once a week, at the same time:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Five minutes: your child shows you one passage from the week and the claim they wrote about it.<\/li>\n<li>Five minutes: you ask the three questions about that claim.<\/li>\n<li>Five minutes: together you read the three numbers on the Friday report and agree on one thing to keep doing.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>That is all. It keeps you informed without taking over, and it gives your child one more chance each week to explain their reasoning aloud, which is itself one of the strongest drills in this book.<\/p>"},
{"title": "31. Running the session", "part": "Part Seven: For tutors", "html": "<p>Everything before this chapter was written for the student and the parent. This one is for the person on the other side of the table, and it is the chapter a tutor is certified on. The method does not fail in the book. It fails in minute 23 of a session, when a student is stuck, the clock is running, and the tutor, who knows the answer, says it. Each time that happens the student learns that being stuck ends with the tutor talking. The whole of this chapter is about what to do instead.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The 60-minute session, by the minute<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Minute<\/th>\n<th>What happens<\/th>\n<th>What the tutor does<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0 to 3<\/td>\n<td>Checkpoint review: this week's independent score on an unfamiliar text, and the gap to the assisted score<\/td>\n<td>One sentence on what moved. Ask the student why before you say why<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3 to 6<\/td>\n<td>Oral reading: one paragraph of today's passage aloud, timed<\/td>\n<td>Count misread and skipped words; note phrasing. Say nothing until the end of the paragraph<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6 to 12<\/td>\n<td>Demonstration: the tutor reads the first paragraph aloud and thinks aloud<\/td>\n<td>Show the moves, not the answer: the small word, the sentence that matters, whose view. Keep it short<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12 to 32<\/td>\n<td>Guided lesson: two tasks. The sentence before the answer, then one line of why and a confidence rating<\/td>\n<td>Hold the hint ladder. Log the rung reached on each task. Name any error in the student's own words<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>32 to 42<\/td>\n<td>Claim and revise: one claim with evidence; the student names the error in their own first draft; one revision, read aloud<\/td>\n<td>Ask \"which of the eight is it?\" before offering a name<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>42 to 50<\/td>\n<td>Explain it aloud: the argument in one sentence, the strongest evidence, what a critic would say, what is uncertain<\/td>\n<td>One follow-up question and no more<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>50 to 55<\/td>\n<td>Retrieval: vocabulary due, cold; a closed-book summary in two sentences if time allows<\/td>\n<td>The student says the parts before the meaning. Mark every word that says more than the passage did<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>55 to 60<\/td>\n<td>Close: the one line for the Friday report, said aloud; the next lesson named; three workspace sessions assigned<\/td>\n<td>Let the student disagree with the line. Confirm the one number you will both watch<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Ninety-minute sessions, the six-student cohort and the international verbal session, keep the same order with more room in each segment; the tutor playbook has both by the minute. A session that runs long because the tutor was talking is a session that failed its standard.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The hint ladder, verbatim<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The ladder has three rungs, and none of them is the answer. A tutor goes up it one rung at a time, waits at each rung, and logs the rung at which the student moved. Hints per task is a Friday number because it measures how much of the work the student is doing.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Rung<\/th>\n<th>What you say<\/th>\n<th>What it gives<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Location<\/td>\n<td>\"Which paragraph is the question about? Which sentence?\"<\/td>\n<td>Where to look, nothing about what is there<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. The word<\/td>\n<td>\"Read that sentence aloud. Which word is doing the work?\" or \"What does <em>however<\/em> do here?\"<\/td>\n<td>The move, without the result of the move<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. The question<\/td>\n<td>\"What would have to be true for B to be right? Is it in the passage?\"<\/td>\n<td>The reasoning shape, with the student supplying the content<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Three rules on the ladder. First, wait. Silence of ten seconds feels long to a tutor and is normal thinking time for a student; count it. Second, never skip a rung downward. If rung 1 produced the right sentence, the next rung is 2, not the answer. Third, there is no fourth rung. If three hints have not moved the student, the item is parked: logged as a miss, opened first at the next session, and never solved for them. An answer from the tutor teaches one thing, that being stuck ends with the tutor talking.<\/p>\n<p><strong>What to say at the five moments that decide a session<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><em>The student is certain and wrong.<\/em> Do not say \"wrong.\" Say \"Show me the sentence.\" Certainty that survives contact with the sentence is rare; let the sentence do the correcting, and then ask which build the distractor was.<\/p>\n<p><em>The student is right for the wrong reason.<\/em> Say \"Right answer. Which sentence proves it?\" If the sentence they point to does not prove it, the item is logged as unlinked, and that is the lesson, not the correct letter.<\/p>\n<p><em>The student says \"I just knew.\"<\/em> Say \"Then teach it to me as if I did not.\" The explanation is the skill; a correct answer without one does not transfer to a passage they have never seen.<\/p>\n<p><em>The student is stuck and silent.<\/em> Rung 1, and wait. If rung 1 is silent too, read the sentence aloud yourself, slowly, and stop before the end. Most students finish it.<\/p>\n<p><em>The student disagrees with the key.<\/em> Take it seriously. Ask them to make the case from the sentence. If the case holds, flag the item for review and tell them so; the keys have acceptable alternative readings for a reason. If it does not hold, the gap between their case and the sentence is the lesson.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Reading the room without lowering the bar<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The standard is fixed: evidence on every answer, the ladder every time, the summary closed-book. What flexes is the load. A student who misses three of four items on a passage gets an easier passage next week, not an easier standard this week. A student who clears every item at rung 0 for two sessions gets a harder passage, timed. Adjust the text; never adjust what counts as done.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The teach-back<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>A tutor is certified by teaching one lesson back to the founder or a senior tutor, who plays the student and makes the three most common errors on purpose. The full scoring sheet has ten lines and lives in the tutor playbook. These six are the ones candidates miss most, and all of them must be true:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>The warm-up was timed and the errors were counted, aloud, at the end of the paragraph and not during it.<\/li>\n<li>Every item got the ladder, in order, with a wait at each rung.<\/li>\n<li>The tutor named the error with the program's name for it, after asking the student to name it first.<\/li>\n<li>The closed-book summary was checked word by word against the passage.<\/li>\n<li>No passage from the lesson library was described as a checkpoint, and no checkpoint passage was shown as a lesson.<\/li>\n<li>The tutor said less than the student. Count the turns.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>A teach-back that fails is re-run in a week on a different lesson. Two failures end the certification attempt for that cycle.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The Friday report, from the tutor's side<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The report is written by the log, not by you. Your job is the one sentence at the bottom: what the parent can ask at dinner this week. It names the passage, not the score. \"Ask what the museum passage could not show\" is a good sentence. \"Great progress this week\" is not; it tells the parent nothing to do.<\/p>\n<p>Read the report before the student does, and look for two things. A rising hint count with a steady score means the student is leaning on you; next session, hold rung 1 longer. A falling evidence rate with a rising score means recognition is doing the work; next session, a passage on a subject they know nothing about.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Two recordings a month<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Every session is recorded. Two a month are watched by the founder or a senior tutor against the playbook's ten-line checklist. The lines tutors miss most: the ladder held, the waits counted, the error named by the student first, no answer given by anyone, the Friday line said aloud at the close, and fewer tutor turns than student turns. The results go to the tutor, not to the parent, and they go into the after-action review at the end of the cohort. Nobody improves at a thing nobody watches.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Write the ladder for one item of your choice from chapter 23, all three rungs, in the words you would say, and the sentence you would say when you park it.<\/li>\n<li>Record yourself running a 20-minute routine with a willing adult. Count your turns and theirs.<\/li>\n<li>For each of the five moments above, write the sentence you would most want to say and then the sentence this chapter says instead.<\/li>\n<li>Take the Friday report of any student in the program, or an imagined one, and write the one dinner sentence.<\/li>\n<li>Name the rule in this chapter you expect to find hardest to keep, and the check that would catch you breaking it.<\/li>\n<\/ol>"},
{"title": "32. Reading on a screen", "part": "Part Eight: The digital SAT, closely", "html": "<p>Every test this book prepares you for is now taken on a screen. The moves do not change. What changes is where your finger goes, what replaces the pencil in the margin, and how the clock is felt. A student who reads well on paper and has never practiced on the screen gives back points in the first module that have nothing to do with reading. This chapter moves the method onto the screen, test by test, and the three chapters after it take on the half of the SAT's Reading and Writing section that is not reading passages at all: transitions, rhetorical synthesis, and the grammar of sentence boundaries. Together those make up close to half of the section's questions. The Marlow app has thirty practice items for those three chapters, with the choices covered until you have made the move.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The four formats<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Test<\/th>\n<th>Reading items<\/th>\n<th>Time<\/th>\n<th>Seconds per item<\/th>\n<th>What one passage carries<\/th>\n<th>Going back<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>SAT Reading and Writing<\/td>\n<td>54, in two modules of 27<\/td>\n<td>32 minutes a module<\/td>\n<td>about 71<\/td>\n<td>One short passage, or a pair, per question; rarely more than 150 words<\/td>\n<td>Anywhere within a module; never to an earlier module<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ACT Reading<\/td>\n<td>36<\/td>\n<td>40 minutes<\/td>\n<td>about 67<\/td>\n<td>A long passage, or a pair, with several questions<\/td>\n<td>Anywhere within the section<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GRE Verbal<\/td>\n<td>27, in sections of 12 and 15<\/td>\n<td>18 and 23 minutes<\/td>\n<td>about 90<\/td>\n<td>Short and long passages, plus sentence blanks<\/td>\n<td>Anywhere within a section<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GMAT Focus Verbal<\/td>\n<td>23<\/td>\n<td>45 minutes<\/td>\n<td>about 117<\/td>\n<td>Passages with several questions, and single arguments<\/td>\n<td>Bookmarks; after the last item, up to three changed answers<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>These are the formats as the test makers describe them at the time of writing. Formats change; check the official page in the month you test.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Stem first or passage first<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The SAT gives each question its own passage, so read the question first. It is short, it tells you which one thing to do (the question-families table in the appendix lists them), and on a passage of a hundred words, knowing what you are hunting for turns a read into a search. Then read the whole passage, every word, once. Do not hunt in a passage you have not read; the qualifier that decides the item is usually in the sentence you would have skipped.<\/p>\n<p>The ACT, the GRE's long passages, and the GMAT hang several questions on one passage, so read the passage first, with the one-line map from chapter 23, and only then the questions. Reading four stems first loads four goals into a head that needs one: what is this passage doing?<\/p>\n<p>The rule in one line: one passage, one question, stem first; one passage, many questions, passage first.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The tools, mapped onto the moves<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>On paper the moves have a physical form: finger on the sentence, circle the small word, the speaker written above the verb, a one-line map in the margin. On the screen each has a replacement.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>On paper<\/th>\n<th>On the screen<\/th>\n<th>How<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Finger on the sentence<\/td>\n<td>Highlight<\/td>\n<td>Highlight the one sentence the answer rests on, never more. Two sentences highlighted means you have not decided.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Circle the small word<\/td>\n<td>A note on the highlight, or scratch paper<\/td>\n<td>Write the qualifier alone: <em>most<\/em>, <em>only<\/em>, <em>not yet<\/em>.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The speaker above the verb<\/td>\n<td>Scratch paper<\/td>\n<td>One letter and a name: A for the author, R for a reported view (R: critics).<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The one-line map<\/td>\n<td>Scratch paper, one line per paragraph<\/td>\n<td>On the ACT, GRE, and GMAT. An SAT passage is too short to need one.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cross out the wrong choice<\/td>\n<td>The answer eliminator<\/td>\n<td>Strike each choice as you rule it out, and say its seam (chapter 11) as you do.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Star and return<\/td>\n<td>Mark for review<\/td>\n<td>Mark any item you answered below <em>fairly sure<\/em> (chapter 18).<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>The SAT's testing app has highlighting and notes, an answer eliminator, mark for review, and a countdown timer you can hide, and you are given scratch paper. The other tests have their own versions of the same tools. Practice on the real app at least four times before test day, so the tool costs no attention on the day.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Scratch paper is a calibration log<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The best use of scratch paper on a screen test is not notes. It is a column of question numbers with two marks beside each: the letter you chose and your confidence from 1 to 3. When you return to marked items, start with the 1s. After a practice module, that column is chapter 18's four-box sort, already done: score it, and every 3 that was wrong goes on the certain-and-wrong list.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Pacing by checkpoint<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Watching a clock count down is a tax on attention. Hide the timer and use one checkpoint instead. On an SAT module of 27 questions in 32 minutes, you want to be finishing question 14 with about 15 minutes left. Ahead of that, keep your pace. More than two minutes behind, apply the 90-second rule strictly from there on.<\/p>\n<p>The 90-second rule: if at 90 seconds on an SAT item you do not have an answer, choose the best remaining choice, mark it, write it in your column with a 1, and move on. An item you guessed and marked costs you nothing whether or not you return to it. An item you sat on for three minutes costs the two items at the end that you never read. Never leave a blank; there is no penalty for a wrong answer.<\/p>\n<p>Questions in an SAT module are grouped by type, and within a type they run roughly from easier to harder. So the last item of one type can be harder than the first item of the next. Do not read the order of difficulty as a signal of how you are doing.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The second module<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The SAT adapts by module. How you do on the first module decides whether your second module is harder or easier, and the highest scores are only reachable from the harder one. Two consequences follow. First, the first module matters more than it feels: careless points there change which test you take next. Second, a second module that feels hard is usually good news. Students who notice the difficulty and decide they are failing start to rush, and rushing is the one thing that is certain to cost points. Hard is the sign you earned the harder test. Read it the same way you read the first.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Reading on a screen is reading faster and checking less<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Studies that compare reading on screens with reading on paper find a small but consistent cost to comprehension on screens for informational text, larger under time pressure. The cost seems to come less from the screen than from what readers do on it: they move faster and check less, the way they read a feed. The method is the correction. The highlight forces a decision about which sentence matters. The eliminator forces a named seam. The stem-first read gives the eye a target. Three practical habits help as well: zoom until a line of the passage is comfortable at arm's length; keep the eye on one sentence at a time; on a long passage, scroll so the paragraph you are reading sits in the upper half of the screen, where the eye rests.<\/p>\n<p><strong>A worked example, SAT format<\/strong><\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>For decades, birders reported that the house finches visiting feeders in the eastern United States were redder than those in the West. Ornithologist Lena Hartwell suspected diet rather than ancestry, since the red comes from pigments in what the birds eat. She moved two groups of eastern finches into aviaries and fed one group the seeds common at eastern feeders and the other a western mix. After one molt, the two groups differed in redness, but by less than eastern and western finches differ in the wild. Hartwell concluded that diet explains some of the regional difference but ________<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?<\/p>\n<p>A. that eastern finches are redder than western finches. B. that ancestry or some other factor likely accounts for the rest. C. that western finches would become redder if fed eastern seed. D. that birders' reports of color are unreliable.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Walkthrough<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Stem first: <em>most logically completes<\/em>, so predict the ending from the logic words before looking. The logic word is <em>but<\/em>, after <em>some<\/em>. The sentence that matters is the result: the groups differed <em>by less than<\/em> wild finches do. Diet moved redness some of the way, not all of it. Prediction: \"but not all of it.\" B says exactly that, and at the right rung (<em>likely<\/em>). A is <em>true but off<\/em>: it restates the birders' report, which is not what Hartwell concluded. C is <em>outside the evidence<\/em>: Hartwell fed only eastern birds, so nothing was learned about western ones. D is <em>too strong and outside<\/em>: nothing in the passage questions the reports; the study explains them. B.<\/p>\n<p>The item took about seventy seconds: five on the stem, thirty-five on the passage, ten on the prediction, twenty on the choices. That is the budget.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The routine on a screen<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>SAT: the stem, then the whole passage once. Long-passage tests: the passage with a one-line map, then the stems.<\/li>\n<li>Highlight one sentence. Note the small word.<\/li>\n<li>Predict in plain words before you look at the choices.<\/li>\n<li>Eliminate with a seam named, never by feel.<\/li>\n<li>Write the number, the letter, and a confidence from 1 to 3. Mark anything below 3.<\/li>\n<li>At 90 seconds: answer, mark, move.<\/li>\n<li>When you return, take the 1s first.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Take one practice module in the real testing app with the timer hidden. Note the time left when you finish question 14 and when you finish question 27.<\/li>\n<li>On ten SAT-format items, record the seconds you spend on each with a phone timer. Which items went past 90 seconds, and which families from the appendix table were they?<\/li>\n<li>Keep the scratch-paper column for one full module. After scoring, sort it into chapter 18's four boxes.<\/li>\n<li>On ten single-passage items, read the passage first on five and the stem first on five. Compare the time and the accuracy.<\/li>\n<li>Write the one-line map of a three-paragraph passage on scratch paper in under a minute.<\/li>\n<\/ol>"},
{"title": "33. Transitions", "part": "Part Eight: The digital SAT, closely", "html": "<p>A transition question gives you two sentences with a blank at the start of the second and four words to fill it: <em>however<\/em>, <em>therefore<\/em>, <em>for example<\/em>, <em>in addition<\/em>. It looks like a vocabulary question. It is a reading question in its shortest form. The first two moves of this book ask which sentence matters and how far it goes. A transition asks a third question: how does this sentence stand to the one before it?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Cover the choices<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The one habit that wins these items: cover the choices, read the sentence before the blank and the sentence after it, each to the end, and name the relationship in one word before you look. The choices are written so that each one sounds acceptable in place. A reader who tries each word and listens for the one that sounds right will hear all four as right, because the ear forgives almost any connective. Only a named relationship rules three out.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The ten relationships<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Relationship<\/th>\n<th>The second sentence<\/th>\n<th>Words that signal it<\/th>\n<th>The test<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Contrast<\/td>\n<td>Points the other way from the first<\/td>\n<td><em>however; by contrast; on the other hand; yet<\/em><\/td>\n<td>Could <em>but<\/em> join them?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Concession<\/td>\n<td>Holds in spite of the first<\/td>\n<td><em>nevertheless; nonetheless; still; even so<\/em><\/td>\n<td>Would the first lead you to expect the second not to happen?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cause and effect<\/td>\n<td>Follows from the first<\/td>\n<td><em>therefore; thus; consequently; as a result; hence<\/em><\/td>\n<td>Could <em>so<\/em> join them?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Example<\/td>\n<td>Gives one case of the first<\/td>\n<td><em>for example; for instance<\/em><\/td>\n<td>Is the first general and the second one instance of it?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Specification<\/td>\n<td>Narrows the first to its exact detail<\/td>\n<td><em>specifically; in particular; namely; in fact<\/em><\/td>\n<td>Does the second say exactly which, how much, or how?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Addition<\/td>\n<td>Adds a separate point on the same side<\/td>\n<td><em>in addition; moreover; furthermore; also<\/em><\/td>\n<td>Is the second a new point about the same subject?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Similarity<\/td>\n<td>Says the same holds for something else<\/td>\n<td><em>similarly; likewise; in the same way<\/em><\/td>\n<td>Is there a second subject doing the same thing?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Restatement<\/td>\n<td>Says the first again, more plainly or more briefly<\/td>\n<td><em>in other words; that is; in short<\/em><\/td>\n<td>Could you delete either sentence and lose nothing?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sequence<\/td>\n<td>Comes next in time or order<\/td>\n<td><em>then; next; later; subsequently; finally<\/em><\/td>\n<td>Is there a clock?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Replacement<\/td>\n<td>Says what was done in place of what the first set aside<\/td>\n<td><em>instead; rather<\/em><\/td>\n<td>Did the first sentence decline, fail, or rule something out?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>The four confusions<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><em>However and nevertheless.<\/em> Both can mark a turn. When both fit, the test does not offer both; when one is offered against the other, the precise word wins. <em>Nevertheless<\/em> says the second fact holds in spite of the first: the forecast called for storms; nevertheless, the race went on. If the first sentence would lead you to expect the second not to happen, <em>nevertheless<\/em>. If the two simply point in different directions (the east branch grew; the west branch shrank), <em>however<\/em> or <em>by contrast<\/em>.<\/p>\n<p><em>Instead.<\/em> <em>Instead<\/em> needs something to replace. It is right only when the first sentence declines, fails, or rules out an action and the second says what was done in its place: the council did not repave the road; instead, it closed it. If nothing was set aside, <em>instead<\/em> is wrong, however sharply the sentences contrast.<\/p>\n<p><em>For example and specifically.<\/em> <em>For example<\/em> gives one case of a general statement, and other cases could have been given. <em>Specifically<\/em> narrows a statement to the exact detail it was pointing at, and there is only one. \"The study had a flaw. Specifically, it surveyed only volunteers.\" The volunteers are not one example of several flaws; they are the flaw.<\/p>\n<p><em>In addition and similarly.<\/em> <em>In addition<\/em> adds a new point about the same subject. <em>Similarly<\/em> says a different subject does the same thing. \"Bats eat mosquitoes. In addition, they pollinate agave.\" Same bats, new point. \"Bats pollinate agave. Similarly, some moths pollinate yucca.\" New subject, same act.<\/p>\n<p>One more trap, from chapter 4: <em>therefore<\/em> claims a cause. If the two sentences only report events in order (the library extended its hours; registrations rose), the passage has given you a sequence, and <em>then<\/em> or <em>later<\/em> is what it can support. A transition can overclaim exactly the way an answer choice can.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Three worked items<\/strong><\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>In its first year, the program placed forty new reading tutors in the district's schools. ______, the number of students reading below grade level rose slightly, a change the district attributes to an influx of students new to English.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>A. As a result B. For example C. Nevertheless D. Similarly<\/p>\n<p>Covered, the relationship is plain: forty tutors would lead you to expect fewer struggling readers, and the second sentence reports more. That is concession. C. A claims the tutors caused the rise, which the end of the second sentence rules out; the district names another cause. B needs a general first sentence. D needs a second subject.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The region's early bridges were built of timber, which rotted within a generation. ______, the engineers of the 1890s turned to iron, which promised to last a century.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>A. Consequently B. However C. Specifically D. In other words<\/p>\n<p>The trap is B: timber and iron look like a contrast. Ask why the engineers turned. Because timber rotted. The second sentence is a response to the first, not a reversal of it. Cause. A.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The survey found that most residents valued the park. ______, 71 percent named it the city's most important public space.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>A. Specifically B. Instead C. Moreover D. Even so<\/p>\n<p>The second sentence is the first one made exact: <em>most<\/em> becomes 71 percent. Specification. A. C would add a separate point, but there is no new point here, only the old one with a number on it.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When two still seem to fit<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Read the second sentence to its last word. Many transition items turn on a clause at the end, as in the first item above. Then read the sentence before the one shown, if the passage gives it; sometimes the relationship is to the paragraph, not the sentence next door.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The routine<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Cover the choices.<\/li>\n<li>Read both sentences to the end.<\/li>\n<li>Name the relationship in one word from the table.<\/li>\n<li>Uncover. Eliminate every choice from a different row.<\/li>\n<li>If two remain, apply the four confusions.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Name the relationship first, then supply the best transition.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>The bakery raised its prices in March. ______, its April sales were the highest in its history.<\/li>\n<li>Many desert plants store water in their stems. The saguaro, ______, can swell visibly after a heavy rain.<\/li>\n<li>The committee did not vote on the proposal. ______, it sent the proposal back for a cost estimate.<\/li>\n<li>Honeybees communicate the location of food by dancing. ______, many species of ant lay scent trails to food.<\/li>\n<li>The eastern route is shorter. ______, the western route has fewer tolls.<\/li>\n<li>The museum moved its collection to a new building in 1998. ______, it reopened the old building as a library.<\/li>\n<li>Most of the town's water comes from the reservoir. ______, about four of every five gallons used in town are drawn from it.<\/li>\n<\/ol>"},
{"title": "34. Rhetorical synthesis", "part": "Part Eight: The digital SAT, closely", "html": "<p>A block of items in every SAT module looks like this: a short list of notes a student has taken, a goal the student has, and four sentences built from the notes. Every choice is accurate. Every choice uses the notes. Only one does what the goal asks. These items are chapter 11's <em>true but off<\/em> trap with everything else stripped away, and they reward a single discipline: the goal decides, not the facts.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Read the goal first<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The goal sits in the stem: <em>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the two sculptures. The student wants to introduce the novel to an audience unfamiliar with it. The student wants to present the study and its findings.<\/em> Read it before anything else and mark two things: the verb (<em>emphasize<\/em>, <em>introduce<\/em>, <em>explain<\/em>, <em>contrast<\/em>, <em>present<\/em>, <em>generalize<\/em>) and the object (<em>a similarity<\/em>, <em>the conclusion<\/em>, <em>an advantage of the method<\/em>, <em>an audience unfamiliar with it<\/em>). Then say what a sentence would need to contain to do that. The table gives the common goals.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The goal says<\/th>\n<th>The right sentence must contain<\/th>\n<th>The usual wrong answer<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Emphasize a similarity or a difference<\/td>\n<td>Both things, the shared or differing feature, and a word like <em>both<\/em>, <em>like<\/em>, <em>unlike<\/em>, <em>whereas<\/em><\/td>\n<td>Describes one thing well, or both separately<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Introduce X to an audience unfamiliar with it<\/td>\n<td>An identification: who or what X is, in a phrase (<em>the 1903 essay collection by...<\/em>)<\/td>\n<td>Uses the bare name, as if the reader knew it<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Present a study and its findings<\/td>\n<td>What was done and what was found<\/td>\n<td>The finding alone, or the researcher's background<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explain an advantage, or a reason<\/td>\n<td><em>because<\/em>, <em>so<\/em>, <em>allows<\/em>, or <em>since<\/em><\/td>\n<td>States the feature without the benefit<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Emphasize the aim or purpose<\/td>\n<td><em>to<\/em>, <em>in order to<\/em>, <em>sought to<\/em><\/td>\n<td>Describes the result instead of the aim<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Make a generalization<\/td>\n<td>The category, not one member of it<\/td>\n<td>One example, however vivid<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Specify a date, place, or amount<\/td>\n<td>That fact<\/td>\n<td>Every other fact<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>The notes are not a passage<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The notes are a pile, not an argument. There is no author whose view you need to find and no qualifier hiding a reversal. You can often answer from the goal and the choices alone, because the item tests whether a sentence does a job, not whether its facts are right. Read the notes when two choices remain, or when a fact in a choice looks wrong to you.<\/p>\n<p>Watch length. The wrong answers are often the fullest sentences, packed with every fact in the pile, because a tired reader takes completeness for correctness. The goal never asks for all the facts.<\/p>\n<p><strong>A worked item<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Orla Brennan is an Irish textile artist.<\/li>\n<li>Her 2019 work <em>Tidewrack<\/em> is a tapestry about four meters long.<\/li>\n<li><em>Tidewrack<\/em> is woven from fishing net recovered from beaches in County Clare.<\/li>\n<li>Brennan's earlier tapestries were woven from wool dyed with lichens.<\/li>\n<li><em>Tidewrack<\/em> was first shown at a gallery in Galway.<\/li>\n<li>Brennan has said she wanted the work to \"carry the beach indoors.\"<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The student wants to emphasize how <em>Tidewrack<\/em> differs from Brennan's earlier work. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?<\/p>\n<p>A. Irish textile artist Orla Brennan's <em>Tidewrack<\/em> (2019), a tapestry about four meters long, was first shown at a gallery in Galway. B. Whereas Brennan's earlier tapestries were woven from lichen-dyed wool, her 2019 work <em>Tidewrack<\/em> is woven from fishing net recovered from beaches. C. Brennan wove <em>Tidewrack<\/em> from fishing net recovered from beaches in County Clare, hoping to \"carry the beach indoors.\" D. Brennan, an Irish textile artist, has worked with both wool and fishing net.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Walkthrough<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The verb is <em>emphasize<\/em>; the object is a <em>difference<\/em> between <em>Tidewrack<\/em> and the earlier work. The right sentence must name both, name the feature that differs, and mark the contrast. B does all three, with <em>whereas<\/em>. A introduces the work and never mentions the earlier tapestries. C is the trap: vivid, accurate, about <em>Tidewrack<\/em> alone, and the best sentence of the four if the goal were to describe the work. D names both materials but does not say which work used which, and <em>both<\/em> points at Brennan's range, which is a similarity, not a difference. B.<\/p>\n<p>Now change only the goal. <em>The student wants to introduce<\/em> Tidewrack <em>to an audience unfamiliar with Brennan.<\/em> The right answer is now A, which identifies the artist and the work in one sentence, and B becomes the wrong answer, because it assumes the reader knows who Brennan is. Same notes, same four sentences, a different right answer. That is the whole lesson of the item type: the facts never decide it.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The routine<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Read the goal. Mark its verb and its object.<\/li>\n<li>Say what the right sentence must contain, from the table.<\/li>\n<li>Read the choices. Eliminate any choice missing the must-have.<\/li>\n<li>Of what is left, pick the choice that does the goal and nothing that works against it.<\/li>\n<li>Go to the notes only to break a tie or check a fact.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Use these notes for every item.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>The American pika is a small mammal related to rabbits.<\/li>\n<li>Pikas live on rocky slopes in mountains of western North America.<\/li>\n<li>Pikas do not hibernate.<\/li>\n<li>In summer, pikas gather plants and store them in piles called haypiles.<\/li>\n<li>Pikas eat from their haypiles through the winter.<\/li>\n<li>Marmots, which live on some of the same slopes, hibernate through the winter.<\/li>\n<li>Pikas are sensitive to heat and are found mostly at high, cool elevations.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>For each goal, write the sentence that accomplishes it. Then write one accurate sentence from the same notes that a test would offer as a wrong answer, and name why it is wrong.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Explain the purpose of the haypiles.<\/li>\n<li>Introduce the pika to an audience unfamiliar with it.<\/li>\n<li>Emphasize a difference between pikas and marmots.<\/li>\n<li>Specify where pikas live.<\/li>\n<li>Make a generalization linking the pika's sensitivity to heat with where it is found.<\/li>\n<\/ol>"},
{"title": "35. Boundaries", "part": "Part Eight: The digital SAT, closely", "html": "<p>About a quarter of the SAT's Reading and Writing questions test the conventions of written English, and most of those turn on one question: where does one sentence end and the next begin? The test calls these items <em>boundaries<\/em>. The ACT's English section tests the same rules at greater length. The rules are few, they are learnable in a week, and they belong in a reading book for a reason that has nothing to do with the test. Punctuation is how a writer shows you the shape of a thought. A reader who sees where the independent clause is has found the claim, which is chapter 1's move, and a reader who sees which phrase sits between commas knows what can be lifted out without changing what the sentence says, which is chapter 2's.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The one test: can it stand alone?<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>An independent clause has a subject and a verb and can stand as a sentence. To test one, put a period after it and read it aloud. <em>The bridge reopened in May<\/em> stands. <em>Because the bridge reopened in May<\/em> does not; neither does <em>which reopened in May<\/em>. Almost every boundary item is decided by asking that question of the words on each side of the blank.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Joining two independent clauses<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>There are four legal joins.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Join<\/th>\n<th>Example<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A period<\/td>\n<td>The bridge reopened in May. Traffic returned within a week.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A semicolon<\/td>\n<td>The bridge reopened in May; traffic returned within a week.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A comma and one of <em>for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so<\/em><\/td>\n<td>The bridge reopened in May, and traffic returned within a week.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A colon, when the second clause delivers what the first promises<\/td>\n<td>The reopening had one effect no one predicted: traffic on the river road fell by half.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>And two illegal ones. A comma alone between two independent clauses is a <em>comma splice<\/em>: The bridge reopened in May, traffic returned within a week. No mark at all is a <em>fused sentence<\/em>: The bridge reopened in May traffic returned within a week. On a four-choice item, the splice is almost always one of the wrong answers.<\/p>\n<p>Words like <em>however<\/em>, <em>therefore<\/em>, <em>moreover<\/em>, and <em>consequently<\/em> are not joining words. They are adverbs, and they cannot hold two clauses together by themselves. <em>The bridge reopened in May; however, traffic was slow to return<\/em> is right. <em>The bridge reopened in May, however, traffic was slow to return<\/em> is a splice. The test for whether a word can join: move it. <em>Traffic, however, was slow to return<\/em> still works, so <em>however<\/em> is an adverb. <em>And<\/em> cannot move that way, so <em>and<\/em> is a conjunction.<\/p>\n<p>A colon needs an independent clause before it; what follows can be a clause, a list, or a single phrase. <em>The council approved three projects: a bridge, a library, and a park<\/em> is right. <em>The projects included: a bridge, a library, and a park<\/em> is not, because <em>the projects included<\/em> cannot stand alone.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Nonessential elements: matching pairs<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>A phrase that can be lifted out without changing the sentence's claim is set off on both sides by the same mark: two commas, two dashes, or two parentheses. Never one of each, and never only one when the phrase sits in the middle of the sentence.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The engineer, who had opposed the design, signed the report.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>Lift the phrase out: <em>The engineer signed the report.<\/em> The claim is unchanged; the phrase is extra information, so it takes a pair of commas.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The engineers who had opposed the design refused to sign.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>No commas. The phrase tells you <em>which<\/em> engineers. Lift it out and the claim changes: now every engineer refused. This is chapter 2 at the level of punctuation. The commas decide how far the claim goes.<\/p>\n<p>Names and titles follow the same rule. <em>The novelist Willa Cather<\/em> takes no commas, because there are many novelists and the name says which. <em>Cather's first novel, Alexander's Bridge, appeared in 1912<\/em> takes a pair, because she had only one first novel and the title is extra.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Where the sentence holds together, no mark at all<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>No comma between a subject and its verb, however long the subject: <em>The report that the council commissioned in the spring of last year found<\/em> no comma before <em>found<\/em>. No comma between a verb and its object, or between a verb and a <em>that<\/em> clause: <em>The study found that most residents walked<\/em>, with nothing after <em>found<\/em>. No comma between a preposition and its object. Most wrong answers on boundary items add a comma the sentence does not want; when in doubt, read it aloud without the mark.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Form, structure, and sense: the five that recur<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The rest of the conventions items test a handful of rules over and over.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Agreement across an interrupter.<\/em> Find the subject by covering every phrase that starts with a preposition. <em>The list of approved projects is long<\/em>: cover <em>of approved projects<\/em>, and the subject is <em>list<\/em>.<\/li>\n<li><em>Pronoun agreement.<\/em> A singular noun takes a singular pronoun: <em>The council released its report<\/em>, not <em>their<\/em>. Find the noun the pronoun points back to and check the number.<\/li>\n<li><em>Tense.<\/em> Match the time the passage has set. A paragraph in the past stays in the past unless something in it moves the clock.<\/li>\n<li><em>Modifiers.<\/em> An opening phrase describes whatever comes right after the comma. <em>Walking to the station, the rain began<\/em> says the rain was walking. The right choice puts the person who did the action right after the comma: <em>Walking to the station, Ruiz felt the rain begin.<\/em><\/li>\n<li><em>Possessives.<\/em> <em>Its<\/em> is possessive; <em>it's<\/em> is <em>it is<\/em>. <em>The researcher's notes<\/em> belong to one researcher; <em>the researchers' notes<\/em> to several. Test by turning it around: <em>the notes of the researchers<\/em>.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Four worked items<\/strong><\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The library's oldest map was drawn in 1790 ______ it shows a road that no longer exists.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>A. 1790, B. 1790; C. 1790 (no mark) D. 1790 and (no comma). Left: <em>The library's oldest map was drawn in 1790<\/em> stands. Right: <em>it shows a road that no longer exists<\/em> stands. Two independent clauses need a legal join. A is a splice, C is fused, D drops the comma before <em>and<\/em> that a join of two clauses needs. B.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The survey's leader, ecologist Imani Osei ______ counted every beetle herself.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>A. Osei, B. Osei; C. Osei: D. Osei (no mark). The comma before <em>ecologist<\/em> opens a nonessential phrase; it needs its partner. A. The others break the subject from its verb.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Trained as a chemist, ______<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>A. the museum hired Ruiz to restore its paintings. B. Ruiz was hired by the museum to restore its paintings. C. the paintings were restored by Ruiz. D. the museum's paintings needed Ruiz. Who was trained as a chemist? Ruiz. Ruiz must come right after the comma. B.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The results of the second survey, which reached twice as many households, ______ the first.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>A. confirms B. confirm C. has confirmed D. was confirming. Cover <em>of the second survey<\/em> and the phrase between commas: <em>The results ... confirm the first.<\/em> Plural subject, plural verb. B.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The routine<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Find the boundary the blank sits on.<\/li>\n<li>Read the words to its left: can they stand alone? Read the words to its right: can they?<\/li>\n<li>Both stand: a period, a semicolon, a comma with <em>and<\/em>, <em>but<\/em>, or <em>so<\/em>, or a colon if the second delivers what the first promised. One does not: a comma or nothing.<\/li>\n<li>A comma earlier in the sentence: look for its partner.<\/li>\n<li>Never a mark between subject and verb, or verb and object.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Fix each sentence, or say it is already right.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>The orchard lost half its trees in the storm, the owners replanted in the spring.<\/li>\n<li>The architect who designed the station, also designed the post office.<\/li>\n<li>The museum's new director, Ana Ferro will start in June.<\/li>\n<li>The town's oldest building which was a mill until 1920 is now a school.<\/li>\n<li>The council approved the budget; and the mayor signed it the same day.<\/li>\n<li>Each of the reports were reviewed by two editors.<\/li>\n<li>Having finished the survey, the results were sent to the board.<\/li>\n<li>The report found, that most residents favored the plan.<\/li>\n<\/ol>"},
{"title": "36. How to read a whole hard book", "part": "Part Nine: The long reads", "html": "<p>Chapters 15 and 16 made the case that knowledge and volume move a reading score further than anything else in this book, and more slowly. A passage is easy when you already know something about its subject, and the only way to know something about many subjects is to read whole books about them. The twelve-week plan asks for 150 minutes a week of a whole book at the edge of what you can read, and two long reads finished by the end. This chapter is how to do that without it turning into the thing every student dreads: a classic open on the desk, page 40, three weeks running.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Choosing the book<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The right book is at the edge: hard enough that you are learning from it, easy enough that you can follow it. The test takes one minute. Read the first full page aloud. Count the words you could not explain to someone else. Two or three is right. Five or more, and the book is too hard for now; set it aside for a later season, not forever. Zero, and it is too easy to count as the long read, though it may be a fine book.<\/p>\n<p>Nonfiction or fiction both count. The six books in chapter 37 were chosen because the tests draw on texts of their kind: founding-era argument, nineteenth-century prose, and the long American argument about freedom and education. All six are in the public domain and free to read.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The pace<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>One hundred fifty minutes a week is five sessions of 30 minutes, or three of 50. At the pace most readers manage on hard prose, that is a short book in about two weeks and a long novel in about five. The minutes are the commitment, not the pages. A week of 150 minutes on forty hard pages counts; a week of 30 minutes on a hundred easy ones does not.<\/p>\n<p>Log every session: the date, the minutes, the first and last page. The log is what the Friday report counts, and it is the honest answer to \"are you reading?\"<\/p>\n<p><strong>Before page one: ten minutes<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Read the table of contents. Read any introduction the author wrote, but not an editor's introduction, which will tell you what to think before you have read a line. Read the first page of the first chapter. Then write one line: what do I expect this book to be about, and what does the author want me to believe or feel? You will be wrong in interesting ways, and the line gives the reading a question to answer.<\/p>\n<p><strong>While reading: three marks and one rule<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><em>The one-line map.<\/em> At the end of every chapter, close the book and write one line: what the chapter did. Not what it was about; what it did. <em>Douglass learns to read, and learns why he was forbidden to.<\/em> This is chapter 23's map at book scale, and a book with a one-line map per chapter is a book you can explain.<\/p>\n<p><em>The knowledge ledger.<\/em> One page, started on page one: names, places, dates, and terms, one line each, in your own words. Chapter 15's ledger, built as you go. It is how the second book in a field gets easier than the first.<\/p>\n<p><em>One question per chapter.<\/em> Write one question the chapter raised that you cannot yet answer, or one claim you doubt. A reader with a question reads the next chapter looking for its answer.<\/p>\n<p><em>The circle-and-continue rule.<\/em> When you meet a word you do not know, circle it, make your best guess from the sentence, and keep going. Do not stop to look it up. Stopping breaks the sentence, and the sentence is usually what tells you the word. At the end of the session, look up only the circled words that blocked the meaning of a passage, or that you circled twice. Up to five a week go into the word bank with chapter 14's five entries. The rest you leave; you will meet the important ones again.<\/p>\n<p><strong>After each session: the two-minute close<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Close the book. Write two sentences: what happened or was argued in what you read today, and one thing you are unsure of. Then open the book and check the first sentence against the pages. This is chapter 17's closed-book retrieval, and it is the step that turns minutes into memory. A session without the close is reading; a session with it is training.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Reading older prose<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Nineteenth-century sentences are long, built from semicolons and qualifications, and they reward chapter 35's stand-alone test. When a sentence will not parse, find its main subject and verb, bracket everything else, and read the bracketed parts back in one at a time. If it still will not come, read it aloud, slowly, with the commas as pauses.<\/p>\n<p>Watch for words whose meanings have moved. They are the most dangerous words in an old book, because you think you know them.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word<\/th>\n<th>Then<\/th>\n<th>Now<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>want<\/em><\/td>\n<td>lack<\/td>\n<td>desire<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>nice<\/em><\/td>\n<td>precise, fine in distinction<\/td>\n<td>pleasant<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>awful<\/em><\/td>\n<td>inspiring awe<\/td>\n<td>very bad<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>presently<\/em><\/td>\n<td>soon<\/td>\n<td>now<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>candid<\/em><\/td>\n<td>generous in judging others<\/td>\n<td>frank<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>condescension<\/em><\/td>\n<td>graciousness toward those of lower rank, often praised<\/td>\n<td>talking down, always a fault<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>sensible<\/em><\/td>\n<td>aware of, feeling<\/td>\n<td>reasonable<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>interest<\/em><\/td>\n<td>advantage, a stake in something<\/td>\n<td>curiosity<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Chapter 28 showed what <em>want<\/em> does to a sentence read in its modern sense. Austen's <em>candid<\/em> and <em>condescension<\/em> do the same to whole scenes: a reader who takes them in today's sense misreads the narrator's judgment.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When it stalls<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Every long read stalls somewhere. Three fixes, in order. Lost: reread the last page only, then go on; rereading a whole chapter is the slowest fix there is. Bored: read the first page of the next chapter; books vary in pace, and the slow chapter is rarely the next one too. Stuck for three sessions running: skip to a chapter that looks interesting from the table of contents. This is not a test, and a skipped chapter does not cancel the minutes. If after three sessions the book is still a wall, stop, write one line about why, and choose the next one. Quitting a book well is a skill too.<\/p>\n<p><strong>For parents<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>One question a week at dinner, and only one: <em>What does the author want you to believe?<\/em> Then, if there is time: <em>Do you?<\/em> Chapter 30's rule holds; you are not checking the reading, you are giving your child a reason to say it aloud.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Choose a book. Read its first page aloud and count the words you could not explain. Write the number down.<\/li>\n<li>Log 150 minutes this week with dates and page numbers.<\/li>\n<li>Write the one-line map for every chapter you finish.<\/li>\n<li>Start the knowledge ledger with at least ten entries.<\/li>\n<li>Write the two-minute close after three sessions. A week later, reread the closes against the book. Did any sentence say more than the book did?<\/li>\n<\/ol>"},
{"title": "37. Six books, a reading guide", "part": "Part Nine: The long reads", "html": "<p>These six are the long reads I give most often. They are free, in the public domain, and short enough to finish in a season. They are the kind of text the tests quote: the SAT's historical documents come from founding-era and civil rights argument, its literature passages often from nineteenth-century fiction, and AP Language is built on argument of exactly this sort. And they talk to each other. Douglass and Washington were both born into slavery and drew different lessons from it; Du Bois answered Washington by name; Madison's argument about factions is the frame for every argument about who gets a voice. Read two that talk to each other and you have done chapter 10 at book length.<\/p>\n<p>For each book: why it is worth the minutes, what to read, what to watch for, and three questions to answer in writing, each with a quoted line. Every quotation below is from the original text. Spelling and punctuation vary a little between editions, so check each against yours.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Book<\/th>\n<th>Year<\/th>\n<th>Length at 150 minutes a week<\/th>\n<th>Trains chapters<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Frederick Douglass, <em>Narrative of the Life of Frederick Douglass, an American Slave<\/em><\/td>\n<td>1845<\/td>\n<td>about two weeks<\/td>\n<td>5, 6, 8<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Booker T. Washington, <em>Up from Slavery<\/em><\/td>\n<td>1901<\/td>\n<td>chapters 1 to 3 and 14: one week<\/td>\n<td>6, 24<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>W. E. B. Du Bois, <em>The Souls of Black Folk<\/em><\/td>\n<td>1903<\/td>\n<td>chapters 1 and 3: one week<\/td>\n<td>8, 10, 26<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>James Madison, <em>The Federalist<\/em> No. 10 and No. 51<\/td>\n<td>1787 and 1788<\/td>\n<td>one week, each read twice<\/td>\n<td>7, 23, 28<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Henry David Thoreau, <em>Walden<\/em><\/td>\n<td>1854<\/td>\n<td>three chapters: two weeks<\/td>\n<td>2, 6, 20<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Jane Austen, <em>Pride and Prejudice<\/em><\/td>\n<td>1813<\/td>\n<td>about five weeks<\/td>\n<td>5, 25, and every error in the book<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>A good order for the twelve-week plan: Douglass in the base phase, while the moves are new; Madison in the build phase, with chapter 7; Washington and Du Bois together, as a pair, in the build phase; Austen or Thoreau as the second long read.<\/p>\n<p><strong>1. Frederick Douglass, <em>Narrative of the Life of Frederick Douglass, an American Slave<\/em> (1845)<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><em>Why.<\/em> One of the most powerful arguments in American prose, made as a life story. Every chapter is evidence in a case, and Douglass knew his readers would doubt him, so he builds the case the way chapter 7 builds a claim: specific, named, dated where he could date it.<\/p>\n<p><em>What to read.<\/em> All of it.<\/p>\n<p><em>Watch for.<\/em> Reported views and the author's own (chapter 5). Douglass constantly reports what enslavers said and believed, and the reader has to track where his own judgment enters. The center of the book is chapter 6, where his enslaver, Hugh Auld, forbids his wife to keep teaching the boy to read, arguing that learning would ruin him as a slave. Douglass turns the objection into his evidence: \"From that moment, I understood the pathway from slavery to freedom.\" That is chapter 8's turn, an opponent's argument used against him, and it is more persuasive coming from Auld than it could be from Douglass. Watch also the appendix, where Douglass narrows a claim at the scale of the whole book: he explains that what he has attacked is the religion of slaveholders, not Christianity itself. A qualifier placed at the end, after the reader has felt the full force of the attack.<\/p>\n<p><em>Three questions.<\/em><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>In chapter 6, what does Auld's objection teach Douglass, and why is the lesson stronger for coming from Auld?<\/li>\n<li>Find one passage where Douglass reports what an enslaver believed. Where does Douglass's own view enter, and which word shows it?<\/li>\n<li>What does the appendix narrow, and why would Douglass put the narrowing at the end rather than the start?<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>2. Booker T. Washington, <em>Up from Slavery<\/em> (1901)<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><em>Why.<\/em> A strategic autobiography, written for an audience Washington needed something from. It is the best book I know for the question every test asks and few students ask on their own: who is this for, and what does that change?<\/p>\n<p><em>What to read.<\/em> Chapters 1 to 3, and chapter 14, which gives the Atlanta Exposition address of 1895.<\/p>\n<p><em>Watch for.<\/em> Tone, from the first lines: \"I was born a slave on a plantation in Franklin County, Virginia. I am not quite sure of the exact place or exact date of my birth, but at any rate I suspect I must have been born somewhere and at some time.\" That is understatement (chapter 6) about a grave absence, and the dry humor is doing work: it lets Washington report a cruelty without accusing anyone in the room. In the address, track what he concedes to his audience and what he asks of it (chapter 24's function question, sentence by sentence).<\/p>\n<p><em>Three questions.<\/em><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>What does \"somewhere and at some time\" tell you about the narrator's tone, and what does the tone let him say that a plainer sentence could not?<\/li>\n<li>In the Atlanta address, find the sentence that concedes the most to his audience and the sentence that asks the most of it.<\/li>\n<li>Who was Washington's audience? Name one thing the book does not say that a different audience would have expected it to.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>3. W. E. B. Du Bois, <em>The Souls of Black Folk<\/em> (1903)<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><em>Why.<\/em> It answers Washington directly, two years after <em>Up from Slavery<\/em>, and the two together are the best Text 1 and Text 2 pairing in American prose.<\/p>\n<p><em>What to read.<\/em> Chapter 1, \"Of Our Spiritual Strivings,\" and chapter 3, \"Of Mr. Booker T. Washington and Others.\"<\/p>\n<p><em>Watch for.<\/em> The first chapter turns on a question others never ask aloud: \"How does it feel to be a problem?\" It then names and defines a term, <em>double-consciousness<\/em>. Read the definition the way chapter 26 reads a technical term: exactly, in the author's sense, not the everyday sense the word might suggest. Chapter 3 opens with praise: \"Easily the most striking thing in the history of the American Negro since 1876 is the ascendancy of Mr. Booker T. Washington.\" Du Bois concedes a great deal before he disagrees, which is chapter 8's structure, and it is why his disagreement lands.<\/p>\n<p><em>Three questions.<\/em><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Write the definition of <em>double-consciousness<\/em> in one sentence of your own, then check it against the paragraph where Du Bois defines it.<\/li>\n<li>In chapter 3, list what Du Bois grants Washington before he disagrees. Why grant so much?<\/li>\n<li>Where would Washington and Du Bois agree? State it as a fact both would accept. Then name the point where they part, in each author's own words.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>4. James Madison, <em>The Federalist<\/em> No. 10 (1787) and No. 51 (1788)<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><em>Why.<\/em> The founding documents the SAT draws on most, and argument at its most compressed. Each essay is a few thousand words; each rewards a second reading more than any other text here.<\/p>\n<p><em>What to read.<\/em> Both essays, each twice: once for the map, once for the sentences. Older editions sign No. 51 \"Hamilton or Madison\"; historians credit Madison.<\/p>\n<p><em>Watch for.<\/em> Defined terms. Madison defines <em>faction<\/em> precisely, and his definition includes a majority as well as a minority; a reader who imports the modern sense misreads the whole essay. No. 10 is a chain: what a faction is, two ways to remove its causes, both rejected, then the hinge: \"The inference to which we are brought is, that the CAUSES of faction cannot be removed, and that relief is only to be sought in the means of controlling its EFFECTS.\" Find that sentence and the structure (chapter 23) falls out. No. 51 is shorter and turns on two famous lines: \"If men were angels, no government would be necessary,\" and \"Ambition must be made to counteract ambition.\" Ask of each what it assumes (chapter 7's warrant).<\/p>\n<p><em>Three questions.<\/em><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Write Madison's definition of <em>faction<\/em> in your own words. Does it include a majority? Quote the words that settle it.<\/li>\n<li>Map No. 10 in one line per step: the problem, the two cures for its causes, why both fail, the cure for its effects, and why a large republic supplies it.<\/li>\n<li>In No. 51, what does \"Ambition must be made to counteract ambition\" claim, and what does it assume about the people who will hold power?<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>5. Henry David Thoreau, <em>Walden<\/em> (1854)<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><em>Why.<\/em> Argument dressed as memoir, and the hardest of the six for tone: irony, exaggeration, and complete sincerity in the same paragraph. A student who can tell which is which in <em>Walden<\/em> can tell on any test.<\/p>\n<p><em>What to read.<\/em> \"Economy\" (the long first chapter; the first half is enough), \"Where I Lived, and What I Lived For,\" and \"Conclusion.\"<\/p>\n<p><em>Watch for.<\/em> Reach. \"The mass of men lead lives of quiet desperation.\" Ask chapter 2's question: <em>the mass<\/em>, not all; and does Thoreau count himself in it? In \"Economy,\" Thoreau lists what his house cost, item by item, down to the half cent. That is data with text (chapter 20), and the question is what the numbers can and cannot prove. Then the purpose statement: \"I went to the woods because I wished to live deliberately, to front only the essential facts of life.\" Hold the book to it.<\/p>\n<p><em>Three questions.<\/em><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>\"The mass of men lead lives of quiet desperation.\" What does the sentence claim, and what does it not claim?<\/li>\n<li>What is the list of building costs in \"Economy\" for? What does it prove, and what can it not prove?<\/li>\n<li>Find one sentence you think is ironic and one you think is sincere. What signal in each decided it for you?<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>6. Jane Austen, <em>Pride and Prejudice<\/em> (1813)<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><em>Why.<\/em> The novel of manners is a staple of test literature passages, and Austen is the hardest of its writers to read straight, because her narrator is ironic and her heroine is wrong for most of the book. It is also, almost exactly, a novel about the eight errors.<\/p>\n<p><em>What to read.<\/em> All of it. If time is short, chapters 1 to 6, 16 to 18, and 34 to 36.<\/p>\n<p><em>Watch for.<\/em> The first sentence: \"It is a truth universally acknowledged, that a single man in possession of a good fortune, must be in want of a wife.\" Whose truth? The next sentence tells you: the neighborhood's, which treats any such man as the rightful property of some family's daughter. And <em>want<\/em> means lack as well as desire (chapter 28): the sentence says he is missing a wife, which is the neighborhood's view of him, not his own. At the first ball, Darcy calls Elizabeth \"tolerable; but not handsome enough to tempt me,\" and she hears it; from then on, read her judgments of him as a reported view (chapter 5) from a reader with a stake. In chapter 16, Wickham tells her his story of Darcy and she believes it with no evidence but the telling. In chapter 35, Darcy's letter supplies the counterevidence, and in chapter 36 she does what she did not do before: she reads it twice, checks it against what she knows, and reverses. \"Till this moment I never knew myself.\" The novel is chapter 18 with a happy ending.<\/p>\n<p><em>Three questions.<\/em><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Rewrite the first sentence as two: what the neighborhood believes, and what the narrator thinks of the belief.<\/li>\n<li>In chapter 16, list each thing Wickham tells Elizabeth and what evidence she has for it. Which of the eight errors is she making?<\/li>\n<li>In chapter 36, what does Elizabeth do with Darcy's letter that she did not do with Wickham's story?<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Where to find them<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>All six are free from Project Gutenberg, and the Federalist papers are also on the Library of Congress's website. Any edition will do. The Long reads page of the Marlow app keeps the log, the one-line maps, and your answers to the three questions. Print the chapters if you can; a long read on paper is the one place in this book where I would choose paper over the screen.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The drill<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Choose one of the six and read it with chapter 36's method: the log, the one-line maps, the ledger, the circle-and-continue rule, the two-minute close.<\/li>\n<li>Answer its three questions in writing, each with a quoted line and its chapter or page.<\/li>\n<li>Read Washington's chapter 14 and Du Bois's chapter 3 in the same week. Run chapter 10's routine on them as Text 1 and Text 2.<\/li>\n<li>From whichever book you read, choose one paragraph and write the one-line map, the function of its last sentence, and the strongest claim it supports, with its rung.<\/li>\n<li>Explain the book aloud in two minutes to someone who has not read it. If you cannot, the ledger and the maps will show you where the gap is.<\/li>\n<\/ol>"},
{"title": "The method on one page", "part": "Appendix", "html": "<p>Everything the program measures and teaches, on one page, for the wall above the desk.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The three moves.<\/strong> Find the sentence that matters. Ask how far it goes. Write down why.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The question the moves answer.<\/strong> Does the sentence say the thing happened, that it probably happened, or that someone else thinks it happened?<\/p>\n<p><strong>The eight errors.<\/strong> Overlooks a qualifier. Infers beyond the evidence. Gives the author a view the author only reports. Uses outside knowledge. Right answer, wrong sentence. Quotation without a warrant. Ignores the counterevidence. Certain and wrong.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The five builds of a wrong answer.<\/strong> Too strong. Wrong speaker. True but off. Outside the passage. Reversed.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The ladders.<\/strong> suggests &lt; indicates &lt; shows &lt; demonstrates &lt; proves. some &lt; many &lt; most &lt; nearly all &lt; all. possible &lt; plausible &lt; probable &lt; likely &lt; certain. The right answer is usually the weakest rung that still fits.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The hint ladder.<\/strong> Location. The word. The question. After three, the item is parked; nobody gives the answer.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The 20-minute routine.<\/strong> Read the first paragraph aloud, timed. Read once. Two questions with sentence, reason, and confidence. One claim, one revision. Two sentences with the book closed. Vocabulary due.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The Friday numbers.<\/strong> Evidence rate. Certain-and-wrong. Oral reading speed and accuracy on a fresh text. Hints per task. Compared with last week, nobody else.<\/p>\n<p><strong>What moves the score, in order.<\/strong> The moves. Knowledge in coherent domains. Vocabulary by root and context. Writing about reading. Volume at the edge. Automaticity, if it is missing. Spacing and interleaving. Calibration. Test craft, last.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The season.<\/strong> Base, build, peak, taper. Nothing new in the last week. Sleep is a study method.<\/p>"},
{"title": "The eight errors", "part": "Appendix", "html": "<p>These are the names the diagnostic, the workspace coach, the tutor, and the Friday report all use. A student who knows them can name their own mistake, which is most of the way to not making it.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Error<\/th>\n<th>What it looks like<\/th>\n<th>Chapter<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Overlooks qualifying language<\/td>\n<td>\"Nearly\" read as \"completely\"; \"most\" as \"all\"; \"unless\" as \"because\"<\/td>\n<td>2<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inference goes beyond the evidence<\/td>\n<td>\"Caused\" with no comparison group; a moral the poem never states<\/td>\n<td>4, 7, 9<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Confuses the author's view with a reported view<\/td>\n<td>A merchant's warning becomes \"the passage argues\"<\/td>\n<td>5, 10<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Uses outside knowledge where the question asks about the passage<\/td>\n<td>The sleep that was never mentioned; what rivers usually symbolize<\/td>\n<td>4, 9<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Correct answer, evidence that does not support it<\/td>\n<td>The right choice with the wrong sentence highlighted<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Quotation relevant but does not establish the conclusion<\/td>\n<td>Evidence and claim with no warrant between them<\/td>\n<td>7<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ignores counterevidence<\/td>\n<td>A claim written as if the concession in the passage did not exist<\/td>\n<td>8<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High confidence on missed items<\/td>\n<td>\"Certain\" and wrong, because the distractor felt like the passage<\/td>\n<td>11<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Two more that the diagnostic tracks: <em>low confidence on correct, well-supported items<\/em> (the skill is there; the trust is not) and <em>explanations too thin to assess<\/em> (\"because it says so\"). Both are fixed by the same habit: write down why.<\/p>"},
{"title": "The 20-minute routine", "part": "Appendix", "html": "<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Minutes<\/th>\n<th>Do<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0 to 1<\/td>\n<td>Read the first paragraph aloud, timed. Count misread or skipped words<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1 to 7<\/td>\n<td>Read the passage once, no annotation, at the pace you read for meaning<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7 to 12<\/td>\n<td>Two questions. For each: answer, the sentence it depends on, one line of why, a confidence rating<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12 to 16<\/td>\n<td>One claim with evidence. Let the coach name one error. Revise once<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16 to 18<\/td>\n<td>Close the book. Two sentences: what it showed, what it could not show<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>18 to 20<\/td>\n<td>Vocabulary due today, retrieved before you look<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Three times a week between tutor sessions. Never longer; stamina is built by the long reads, not by the routine.<\/p>"},
{"title": "The calibration ladders", "part": "Appendix", "html": "<p>Weakest to strongest. The right answer is usually the weakest rung that still fits the passage.<\/p>\n<p><em>How sure is the writer?<\/em> suggests &lt; indicates &lt; shows &lt; demonstrates &lt; proves<\/p>\n<p><em>How many?<\/em> some &lt; many &lt; most &lt; nearly all &lt; all<\/p>\n<p><em>How likely?<\/em> possible &lt; plausible &lt; probable &lt; likely &lt; certain<\/p>"},
{"title": "Drill answer key", "part": "Appendix", "html": "<p><strong>Chapter 1.<\/strong> 1: sentence 4 (B, timing). 2: sentence 2 and 3 (B). 3: sentence 5 (B, the least-traveled towns). 4: sentence 5 (B; \"harder in the towns\" is the uncounted cost). 5: sentences 2 and 3.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Chapter 2.<\/strong> 1: <em>most<\/em> and <em>fewer than a third<\/em>; the support is among responders, who may not represent residents. 2: <em>nearly all<\/em> and <em>only ... about six hours<\/em>; the effect is broad and short. 3: <em>not yet<\/em> and <em>not the same<\/em>; no loss reported is not a profit. 4: <em>unless<\/em>; the bridge is open by default. 5: <em>for the most part<\/em> and <em>except<\/em>; well received by most, not by those who knew the original.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Chapter 3.<\/strong> 1: The data could not show success or failure. 2: Both the engineer's count and the merchants' estimate were accurate; neither addressed Saturdays, which is what the council asked about. 3: The river has flooded the lower road in the past; it has not since the culvert was widened; the report credits the culvert for the absence of flooding. 4: Most objections were old; the new ones were not serious. 5: The author accepts that trees cool streets by day; she rejects that daytime cooling is the whole story; officials quoted the daytime claim.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Chapter 4.<\/strong> 1: The lights, not the publicity, are the better explanation for the reports on those blocks (a comparison exists). 2: Breakfast and scores travel together; no cause (the groups may differ in many ways). 3: Registrations rose after two changes; cannot separate fees from hours. 4: Tree cover and cooler daytime temperatures travel together; wealth may explain both. 5: Wait times fell after two changes; cannot separate the receptionist from the walk-in policy.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Chapter 5.<\/strong> 1: Critics; the author distances (<em>alleged<\/em>). 2: The study owns the finding (neutral-strong); the superintendent owns the attribution (reported, not endorsed). 3: \"Often said\" is the public's view; the author distances and then contradicts it with data. 4: The engineer, against his own side; neutral report of a concession. 5: The board insists (reported); \"Perhaps\" and the holds list are the author, skeptical.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Chapter 6.<\/strong> 1: Ironic; the signal is the room that agreed on nothing. 2: Understated; the signal is closing two schools. 3: Straight; the signal is eleven years of action matching the words. 4: Ironic; three supporters, two of whom wrote it. 5: Ironic or at least distanced; he voted against what he called persuasive.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Chapter 7.<\/strong> 1: Claim, the policy brought families back; evidence, registrations near the branch up a third; warrant, the families who returned were the ones fines had kept away. Reach: near the branch, one spring. 2: Claim, practice volume drives gains; evidence, three-a-week students gained more; warrant, nothing else differed between the groups (which is not shown; motivated students may practice more). Reach: correlation only. 3: Claim, Market Street's foot traffic would rise; evidence, the neighboring town's did; warrant, the streets are alike (conceded false). Reach: weak. 4: Claim, trees cool streets by day; evidence, forty neighborhoods; warrant, nothing else explains both (wealth might). Reach: daytime, correlation. 5: Claim, no decline; evidence, higher tax payments; warrant, a declining guild would pay less (plausible, not certain). Reach: suggests, not proves.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Chapter 8.<\/strong> 1: Objection, childcare cost; conceded for one in six; kept for five in six; rebuttal narrows and then answers with the early room. 2: Objection, holds doubled; conceded as a real cost; rebuttal turns it (a list of readers was the point). 3: Objection, the streets differ; conceded; rebuttal narrows: the question is which way the differences cut. 4: Objection, self-report only; conceded; rebuttal turns it (self-report is what was meant to change). 5: Objection, sparse records; conceded; rebuttal denies the inference (everyone's records are sparse; the courthouse burned).<\/p>\n<p><strong>Chapter 9.<\/strong> 1: <em>Owed<\/em>; the river is a creditor. 2: Release (\"could not think of one thing it had promised\"); loss (\"or one thing, in the end, that I had meant\"). 3: That she is upset; he says aloud what her actions show. 4: Dry, understated; <em>polite<\/em> for a rejection; confirmed by <em>thin<\/em> and the spice drawer. 5: \"Nature teaches\" imports a teacher the poem lacks; \"material concerns\" imports a theme the ledger does not support; the poem is about the speaker's own accounting.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Chapter 10.<\/strong> 1: That merchants' concerns are reasonable, even if their numbers are not counts. 2: B would say the estimates do not need to be counts, because the customers who care about parking already left. 3: B, last sentence (circling the block is the risk). 4: A, what happened at the meeting and what each side's evidence could show; B, whether the town should charge. 5: Neither passage says merchants exaggerate; A says their estimates were not counts (not the same), B says some customers will leave (grants their point).<\/p>\n<p><strong>Chapter 11.<\/strong> Fort question: A, too strong and outside; B, too strong and wrong speaker; C, true but off. 1: too strong (\"proves,\" \"fabricated\"). 2: outside (political power is not in the passage). 3: too strong (\"caused,\" \"across the town\" versus \"nearest the branch\"). 4: wrong speaker (sentence 7 is the author's limit on the data). 5: outside (\"healthier\" appears nowhere).<\/p>\n<p><strong>Chapters 14 to 19.<\/strong> These drills produce work rather than answers, so they are scored against criteria. 14: each word has all five entries and a retrieval date; the three words you built from a root are real words not in the table (check a dictionary). 15: the summary on the familiar subject is longer, more specific, and closer to the text; after a week of reading on the unfamiliar subject, the gap has narrowed. 16: the checkpoint gains words per minute while accuracy holds at 97 percent or above; the third repeated reading of a paragraph is faster and cleaner than the first. 17: every place the summary says more than the passage is an overclaim; count them, and the count should fall from week to week. 18: the two middle boxes shrink over the month; certain-wrong sends you to chapter 11, unsure-right to the sentence-finding habit, since you had the evidence and did not trust it. 19: the page names days, minutes, and one Friday number; a week with no oral reading, or with more than about five hours, is not the standard.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Chapter 20.<\/strong> 1: \"After the North branch dropped late fees, its registrations rose about 37 percent while South's rose about 5 percent, which is consistent with the fee change contributing to the rise.\" Rung: <em>consistent with<\/em> or <em>suggests<\/em>; two branches allow no more. 2: <em>Too strong<\/em> in two ways: \"raised\" asserts a cause from a two-branch comparison, and 37 percent is North's whole rise, not its rise over South's, which is about 32 points. 3: Overdue rates unchanged, or changed alike, at both branches after the fee change; the table shows the opposite. 4: South's overdue rate in 2025: at 9, the \"cost the South branch did not pay\" disappears. South's 2025 registrations near 1,600 would do the same for the first half of the claim. 5: \"After the North branch dropped late fees, its card registrations rose about 37 percent while South's rose about 5 percent, and North's overdue items rose from 6 to 9 per 100 loans while South's stayed at 6.\"<\/p>\n<p><strong>Chapter 21.<\/strong> 1: Warrant: \"the east corridor resembles the north.\" Strengthen: the east corridor's delays have the same cause as the north's did. Weaken: the east corridor's delays come from something the bus cannot change. Assumption: nothing else changed on the north corridor when the bus opened. Any answer of yours that touches the warrant from one of those sides is right; one that praises the bus or the council is not. 2 to 4: self-scored. The author's own line is the one with no reporting verb in front of it. Your predictions after <em>although<\/em> and <em>despite<\/em> should run opposite to the first half. The places your phrasing broke aloud are the sentences to unpack with the chapter 2 routine.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Chapter 22.<\/strong> 1: The paragraph passes if sentence one is the claim, two is the evidence with a cited line, three is the warrant, and four is the limit; in either order of argument. 2 and 3: criteria, not answers; a commentary sentence that begins with what the quotation says instead of what it does is the error. 4: The paragraph names both readings of the river (the creditor the speaker owes, and the speaker's own accounting) and commits to one with a line. 5: The claim should sit at the rung the prompt sets, <em>never<\/em> against <em>serious cost to a few<\/em>; the body earns it only if it gives one case where the cost was justified and one where it was not, and says what separates them.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Chapter 23.<\/strong> 1: Sets up the practice and its case; states the hunch; gives the counts; admits the limits; lands narrower. Any verbs in the same families are right. 2: It concedes the case for labels before the passage tests them, so the experiment reads as a fair test of a reasonable practice, not an attack on a foolish one. 3: It shows the cost of removing labels was real and strongly felt, which prepares the reader for the cards going back up. 4: Paragraph 5: \"Adeyemi did not conclude that labels should go.\" Paragraph 4's limits also stand in the way. 5: It reports a test of a common practice, admits the test's limits, and draws a narrower conclusion.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Chapter 24.<\/strong> 1: To show the rule has consequences (it decides where stops go), so its inaccuracy matters. 2: It states the conclusion in a memorable form, from an insider, and draws the line between the average rider and the actual one. 3: Without it, the passage would seem to argue that the rule is simply wrong; the concession is what makes the conclusion about averages fair. 4: It names an alternative explanation for longer looking (the subject matter, not the missing cards), which is why the paragraph is about limits. 5: <em>Complicate<\/em>: the survey confirms the rule for the average and challenges it for individuals; <em>refute<\/em> is too strong and <em>defend<\/em> is the wrong direction.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Chapter 25.<\/strong> 1: Third person limited to Teodor; the first sentence reports his decision from inside (\"had decided, on the train\"), and the sister's thoughts never appear. 2: \"and anyway it was not so much\" (he is talking himself into silence). \"Always been careless with money\" is also his judgment. 3: Both avoid a subject: he will not mention the money; she talks about the children so as not to be asked anything else. The first and last sentences show it. 4: Something like \"She was wearing a new coat, which he noticed.\" The passage loses Teodor's resentment and the reader's access to it. 5: That the uncle's war stories, at least, were not true; \"including\" signals the older narrator's distance.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Chapter 26.<\/strong> 1: Sentence 1, Q (the gardeners' claim); sentence 2, Q and H (touch or air); sentence 3, M; sentence 4, R; sentence 5, I; sentence 6, L. 2: To separate moving air from touch; without it, a shorter brushed seedling could be the work of either, and she could not say <em>touch, not air<\/em>. 3: <em>Too strong<\/em> (\"proved\") and <em>outside the result<\/em> (fruit was not measured; she said so). 4: Several light levels and more than one greenhouse, the same four groups, and fruit counted at harvest. 5: \"In one greenhouse at one light level, daily brushing, not moving air, accounted for most of the difference in seedling height and stem thickness.\" Rung: <em>shows<\/em>, for these conditions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Chapter 27.<\/strong> 1: That bus use rose, from 41 to 64 percent riding weekly; it is behavior, with a before figure. 2: About 15 percent responded, and students who use and like the pass are likelier to answer a survey about it. 3: It gives a baseline of normal variation, so the reader can see that a 3-point rise may be no effect at all. 4: \"The pass has clearly increased bus use; its effect on attendance is too small yet to separate from normal variation.\" 5: About 340 students, under 6 percent of enrollment. It is also a self-reported counterfactual: students are guessing what they would have done without the pass.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Chapter 28.<\/strong> 1: \"This situation is an unaffordable contradiction.\" Fifteen: \"A nation that gives every man a vote but not an education contradicts itself.\" 2: Lack. Read as <em>desire<\/em>, the sentence would say people pay for wanting the schools they refuse to fund, which is nonsense. 3: That the cost is great: \"It will be said that the cost is great; and it is great.\" 4: Everyone pays either way; the only choice is whether to pay for schools or for courts, prisons, and wasted talent. 5: Taxpayers or legislators reluctant to fund schools: \"any man who urges the expense,\" \"the very schools he declines to support.\"<\/p>\n<p><strong>Chapter 29.<\/strong> 1: All winter people walked and sledded on the frozen lake, over the spot where the boathouse floats in summer, without thinking about what the ice was holding up. 2: Line 7, \"This morning, though\"; the word is <em>though<\/em>, and the shift is from a general past to one present moment. 3: Ending the line on \"I\" isolates the speaker at the moment the poem turns to the speaker's own feeling, and holds the sentence in suspense before \"was sorry.\" 4: That it melts gradually and everywhere, with no single moment or place where it happens. 5: <em>Too broad<\/em> and <em>outside<\/em>: \"exploit\" is not in the poem, and the poem is about one lake and one speaker. Better: \"The speaker feels brief guilt for the weight the lake carried all winter (lines 10 to 11) and then lets it go, because a lake, unlike a person, does not keep accounts (lines 12 to 13).\"<\/p>\n<p><strong>Chapter 31.<\/strong> Self-scored against the teach-back list. 1: three rungs, each giving a little more than the last and none giving the answer; after the third, the item is parked. 2: your turns should be fewer than theirs; if not, the ladder was skipped somewhere. 3 to 5: criteria; the check in item 5 should be something a recording would show.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Chapter 32.<\/strong> Self-scored. 1: finishing question 14 with about 15 minutes left is on pace; more than two minutes behind means the 90-second rule was not applied. 2: items past 90 seconds cluster in one or two families; those families are next week's chapters. 3: every 3 that was wrong goes on the certain-and-wrong list. 4: on single-passage items, stem first should be as accurate and faster; if it is less accurate, you are hunting before reading the whole passage. 5: one line per paragraph, each a verb (sets up, reports, concedes, concludes).<\/p>\n<p><strong>Chapter 33.<\/strong> 1: concession; <em>nevertheless<\/em> or <em>even so<\/em> (<em>however<\/em> works as a plain turn; <em>therefore<\/em> is wrong, since nothing credits the price rise). 2: example; <em>for example<\/em> or <em>for instance<\/em>: the saguaro is one desert plant. 3: replacement; <em>instead<\/em>: the committee set aside a vote. 4: similarity; <em>similarly<\/em> or <em>likewise<\/em>: a new subject, ants, doing the same thing; not <em>in addition<\/em>, because the subject changed. 5: contrast; <em>however<\/em> or <em>by contrast<\/em>; not <em>nevertheless<\/em>, because nothing in the first sentence would lead you to expect the western route to have more tolls. 6: sequence; <em>later<\/em> or <em>subsequently<\/em>. 7: specification; <em>specifically<\/em> or <em>in fact<\/em>: <em>most<\/em> made exact.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Chapter 34.<\/strong> Criteria, with a sample right answer and a sample wrong one. 1: must contain the reason: \"Because pikas do not hibernate, they store summer plants in haypiles to eat through the winter.\" Wrong: \"In summer, pikas gather plants into haypiles\" (the feature without the purpose). 2: must identify the animal: \"The American pika, a small mammal related to rabbits, lives on rocky mountain slopes in western North America.\" Wrong: \"Pikas do not hibernate\" (the bare name, as if the reader knew it). 3: must name both and the differing feature: \"Unlike the marmots that share their slopes, pikas do not hibernate; they live through the winter on stored plants.\" Wrong: \"Marmots hibernate through the winter\" (one animal only). 4: must contain the place: \"Pikas live on rocky slopes in the mountains of western North America.\" Wrong: any sentence about haypiles. 5: must speak of the species and link the two facts: \"Because pikas are sensitive to heat, they are found mostly at high, cool elevations.\" Wrong: \"Pikas are sensitive to heat\" (one fact, no link).<\/p>\n<p><strong>Chapter 35.<\/strong> 1: a comma splice; use a period, a semicolon, or \", and the owners.\" 2: no comma between subject and verb: \"The architect who designed the station also designed the post office.\" 3: the pair is missing its partner: \"The museum's new director, Ana Ferro, will start in June.\" 4: a nonessential clause needs a pair: \"The town's oldest building, which was a mill until 1920, is now a school.\" 5: a semicolon and <em>and<\/em> do not combine: use \", and the mayor\" or \"; the mayor.\" 6: <em>each<\/em> is singular: \"Each of the reports was reviewed.\" 7: a dangling modifier; the results did not finish the survey: \"Having finished the survey, the team sent the results to the board.\" 8: no comma between a verb and its <em>that<\/em> clause: \"The report found that most residents favored the plan.\"<\/p>\n<p><strong>Chapter 36.<\/strong> Self-scored against the method. 1: two or three unknown words on the first page is the edge; five or more, choose another book. 2: the log shows dates, minutes, and pages, totaling 150 or more. 3: each map line is a verb about what the chapter did, not a topic. 4: ten ledger entries, each one line, in your own words. 5: a close that says more than the book is an overclaim (chapter 17); count them, and the count should fall.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Chapter 37.<\/strong> Criteria. Every answer quotes a line and names its chapter or page. Douglass 1: Auld's objection reveals that literacy is what slavery depends on denying; it is stronger from Auld because it is an admission against interest. Madison 1: yes; the definition says a faction may amount to \"a majority or a minority of the whole.\" Austen 2: Elizabeth takes a reported view as fact with no evidence and ignores what does not fit (errors 3 and 7 in the appendix list), and her certainty is the eighth error. Drill 3 passes if the shared ground is stated as a fact both men would accept and each side of the disagreement is in its author's own words.<\/p>"},
{"title": "A note on the passages", "part": "Appendix", "html": "<p>Every passage, poem, and example sentence in this book was written for it. None is taken from a test, a textbook, or another program. Where the program uses a public-domain work in a lesson, the author, title, and year are named. The passages here reappear in the workspace lessons; the checkpoint passages used to measure progress never appear anywhere a student has seen before.<\/p>"},
{"title": "A 12-week self-study plan", "part": "Appendix", "html": "<p>For a reader using the book without the program. Three 20-minute sessions a week on the routine, one longer session on the chapter, 150 minutes of a whole book, one oral reading checkpoint. The chapters are ordered by phase, not by number.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Week<\/th>\n<th>Phase<\/th>\n<th>Read<\/th>\n<th>Drill<\/th>\n<th>Checkpoint<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>Base<\/td>\n<td>Introduction, 1, 16<\/td>\n<td>Chapter 1 drill; first read-aloud, timed, errors counted<\/td>\n<td>Baseline: one unfamiliar passage, four items, confidence rated<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2<\/td>\n<td>Base<\/td>\n<td>2, 14<\/td>\n<td>Chapter 2 drill; unpacking set, sentences 1 to 10; start the root table<\/td>\n<td>Read-aloud<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3<\/td>\n<td>Base<\/td>\n<td>3, 13<\/td>\n<td>Chapter 3 drill; unpacking set, sentences 11 to 20; the knowledge ledger begun<\/td>\n<td>Read-aloud; unfamiliar passage<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4<\/td>\n<td>Base<\/td>\n<td>15, 17<\/td>\n<td>Closed-book summaries on every passage from now on; first long read half done<\/td>\n<td>Read-aloud; unfamiliar passage; practice Set A, untimed<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5<\/td>\n<td>Build<\/td>\n<td>4, 7<\/td>\n<td>Chapters 4 and 7 drills; claim-evidence-warrant on three passages<\/td>\n<td>Read-aloud; unfamiliar passage<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6<\/td>\n<td>Build<\/td>\n<td>5, 6<\/td>\n<td>Chapters 5 and 6 drills; map the voices in every passage<\/td>\n<td>Read-aloud; unfamiliar passage; certain-and-wrong audit begins<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7<\/td>\n<td>Build<\/td>\n<td>8, 18<\/td>\n<td>Chapter 8 drill; the four-box calibration sort<\/td>\n<td>Read-aloud; practice Set B, timed<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8<\/td>\n<td>Build<\/td>\n<td>9, 10<\/td>\n<td>Chapters 9 and 10 drills; long read finished; second long read begun<\/td>\n<td>Read-aloud; unfamiliar passage<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9<\/td>\n<td>Peak<\/td>\n<td>11, 20<\/td>\n<td>Seams on every wrong answer of the last section; data item routine<\/td>\n<td>Two timed sections, hints off<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10<\/td>\n<td>Peak<\/td>\n<td>12, 21 or 22 (by test)<\/td>\n<td>The week-before plan rehearsed; the explain-aloud on three passages<\/td>\n<td>Two timed sections<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11<\/td>\n<td>Peak<\/td>\n<td>19<\/td>\n<td>The training week audited; unpacking set, sentences 21 to 30, cold<\/td>\n<td>Two timed sections; certain-and-wrong list final<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12<\/td>\n<td>Taper<\/td>\n<td>Appendix only<\/td>\n<td>Vocabulary due; flip cards; the two weakest chapters' drills, closed<\/td>\n<td>One short section, then stop<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>If the baseline read-aloud is under 90 percent accuracy, stop and see a specialist; the plan above assumes decoding is automatic. If it is accurate but under 150 words a minute, add chapter 16's repeated reading to every session in weeks 1 to 6. Chapters 23 to 29 fit the build and peak phases: read 23 and 24 in week 6, 25 in week 8, and the domain chapter that matches your weakest passage type (26 to 29) in weeks 9 and 10. A parent working alongside should read chapter 30 in week 1. SAT students should add chapters 32 and 33 in week 9 and chapters 34 and 35 in week 10, and take every timed section from week 9 on in the real testing app. The two long reads come from chapter 37; read chapter 36 in week 1, before the first one begins.<\/p>"},
{"title": "Two practice sections", "part": "Appendix", "html": "<p>Two sections of two passages and ten items each, written for this book. Take Set A untimed in week 4 and Set B timed (13 minutes) in week 7, so that the timed run is on text you have never seen; a passage read once stops measuring. For every item, write the answer, the sentence it depends on, and a confidence rating before you check the key. Each key names the build of every wrong choice, using the seams from chapter 11: <em>too strong, outside, wrong speaker, off, partial, inverted, near miss.<\/em><\/p>\n<p><strong>Set A<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Passage A<\/strong><\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>[1]<\/strong> When the city of Halden installed countdown timers at 60 of its busiest crosswalks in 2021, the goal was simple: tell pedestrians how many seconds they had left, and fewer would be caught in the street when the light changed. By that measure the timers worked. Video from a sample of the crossings showed that the share of pedestrians still in the crosswalk when the signal turned was about a third lower than before.<\/p>\n<p><strong>[2]<\/strong> Drivers, it turned out, could see the timers too. At several crossings, the city's traffic engineer, Rosa Lindqvist, noticed cars speeding up as the numbers fell toward zero, racing to get through before the light changed. Collisions at the 60 crossings did not fall in the following two years; at the busiest ten, rear-end crashes rose slightly, although the numbers were small enough that Lindqvist warned against reading much into them.<\/p>\n<p><strong>[3]<\/strong> The city's response was not to remove the timers. Pedestrians liked them, and the drop in people stranded in the street was real. Instead, Lindqvist proposed testing, at a few crossings first, timers angled so that only pedestrians can read them. \"We designed the timer for one audience,\" she wrote in her report, \"and forgot that a number on a pole is read by everyone who can see it.\"<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<ol>\n<li>The main purpose of the passage is to<br>A. argue that countdown timers should be removed from city streets.<br>B. describe a safety measure that worked for its intended audience, had an unintended effect on another, and led to a narrower response.<br>C. prove that countdown timers cause rear-end collisions.<br>D. compare Halden's results with those of other cities.<\/li>\n<li>In paragraph 1, the phrase \"By that measure\" mainly serves to<br>A. signal that the author considers this the only measure that matters.<br>B. limit the claim of success to the stated goal, leaving room for other outcomes.<br>C. suggest that the video measurements were unreliable.<br>D. emphasize how precisely the timers were measured.<\/li>\n<li>Which choice provides the best evidence that the timers changed how drivers behaved?<br>A. \"the share of pedestrians still in the crosswalk when the signal turned was about a third lower than before\"<br>B. \"noticed cars speeding up as the numbers fell toward zero\"<br>C. \"Pedestrians liked them\"<br>D. \"at the busiest ten, rear-end crashes rose slightly\"<\/li>\n<li>Lindqvist's warning \"against reading much into them\" functions mainly to<br>A. dismiss the drivers' behavior as unimportant.<br>B. qualify the crash figures as too few to support a strong conclusion.<br>C. suggest that the video evidence was inaccurate.<br>D. defend the original design of the timers.<\/li>\n<li>Lindqvist's final sentence most strongly implies that<br>A. the timers should be visible only to drivers.<br>B. information placed in public view may change the behavior of people it was not meant for.<br>C. pedestrians misread the timers.<br>D. traffic engineers rarely consider pedestrians.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Passage B<\/strong><\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>[1]<\/strong> My grandmother taught piano for forty years in the front room of a house too small for it. The piano took the whole wall, and the students, who came every afternoon from three to six, had to edge past the sofa to reach the bench. She did not praise them. When a child finished a piece she would say \"Again,\" or, very rarely, \"Good. Now again,\" and the first word was the one they waited for.<\/p>\n<p><strong>[2]<\/strong> I was not one of her students. She said, when my mother asked, that she did not teach family, and that was the end of it. So I learned what I learned from the kitchen, where I did my homework with the door open, and from the stairs, where I sat when I was supposed to be asleep and she played for no one. She played differently then, slower, with long silences between the phrases, as if she were waiting for something to answer.<\/p>\n<p><strong>[3]<\/strong> I asked her once what she was listening for. She looked at me as though I had said something rude, and then, after a moment, as though I had said something else. \"For the rest of it,\" she said, and closed the lid.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<ol>\n<li>The passage mainly focuses on<br>A. the grandmother's harshness toward her students.<br>B. what the narrator came to know about the grandmother indirectly, from outside her teaching.<br>C. the narrator's regret at never learning to play the piano.<br>D. the history of the grandmother's house.<\/li>\n<li>The phrase \"the first word was the one they waited for\" suggests that<br>A. the students preferred to repeat their pieces.<br>B. the grandmother's rare praise mattered to her students far more than its brevity would suggest.<br>C. the students did not understand her instructions.<br>D. the grandmother was inconsistent in her teaching.<\/li>\n<li>The narrator says the grandmother's refusal to teach family was \"the end of it\" mainly to show<br>A. the grandmother's firmness.<br>B. the mother's anger at the refusal.<br>C. that the narrator never heard her grandmother play.<br>D. that the grandmother disliked the narrator.<\/li>\n<li>Which choice best supports the claim that the grandmother's private playing was a kind of listening?<br>A. \"The piano took the whole wall\"<br>B. \"She did not praise them\"<br>C. \"with long silences between the phrases, as if she were waiting for something to answer\"<br>D. \"and closed the lid\"<\/li>\n<li>The description of the grandmother's two expressions in paragraph 3 most strongly suggests that she<br>A. was offended and refused to answer.<br>B. first took the question as an intrusion and then as something more serious, and answered it.<br>C. did not hear the question.<br>D. was angry with the narrator's mother.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Key, with the build of every wrong choice<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item<\/th>\n<th>Answer<\/th>\n<th>Wrong choices and their builds<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td>A <em>inverted<\/em> (the city kept the timers); C <em>too strong<\/em> (\"prove\"; Lindqvist calls the crash numbers small); D <em>outside<\/em> (no other cities)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2<\/td>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td>A <em>too strong<\/em> (the passage goes on to other measures); C <em>outside<\/em> (nothing questions the video); D <em>off<\/em> (precision is never discussed)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3<\/td>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td>A <em>wrong part<\/em> (pedestrian behavior, not driver); C <em>wrong part<\/em>; D <em>near miss<\/em>: an outcome that may have many causes, which Lindqvist warns against reading into; B is direct observation of the behavior<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4<\/td>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td>A <em>inverted<\/em> (the passage treats driver behavior as the problem); C <em>off<\/em> (the warning is about crash counts, not video); D <em>off<\/em> (she proposes a new design)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5<\/td>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td>A <em>inverted<\/em> (the new timers would be visible only to pedestrians); C <em>outside<\/em>; D <em>too strong<\/em> and <em>inverted<\/em> (the timer was designed for pedestrians)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6<\/td>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td>A <em>partial<\/em> (paragraph 1 only); C <em>outside<\/em> (no regret is stated); D <em>off<\/em> (the house is setting)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7<\/td>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td>A <em>inverted<\/em>; C <em>outside<\/em>; D <em>outside<\/em> (her responses are consistent)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8<\/td>\n<td>A<\/td>\n<td>B <em>outside<\/em> (the mother's reaction is never given); C <em>inverted<\/em> (paragraph 2 says she heard her play); D <em>too strong<\/em> (a rule about family is not dislike)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9<\/td>\n<td>C<\/td>\n<td>A <em>off<\/em> (setting); B <em>off<\/em> (teaching, not private playing); D <em>near miss<\/em> (an ending, not listening)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10<\/td>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td>A <em>partial<\/em> (the first expression only, and she does answer); C <em>inverted<\/em> (she reacts to the question); D <em>outside<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Score it twice: once for answers, once for evidence. A right answer with the wrong sentence is a miss for the evidence score, and the evidence score is the one that predicts the next test.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Set B<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Passage C<\/strong><\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>[1]<\/strong> For decades, the common swift has nested in the gaps under old roof tiles, and as towns replace those roofs, the gaps disappear. Ornithologist Petra Vlasak wanted to know whether purpose-built nest boxes could replace them, and whether swifts would use boxes at all, since the birds are known to return to the same site for years.<\/p>\n<p><strong>[2]<\/strong> In 2022 she arranged for 300 boxes to be fixed under the eaves of public buildings in Ostrov, a town of 20,000, and for none to be installed in Kadan, a town of similar size and housing stock 15 kilometers away. Volunteers in both towns counted screaming parties, the low fast groups of swifts that signal breeding birds nearby, on the same ten evenings each summer.<\/p>\n<p><strong>[3]<\/strong> Over three summers the Ostrov counts rose by 41 percent and the Kadan counts by 6 percent; by the third year, 112 of the 300 boxes showed signs of use. Vlasak concluded that boxes can hold a town's swift population where roofs no longer can. She noted that she could not tell whether Ostrov's new birds had been hatched there or had moved in from villages nearby, and that three summers is short for a bird that can live twenty years.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<ol>\n<li>Vlasak included Kadan in the study primarily to<br>A. test whether swifts prefer old roofs to boxes.<br>B. provide a comparison showing what happened to swift numbers where no boxes were installed.<br>C. show that boxes do not work in smaller towns.<br>D. measure how far swifts travel between towns.<\/li>\n<li>Which choice best describes the relationship between the counts and Vlasak's conclusion?<br>A. The counts prove that boxes are the only way to keep swifts in towns.<br>B. The counts support the conclusion for one town, and the conclusion is stated more generally than that.<br>C. The counts contradict the conclusion, since fewer than half the boxes were used.<br>D. The counts concern a different species from the one in the conclusion.<\/li>\n<li>The passage explains what \"screaming parties\" are mainly so that the reader<br>A. appreciates how loud swifts can be.<br>B. understands what the volunteers counted and why it indicates breeding birds.<br>C. doubts the reliability of volunteer counts.<br>D. learns the species' scientific name.<\/li>\n<li>Which finding, if true, would most weaken Vlasak's conclusion?<br>A. Swifts in other countries also use nest boxes.<br>B. Kadan's volunteers counted on evenings that were, on average, colder and wetter than Ostrov's, and swifts fly low less often in such weather.<br>C. Some of the 300 boxes were installed facing south.<br>D. In the first summer, the Ostrov counts were lower than Kadan's.<\/li>\n<li>The remark that three summers \"is short for a bird that can live twenty years\" primarily serves to<br>A. suggest that the boxes will last twenty years.<br>B. qualify the length of the study relative to the bird's lifespan.<br>C. argue that older swifts prefer roofs.<br>D. explain why swifts return to the same site.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Passage D: two texts<\/strong><\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Text 1<\/strong>, from a resident's letter to the town council. <strong>[1]<\/strong> The Lyric Theater has stood on Water Street since 1926, and for most of that time it has been the only building on the block anyone could name. The council's plan to sell it to a developer who will keep the facade and gut the rest is not preservation; it is taxidermy. A building is its rooms, its stair, the way sound moves in it, not the face it shows the street. If the town cannot afford to run a theater, it should say so and let the Lyric close with honesty, rather than keep a wall standing as an alibi.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Text 2<\/strong>, from the planning officer's report. <strong>[2]<\/strong> The Lyric's auditorium has been closed to the public since 2019, when the balcony was found to be unsafe, and the cost of restoring it exceeds the town's entire annual capital budget. The proposal keeps the facade, the lobby, and the marquee, and requires the developer to open the lobby as a public room. Critics call this a hollow gesture. It is a choice between a facade with a living building behind it and, within a decade, no building at all.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<ol>\n<li>Both authors would agree that<br>A. the facade should be demolished.<br>B. the council's plan keeps the theater's exterior and not its auditorium.<br>C. the town can afford to restore the theater.<br>D. the Lyric should close.<\/li>\n<li>The author of Text 2 would most likely respond to Text 1's claim that the plan \"is taxidermy\" by<br>A. denying that the auditorium would be gutted.<br>B. acknowledging the objection and arguing that the alternative is losing the building entirely.<br>C. agreeing that the theater should close honestly.<br>D. proposing to restore the balcony first.<\/li>\n<li>As used in Text 1, \"alibi\" most nearly means<br>A. a legal defense.<br>B. a cover that stands in for the real thing.<br>C. a monument.<br>D. a promise.<\/li>\n<li>Which choice best describes a difference in how the two texts treat the facade?<br>A. Text 1 treats keeping it as a false form of preservation; Text 2 treats it as the part that can realistically be saved.<br>B. Text 1 wants it demolished; Text 2 wants it fully restored.<br>C. Both treat it as the most important part of the building.<br>D. Text 2 says the facade is unsafe; Text 1 says it is sound.<\/li>\n<li>In Text 2, the sentence \"Critics call this a hollow gesture\" primarily functions to<br>A. concede that the plan is only a gesture.<br>B. state an objection the author is about to answer.<br>C. quote Text 1 directly.<br>D. introduce the cost figures.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Key for Set B, with the build of every wrong choice<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item<\/th>\n<th>Answer<\/th>\n<th>Wrong choices and their builds<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td>A <em>off<\/em> (nothing compared roofs with boxes); C <em>outside<\/em> and <em>inverted<\/em> (Kadan had no boxes, so it tests nothing about them); D <em>off<\/em> (no distances were measured)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2<\/td>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td>A <em>too strong<\/em> (\"prove,\" \"only way\"); C <em>inverted<\/em> (use of 112 boxes and a 41 percent rise support the conclusion); D <em>outside<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3<\/td>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td>A <em>off<\/em>; C <em>outside<\/em> (nothing questions the volunteers); D <em>off<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4<\/td>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td>A <em>irrelevant<\/em> (says nothing about this comparison); C <em>off<\/em> (no effect of orientation is given); D <em>consistent<\/em> (a lower start makes the rise more striking, not less); B supplies a second difference between the towns besides the boxes<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5<\/td>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td>A <em>off<\/em>; C <em>outside<\/em>; D <em>near miss<\/em> (site fidelity is in paragraph 1, but the remark is about study length)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6<\/td>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td>A <em>outside<\/em> (neither says it); C <em>inverted<\/em> for Text 2 and unstated in Text 1; D <em>too strong<\/em> (Text 1's closing is conditional: \"if the town cannot afford\")<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7<\/td>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td>A <em>inverted<\/em> (Text 2 confirms the auditorium is not kept); C <em>inverted<\/em>; D <em>inverted<\/em> (the cost rules it out)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8<\/td>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td>A <em>near miss<\/em> (the literal sense; the sentence is about a wall that pretends); C <em>off<\/em>; D <em>off<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9<\/td>\n<td>A<\/td>\n<td>B <em>too strong<\/em> on both sides; C <em>inverted<\/em> for Text 1; D <em>wrong part<\/em> (the balcony is unsafe, not the facade)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10<\/td>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td>A <em>too strong<\/em> (the author answers the critics, not joins them); C <em>outside<\/em> (no quotation); D <em>off<\/em> (the cost came two sentences earlier)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Score Set B the same way: once for answers, once for evidence. Compare the two evidence scores, not the two totals; the evidence score on the timed set is the number that predicts the next test.<\/p>"},
{"title": "Thirty sentences to unpack", "part": "Appendix", "html": "<p>The sentence-unpacking set the plan assigns in weeks 2, 3, and 11, ten at a time. For each sentence, do three things before you look at the key: underline the word or phrase doing most of the work, write what the sentence claims in plain words, and write one thing a hurried reader would take it to claim that it does not. The key gives all three. Sentences 1 to 10 lean on qualifiers and negation, 11 to 20 on embedded clauses and reported views, 21 to 30 on concession and comparison, though every sentence has more than one thing in it.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Nearly every town that adopted the curfew reported fewer complaints, though few reported fewer incidents.<\/li>\n<li>The committee did not reject the proposal so much as decline to schedule it.<\/li>\n<li>Unless the river falls by Friday, the lower road will stay closed.<\/li>\n<li>Few of the applicants who were interviewed, and none who were not, received an offer.<\/li>\n<li>The study found that students who slept more scored higher, which the authors caution is not evidence that sleep raised the scores.<\/li>\n<li>It is often said, and rarely shown, that smaller classes raise achievement.<\/li>\n<li>The mayor, who had opposed the bridge, now calls it indispensable, although she has not said what changed her mind.<\/li>\n<li>Only after the second audit did the losses, which the first had attributed to theft, appear as accounting errors.<\/li>\n<li>The critics are right that the novel is long; they are wrong that it is slow.<\/li>\n<li>Whatever the survey shows about preferences, it shows nothing about behavior, since no one was observed.<\/li>\n<li>The drug reduced symptoms in most patients, most of the time, for about six hours.<\/li>\n<li>Not all of the funds were spent, and not all of what was spent was spent well.<\/li>\n<li>The engineer conceded that the design was heavier than needed but denied that weight had caused the failure.<\/li>\n<li>That the records are incomplete is certain; that they were destroyed on purpose is a claim no one has supported.<\/li>\n<li>Registrations rose by a third in the year after the fee was dropped, and by a quarter in the year before.<\/li>\n<li>She praised the plan's ambition in terms that left little doubt about her opinion of its arithmetic.<\/li>\n<li>The witness said she saw the car; she did not say she saw who was driving it.<\/li>\n<li>Except in the two coastal counties, where the pattern reversed, turnout fell as rain increased.<\/li>\n<li>The author does not claim the treaty prevented war, only that it postponed the question of who would start one.<\/li>\n<li>If anything, the new schedule made the delays worse, though the report stops short of saying so.<\/li>\n<li>Hardly anyone who finished the course failed the exam, but fewer than half who enrolled finished.<\/li>\n<li>The company's statement, which did not mention the recall, described the quarter as its strongest.<\/li>\n<li>No one disputes that the bridge is old; what is disputed is whether old means unsafe.<\/li>\n<li>Whether or not the policy worked, and the evidence is thin either way, it was popular.<\/li>\n<li>The orchard yielded less than last year but more than any year before it.<\/li>\n<li>Those who objected were not, as the newspaper suggested, mostly newcomers; most had lived in the town for decades.<\/li>\n<li>Rarely has a smaller change produced a louder response, and rarely has the response had less to do with the change.<\/li>\n<li>The data are consistent with the theory, which is not the same as confirming it, since they are consistent with two others as well.<\/li>\n<li>He admitted that the forecast had been wrong but insisted, with some justice, that it had been less wrong than the others.<\/li>\n<li>Until the second shipment arrives, and it may not, the clinic can treat the children but not the adults.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Key<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#<\/th>\n<th>The word doing the work<\/th>\n<th>What it says<\/th>\n<th>What it does not say<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td><em>nearly; though; few<\/em><\/td>\n<td>Most adopting towns got fewer complaints; most did not get fewer incidents.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"The curfew reduced incidents in every town that adopted it.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2<\/td>\n<td><em>not ... so much as<\/em><\/td>\n<td>The proposal was shelved without a vote either way.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"The committee rejected the proposal.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3<\/td>\n<td><em>unless<\/em><\/td>\n<td>The road reopens only if the river falls by Friday.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"The road will reopen on Friday.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4<\/td>\n<td><em>few; none<\/em><\/td>\n<td>Offers went only to interviewed applicants, and to few of them.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"Most interviewed applicants received offers.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5<\/td>\n<td><em>caution; not evidence<\/em><\/td>\n<td>Sleep and scores moved together; the authors do not claim that one caused the other.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"The study showed that more sleep raised scores.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6<\/td>\n<td><em>rarely shown<\/em><\/td>\n<td>The claim is common and weakly supported.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"Smaller classes raise achievement.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7<\/td>\n<td><em>although she has not said<\/em><\/td>\n<td>The mayor reversed her position and has given no reason.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"The mayor explained why she changed her mind.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8<\/td>\n<td><em>only after; which the first had attributed<\/em><\/td>\n<td>The second audit reclassified the losses from theft to error.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"The first audit found accounting errors.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9<\/td>\n<td><em>right that; wrong that<\/em><\/td>\n<td>The author concedes the length and disputes the slowness.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"The author agrees with the critics.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10<\/td>\n<td><em>shows nothing about; since<\/em><\/td>\n<td>The survey can speak to preferences but not to what people did.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"The survey shows nothing useful.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11<\/td>\n<td><em>most; about six hours<\/em><\/td>\n<td>Broad but temporary relief, and not for everyone.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"The drug cured most patients.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12<\/td>\n<td><em>not all; not all<\/em><\/td>\n<td>Some money was unspent and some was misspent.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"The funds were wasted.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13<\/td>\n<td><em>conceded; denied<\/em><\/td>\n<td>The design was overweight; the engineer says that is not why it failed.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"The engineer admitted that the design caused the failure.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>14<\/td>\n<td><em>is certain; no one has supported<\/em><\/td>\n<td>Gaps in the records are established; deliberate destruction is not.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"The records were destroyed on purpose.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>15<\/td>\n<td><em>and by a quarter in the year before<\/em><\/td>\n<td>Registrations were already rising before the fee changed.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"Dropping the fee raised registrations by a third.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16<\/td>\n<td><em>left little doubt; arithmetic<\/em><\/td>\n<td>Praise for the ambition that implies the numbers do not work.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"She endorsed the plan.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>17<\/td>\n<td><em>did not say<\/em><\/td>\n<td>The testimony covers the car, not the driver.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"The witness identified the driver.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>18<\/td>\n<td><em>except; reversed<\/em><\/td>\n<td>Rain lowered turnout inland and raised it on the coast.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"Turnout fell everywhere as rain increased.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>19<\/td>\n<td><em>does not claim; only<\/em><\/td>\n<td>The treaty delayed war; it did not prevent it.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"The treaty prevented war.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>20<\/td>\n<td><em>if anything; stops short<\/em><\/td>\n<td>Delays probably worsened; the report implies it without stating it.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"The report states that the schedule worsened delays.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>21<\/td>\n<td><em>hardly anyone; fewer than half<\/em><\/td>\n<td>Finishers passed; most enrollees did not finish.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"Most people who enrolled passed the exam.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>22<\/td>\n<td><em>which did not mention<\/em><\/td>\n<td>The statement omitted the recall while calling the quarter strong.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"The company denied the recall.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>23<\/td>\n<td><em>no one disputes; what is disputed<\/em><\/td>\n<td>The age is agreed; the inference to danger is not.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"Experts agree that the bridge is unsafe.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>24<\/td>\n<td><em>whether or not; thin either way<\/em><\/td>\n<td>Popularity is established; effectiveness is unknown.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"The policy worked and was popular.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>25<\/td>\n<td><em>less than; more than any<\/em><\/td>\n<td>The second-best year on record.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"The orchard had a poor year.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>26<\/td>\n<td><em>not, as the newspaper suggested<\/em><\/td>\n<td>The newspaper's account is corrected: the objectors were long-time residents.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"The newspaper lied about the objectors.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>27<\/td>\n<td><em>rarely; less to do with<\/em><\/td>\n<td>A small change, a large reaction, and a reaction about something else.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"The change was large and controversial.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>28<\/td>\n<td><em>consistent with; not the same as<\/em><\/td>\n<td>The data do not favor this theory over its rivals.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"The data confirm the theory.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>29<\/td>\n<td><em>with some justice; less wrong<\/em><\/td>\n<td>A wrong forecast, but the least wrong; the author partly agrees with him.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"The author thinks the forecast was right.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>30<\/td>\n<td><em>until; may not; but not<\/em><\/td>\n<td>Adults go untreated for now, and possibly for good.<\/td>\n<td>Not \"The clinic will treat the adults when the shipment arrives next week.\"<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>"},
{"title": "Question families, by test", "part": "Appendix", "html": "<p>Every question stem the tests use, the move it calls for, and the chapter that teaches it. When the clock is running, the stem's verb tells you which one thing to do.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Stem sounds like<\/th>\n<th>Test<\/th>\n<th>The move<\/th>\n<th>Chapter<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Which choice best states the main idea / central claim<\/td>\n<td>SAT, ACT, GRE<\/td>\n<td>Find the author's own sentence, not the loudest one; check the rung<\/td>\n<td>1, 5, 23<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Based on the text, what is true about X<\/td>\n<td>SAT, ACT<\/td>\n<td>Find the sentence; read its qualifiers; eliminate too strong<\/td>\n<td>1, 2<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Which choice most logically completes the text<\/td>\n<td>SAT<\/td>\n<td>Predict the ending from the logic words before you look<\/td>\n<td>2, 11<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Which finding, if true, would most directly support / weaken<\/td>\n<td>SAT, GMAT, GRE<\/td>\n<td>State the warrant; pick the choice that touches it<\/td>\n<td>4, 7, 21, 26<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Which quotation most effectively illustrates the claim<\/td>\n<td>SAT<\/td>\n<td>Match the claim's qualifiers to the quotation's; strength must match<\/td>\n<td>7, 18<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Which choice most effectively uses data from the table/graph<\/td>\n<td>SAT, ACT science<\/td>\n<td>Sentence first, then the exact cell; compute one number; check for a comparison group<\/td>\n<td>20<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>As used in the text, the word most nearly means<\/td>\n<td>SAT, ACT, GRE, AP<\/td>\n<td>Cover the choices, predict a plain word, substitute and read aloud<\/td>\n<td>11, 28<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The author's primary purpose / main purpose<\/td>\n<td>All<\/td>\n<td>Read the last line of your one-line map; eliminate too narrow and too broad<\/td>\n<td>23, 24<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The function of the underlined sentence / paragraph N is to<\/td>\n<td>SAT, ACT, AP, GRE<\/td>\n<td>Delete test; verb family first, then strength<\/td>\n<td>23, 24<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The author mentions X primarily to<\/td>\n<td>All<\/td>\n<td>Delete test; what did the paragraph lose<\/td>\n<td>24<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Which choice best describes the overall structure<\/td>\n<td>SAT, ACT, AP<\/td>\n<td>Name the shape from your map; eliminate wrong order first<\/td>\n<td>23<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Text 1 and Text 2: both authors would agree / how would author 2 respond<\/td>\n<td>SAT, ACT, AP<\/td>\n<td>Find the overlap as a fact; then the point of disagreement in each author's own words<\/td>\n<td>10<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The author's attitude / tone toward X is best described as<\/td>\n<td>All<\/td>\n<td>Find the word that carries the attitude; if none, the level choice<\/td>\n<td>6, 24, 25<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>It can reasonably be inferred that<\/td>\n<td>All<\/td>\n<td>The weakest claim the evidence forces, never a rung higher<\/td>\n<td>4, 7, 21<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The passage suggests that the author would agree with which statement<\/td>\n<td>GRE, AP<\/td>\n<td>Only what the author owns, not what the author reports<\/td>\n<td>5, 10<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Which choice best describes the narrator's point of view / the speaker<\/td>\n<td>AP Lit, SAT literature<\/td>\n<td>Name the point of view; ask whose words the judgments are<\/td>\n<td>25, 29<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The description of X in lines N serves to<\/td>\n<td>AP Lit, SAT literature<\/td>\n<td>Delete test; then what the detail does to the speaker's situation<\/td>\n<td>24, 29<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Strengthen / weaken / assumption / evaluate / inference<\/td>\n<td>GMAT CR, GRE<\/td>\n<td>Write the warrant in six words; read every choice against it<\/td>\n<td>21<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Text completion / sentence equivalence<\/td>\n<td>GRE<\/td>\n<td>Logic words first, predict the blank in plain English, then look; on SE, the pair that match<\/td>\n<td>2, 11, 21<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rhetorical choice: the writer does X in order to<\/td>\n<td>AP Lang<\/td>\n<td>Name the move, the audience, and the effect; commentary says what the choice does<\/td>\n<td>22, 24<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Which choice best describes the speaker's shift in lines N<\/td>\n<td>AP Lit<\/td>\n<td>Find the turn word; name what changes, from what to what<\/td>\n<td>9, 29<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Which statement about the study's method is supported<\/td>\n<td>ACT science, SAT, GRE<\/td>\n<td>Label Q, H, M, R, I, L; read each group as the cause it rules out<\/td>\n<td>26<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The survey / data most strongly support which claim about people<\/td>\n<td>SAT, ACT, GRE<\/td>\n<td>Who was asked; said or did; compared to what; percent of what; who says so<\/td>\n<td>27<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition<\/td>\n<td>SAT, ACT English<\/td>\n<td>Cover the choices; name the relationship in one word; eliminate every other row<\/td>\n<td>33<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The student wants to [goal]. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes<\/td>\n<td>SAT<\/td>\n<td>Mark the goal's verb and object; say what the sentence must contain; true but off is the trap<\/td>\n<td>34, 11<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English<\/td>\n<td>SAT, ACT English<\/td>\n<td>Find the boundary; can each side stand alone; look for the partner of any comma<\/td>\n<td>35<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>"},
{"title": "Root families, the full table", "part": "Appendix", "html": "<p>Chapter 14 teaches fifty roots. These are all 74 families in the program's word banks, with the words the banks draw from each. Learn the root, then the next unfamiliar word in the family half-explains itself.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Root<\/th>\n<th>Means<\/th>\n<th>Words<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>ced, cess<\/td>\n<td>go, yield<\/td>\n<td>concede, precedent, recede, accessible, process, antecedent<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>dict<\/td>\n<td>say<\/td>\n<td>diction, contradict, predict, verdict, dictate, indict<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>duc, duct<\/td>\n<td>lead<\/td>\n<td>deduce, conduct, induce, reduce, abduct, conducive<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>fid<\/td>\n<td>trust<\/td>\n<td>confide, fidelity, diffident, infidel, bona fide<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ject<\/td>\n<td>throw<\/td>\n<td>reject, conjecture, objective, projection, inject, abject<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>loqu, locu<\/td>\n<td>speak<\/td>\n<td>eloquent, colloquial, loquacious, circumlocution, soliloquy<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>mit, miss<\/td>\n<td>send<\/td>\n<td>submit, omission, emit, intermittent, remiss, remit<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>pel, puls<\/td>\n<td>push, drive<\/td>\n<td>compel, repel, impulse, expulsion, propel, compulsory<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>plic, ply<\/td>\n<td>fold<\/td>\n<td>implicit, explicit, complicate, replicate, duplicity, imply<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>spec, spic<\/td>\n<td>look<\/td>\n<td>speculate, conspicuous, introspective, perspective, specious, circumspect<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>tract<\/td>\n<td>pull<\/td>\n<td>abstract, detract, protracted, intractable, retract, tractable<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>vert, vers<\/td>\n<td>turn<\/td>\n<td>subvert, averse, convert, versatile, introvert, controversy<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>voc, vok<\/td>\n<td>call<\/td>\n<td>equivocal, advocate, evoke, vocation, revoke, provocative<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ambi, amphi<\/td>\n<td>both, around<\/td>\n<td>ambivalent, ambiguous, ambidextrous, amphibious<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>equi, equa<\/td>\n<td>equal<\/td>\n<td>equivocal, equanimity, equitable, equivalent, equinox<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>val, vail<\/td>\n<td>strength, worth<\/td>\n<td>prevalent, ambivalent, valid, prevail, invalidate, valor<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>cred<\/td>\n<td>believe<\/td>\n<td>credit, credible, incredulous, credence, creed<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>scrib, script<\/td>\n<td>write<\/td>\n<td>describe, transcribe, inscription, proscribe, circumscribe<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ten, tin, tain<\/td>\n<td>hold<\/td>\n<td>tenuous, pertinent, sustain, tenacious, abstain, detain<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>tend, tens<\/td>\n<td>stretch<\/td>\n<td>contend, tentative, extend, tension, ostensible, portend<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>pos, pon<\/td>\n<td>place<\/td>\n<td>juxtaposition, proponent, impose, posit, depose, component<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>fer<\/td>\n<td>carry<\/td>\n<td>infer, transfer, defer, proliferate, vociferous, confer<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>cap, cept, ceive<\/td>\n<td>take<\/td>\n<td>anticipate, perception, capable, susceptible, intercept, deceive<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>grad, gress<\/td>\n<td>step<\/td>\n<td>gradual, regress, digress, transgress, egress<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>clin<\/td>\n<td>lean<\/td>\n<td>decline, incline, recline, inclination<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>curr, curs<\/td>\n<td>run<\/td>\n<td>current, recur, cursory, precursor, incursion, discursive<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>fac, fect, fic<\/td>\n<td>make<\/td>\n<td>facilitate, effect, superficial, proficient, efficacious, artifice<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>gen<\/td>\n<td>birth, kind<\/td>\n<td>generate, genre, engender, progenitor, congenital, genesis<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>log, logue<\/td>\n<td>word, reason<\/td>\n<td>logos, analogy, eulogy, prologue, epilogue, logical<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>mod<\/td>\n<td>measure<\/td>\n<td>modest, moderate, mode, modicum, commodious<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>nov<\/td>\n<td>new<\/td>\n<td>innovative, novel, renovate, novice<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>path<\/td>\n<td>feeling, suffering<\/td>\n<td>pathos, apathy, empathy, antipathy, pathetic, sympathy<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>port<\/td>\n<td>carry<\/td>\n<td>report, purport, import, portable, deport, comportment<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>rupt<\/td>\n<td>break<\/td>\n<td>disrupt, abrupt, rupture, interrupt, corrupt, erupt<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sent, sens<\/td>\n<td>feel<\/td>\n<td>consensus, sentiment, dissent, sensible, resent, presentiment<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sequ, secu<\/td>\n<td>follow<\/td>\n<td>sequence, consequence, obsequious, subsequent, consecutive, sequel<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sol<\/td>\n<td>alone<\/td>\n<td>solitary, desolate, soliloquy, solitude, sole<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sta, stat, stit<\/td>\n<td>stand<\/td>\n<td>substantiate, static, status, constitute, obstinate, stable<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>struct<\/td>\n<td>build<\/td>\n<td>construe, obstruct, structure, construct, instruct<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>tempor<\/td>\n<td>time<\/td>\n<td>temporary, contemporary, extemporaneous, temporal<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ver<\/td>\n<td>true<\/td>\n<td>verify, verdict, veracity, aver, verisimilitude<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>vid, vis<\/td>\n<td>see<\/td>\n<td>evident, provisional, revise, visionary, improvise, envision<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>viv, vit<\/td>\n<td>live<\/td>\n<td>vivid, viable, revive, vital, convivial, vivacious<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>chron<\/td>\n<td>time<\/td>\n<td>chronic, chronology, synchronize, anachronism, chronicle<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>anthrop<\/td>\n<td>human<\/td>\n<td>anthropology, misanthrope, philanthropy, anthropomorphic<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>bene, bon<\/td>\n<td>good, well<\/td>\n<td>benefit, benevolent, benign, benediction, bonus<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>mal<\/td>\n<td>bad<\/td>\n<td>malign, malevolent, malady, malicious, malfunction<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>bio<\/td>\n<td>life<\/td>\n<td>biology, biography, symbiosis, antibiotic<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>cogn, gnos<\/td>\n<td>know<\/td>\n<td>recognize, cognizant, incognito, diagnosis, agnostic, prognosis<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>corp<\/td>\n<td>body<\/td>\n<td>corporeal, incorporate, corpulent, corps<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>crat, cracy<\/td>\n<td>rule, power<\/td>\n<td>democracy, aristocracy, bureaucrat, autocratic, technocrat<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>dem<\/td>\n<td>people<\/td>\n<td>democracy, demographic, epidemic, endemic, pandemic<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>dys, eu<\/td>\n<td>bad; good<\/td>\n<td>dysfunction, dystopia; euphemism, eulogy, euphoria<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>graph, gram<\/td>\n<td>write<\/td>\n<td>autograph, biography, epigram, demographic, choreography<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>hetero, homo<\/td>\n<td>different; same<\/td>\n<td>heterogeneous, heterodox; homogeneous, homonym<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>jur, jus<\/td>\n<td>law, right<\/td>\n<td>jurisdiction, perjury, justify, jurisprudence, abjure<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>leg, lect<\/td>\n<td>read, choose, law<\/td>\n<td>legible, lecture, eclectic, select, legislate<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>liber<\/td>\n<td>free<\/td>\n<td>liberate, liberal, libertine, liberty<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>luc, lum<\/td>\n<td>light<\/td>\n<td>lucid, elucidate, pellucid, luminous, illuminate<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>man<\/td>\n<td>hand<\/td>\n<td>manual, manipulate, manufacture, manuscript, emancipate<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>mort<\/td>\n<td>death<\/td>\n<td>mortal, mortify, moribund, immortal<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>morph<\/td>\n<td>form, shape<\/td>\n<td>metamorphosis, amorphous, anthropomorphic, morphology<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>nom, nym<\/td>\n<td>name<\/td>\n<td>nominal, nominate, pseudonym, anonymous, misnomer, synonym<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>omni, pan<\/td>\n<td>all<\/td>\n<td>omniscient, omnipotent, omnivorous; panacea, pandemic, panorama<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>pend, pens<\/td>\n<td>hang, weigh, pay<\/td>\n<td>pending, suspend, pensive, compensate, impending, dispense<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>phil<\/td>\n<td>love<\/td>\n<td>philosophy, philanthropy, bibliophile, philharmonic<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>phon<\/td>\n<td>sound<\/td>\n<td>phonetic, cacophony, euphony, symphony, homophone<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>poly<\/td>\n<td>many<\/td>\n<td>polyglot, polymath, polysyllabic, polygon<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>soph<\/td>\n<td>wisdom<\/td>\n<td>philosophy, sophisticated, sophomoric, sophistry<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>spir<\/td>\n<td>breathe<\/td>\n<td>inspire, conspire, respiration, aspire, expire<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>terr<\/td>\n<td>earth<\/td>\n<td>terrain, territory, subterranean, terrestrial, extraterrestrial<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ven, vent<\/td>\n<td>come<\/td>\n<td>convene, intervene, advent, circumvent, contravene, convention<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>vol<\/td>\n<td>wish, will<\/td>\n<td>volition, benevolent, malevolent, voluntary<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>vor<\/td>\n<td>eat<\/td>\n<td>voracious, carnivore, omnivorous, devour<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>Prefixes and suffixes<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Affix<\/th>\n<th>Means<\/th>\n<th>Examples<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>un-, in-, im-, il-, ir-<\/td>\n<td>not<\/td>\n<td>unequal, inevitable, impartial, illegible, irrelevant<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>re-<\/td>\n<td>again, back<\/td>\n<td>revise, recede, retract<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>dis-<\/td>\n<td>apart, not<\/td>\n<td>discrepancy, dismiss, disparity<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>pre-<\/td>\n<td>before<\/td>\n<td>precedent, predict, preclude<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sub-<\/td>\n<td>under<\/td>\n<td>substantiate, subtle, subvert<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>inter-<\/td>\n<td>between<\/td>\n<td>intermittent, interpret, intervene<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>trans-<\/td>\n<td>across<\/td>\n<td>transfer, transient, transcribe<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>con-, com-, col-<\/td>\n<td>with, together<\/td>\n<td>concede, compel, collaborate<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ex-, e-<\/td>\n<td>out<\/td>\n<td>exacerbate, elicit, evoke<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>de-<\/td>\n<td>down, away<\/td>\n<td>decline, deride, deduce<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>pro-<\/td>\n<td>forward, for<\/td>\n<td>provisional, proponent, prosaic<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ambi-, amphi-<\/td>\n<td>both<\/td>\n<td>ambivalent, ambiguous<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>anti-, contra-<\/td>\n<td>against<\/td>\n<td>antithesis, antipathy, contradict<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>circum-, peri-<\/td>\n<td>around<\/td>\n<td>circumspect, circumlocution, perimeter<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>super-, hyper-<\/td>\n<td>above, beyond<\/td>\n<td>superficial, supersede, hyperbole<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ob-<\/td>\n<td>against, in the way<\/td>\n<td>obstruct, obdurate, obstinate<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-tion, -sion<\/td>\n<td>the act or state of<\/td>\n<td>attribution, concession, allusion<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-ity<\/td>\n<td>quality of<\/td>\n<td>ambiguity, equanimity, uniformity<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-ous, -ious<\/td>\n<td>full of<\/td>\n<td>meticulous, garrulous, conspicuous<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-ate<\/td>\n<td>to make, to do<\/td>\n<td>mitigate, corroborate, vacillate<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-ive<\/td>\n<td>tending to<\/td>\n<td>persuasive, decisive, tentative<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-able, -ible<\/td>\n<td>can be<\/td>\n<td>feasible, viable, impregnable<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-ize<\/td>\n<td>to make<\/td>\n<td>scrutinize, emphasize, synthesize<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-ment, -ance, -ence<\/td>\n<td>result or state of<\/td>\n<td>argument, precedence, prevalence<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-fy<\/td>\n<td>to make<\/td>\n<td>justify, verify, mortify<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-ism, -ist<\/td>\n<td>belief, practice; one who<\/td>\n<td>skepticism, iconoclast, pragmatist<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>"},
{"title": "The word banks", "part": "Appendix", "html": "<p>The 283 words and terms in the program's banks, by test, each with its meaning and the near neighbor it is most often confused with. The app adds the parts, an example sentence, and the spaced review. Ten a week, from the root up.<\/p>\n<p><strong>SAT<\/strong> (62). Words the SAT Reading and Writing section tests in context: precise academic vocabulary where a near neighbor is usually among the choices.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word<\/th>\n<th>Means<\/th>\n<th>Not the same as<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>adept<\/td>\n<td>highly skilled at something<\/td>\n<td>expert: skilled and recognized for it<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>alleviate<\/td>\n<td>to make a problem or pain less severe<\/td>\n<td>cure: to remove it entirely<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ambivalent<\/td>\n<td>having mixed or contradictory feelings about something<\/td>\n<td>indifferent: having no feelings about it<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>anecdotal<\/td>\n<td>based on personal accounts rather than systematic data<\/td>\n<td>false: untrue; anecdotal evidence may be true but is thin<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>arbitrary<\/td>\n<td>decided by chance or whim rather than by reason or rule<\/td>\n<td>random: without pattern; arbitrary means without a justifying reason<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>articulate<\/td>\n<td>to express clearly in words; clear and fluent in speech<\/td>\n<td>eloquent: persuasive and graceful, beyond merely clear<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>candid<\/td>\n<td>honest and direct, even when it is awkward<\/td>\n<td>blunt: honest without care for the listener<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>cohesive<\/td>\n<td>holding together as a unified whole<\/td>\n<td>coherent: logically consistent; a group is cohesive, an argument coherent<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>complacent<\/td>\n<td>satisfied with oneself in a way that ignores danger or need<\/td>\n<td>content: satisfied, without the blindness<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>comprehensive<\/td>\n<td>covering all or nearly all of a subject<\/td>\n<td>thorough: careful in detail; a thorough study of one case is not comprehensive<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>concede<\/td>\n<td>to admit something is true, usually reluctantly<\/td>\n<td>claim: to assert as true<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>conciliatory<\/td>\n<td>intended to calm anger or win back goodwill<\/td>\n<td>apologetic: admitting fault<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>consensus<\/td>\n<td>general agreement among a group<\/td>\n<td>unanimity: agreement of every member<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>constrain<\/td>\n<td>to limit or restrict<\/td>\n<td>prevent: to stop entirely<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>contemporary<\/td>\n<td>living or happening at the same time; of the present<\/td>\n<td>modern: of recent times, as opposed to old<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>conventional<\/td>\n<td>based on what is usually done; traditional<\/td>\n<td>conservative: resistant to change<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>corroborate<\/td>\n<td>to confirm or support with additional evidence<\/td>\n<td>prove: to establish beyond doubt<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>deride<\/td>\n<td>to mock or express contempt for<\/td>\n<td>criticize: to find fault, with or without contempt<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>deter<\/td>\n<td>to discourage from acting, usually by fear of consequences<\/td>\n<td>prevent: to make impossible<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>diminish<\/td>\n<td>to make or become smaller or less important<\/td>\n<td>eliminate: to remove<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>disclose<\/td>\n<td>to make known something previously secret or unknown<\/td>\n<td>confess: to admit wrongdoing<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>discrepancy<\/td>\n<td>a difference between things that should agree<\/td>\n<td>contradiction: a direct logical conflict<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>disparity<\/td>\n<td>a great and often unfair difference<\/td>\n<td>difference: any distinction<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>dubious<\/td>\n<td>doubtful; of questionable value or truth<\/td>\n<td>false: shown to be untrue<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>elusive<\/td>\n<td>hard to find, catch, or pin down<\/td>\n<td>obscure: hard to understand<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>empirical<\/td>\n<td>based on observation or experiment rather than theory<\/td>\n<td>anecdotal: based on individual stories<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>equivocal<\/td>\n<td>open to more than one interpretation; deliberately unclear<\/td>\n<td>ambiguous: unclear, not necessarily on purpose<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>erratic<\/td>\n<td>irregular, unpredictable in movement or behavior<\/td>\n<td>eccentric: unconventional in a consistent way<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>exacerbate<\/td>\n<td>to make a bad situation worse<\/td>\n<td>aggravate: to make worse or to annoy<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>facilitate<\/td>\n<td>to make an action or process easier<\/td>\n<td>cause: to bring about<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>feasible<\/td>\n<td>possible to do with the resources available<\/td>\n<td>desirable: worth doing<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>fluctuate<\/td>\n<td>to rise and fall irregularly<\/td>\n<td>decline: to move in one direction, down<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>hypothesis<\/td>\n<td>a proposed explanation that can be tested<\/td>\n<td>theory: an explanation that has survived testing<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>implicit<\/td>\n<td>suggested without being stated directly<\/td>\n<td>explicit: stated directly<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>indifferent<\/td>\n<td>having no interest or concern<\/td>\n<td>impartial: fair, taking no side<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>inevitable<\/td>\n<td>certain to happen; unavoidable<\/td>\n<td>likely: probable but not certain<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>inherent<\/td>\n<td>existing as a permanent, essential part of something<\/td>\n<td>inherited: received from a predecessor<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>innovative<\/td>\n<td>introducing new ideas or methods<\/td>\n<td>effective: producing the intended result, new or not<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>meticulous<\/td>\n<td>showing great attention to detail<\/td>\n<td>thorough: complete, covering everything<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>mitigate<\/td>\n<td>to make less severe or harmful<\/td>\n<td>prevent: to stop from happening<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>negligible<\/td>\n<td>so small as to be not worth considering<\/td>\n<td>minor: small but worth noting<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>nuance<\/td>\n<td>a subtle difference in meaning or tone<\/td>\n<td>detail: a small particular fact<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>objective<\/td>\n<td>not influenced by personal feelings; based on facts<\/td>\n<td>neutral: taking no side<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>paradox<\/td>\n<td>a statement that seems self-contradictory but may be true<\/td>\n<td>contradiction: a statement that cannot be true<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>plausible<\/td>\n<td>seeming reasonable or probable, whether or not it is true<\/td>\n<td>probable: likely to be true<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>pragmatic<\/td>\n<td>dealing with things practically rather than by principle<\/td>\n<td>practical: useful, workable<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>precedent<\/td>\n<td>an earlier case that guides later decisions<\/td>\n<td>example: any illustrative case<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>prevalent<\/td>\n<td>widespread in a particular area or time<\/td>\n<td>common: frequent<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>reconcile<\/td>\n<td>to make two things consistent, or to restore friendly relations<\/td>\n<td>resolve: to settle a problem<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>redundant<\/td>\n<td>no longer needed, or saying the same thing twice<\/td>\n<td>repetitive: repeated, perhaps usefully<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>refute<\/td>\n<td>to prove a statement or theory wrong<\/td>\n<td>deny: to say it is false, without proof<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>scrutinize<\/td>\n<td>to examine closely and critically<\/td>\n<td>observe: to watch<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>skeptical<\/td>\n<td>not easily convinced; doubting<\/td>\n<td>cynical: assuming bad motives<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>speculative<\/td>\n<td>based on conjecture rather than knowledge<\/td>\n<td>hypothetical: proposed for the sake of argument<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>substantiate<\/td>\n<td>to provide evidence to support a claim<\/td>\n<td>assert: to state firmly without evidence<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>superficial<\/td>\n<td>on the surface only; lacking depth<\/td>\n<td>brief: short<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sustain<\/td>\n<td>to keep going over time; to support<\/td>\n<td>maintain: to keep in the same state<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>tentative<\/td>\n<td>not certain or fixed; provisional<\/td>\n<td>hesitant: slow to act from doubt<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>undermine<\/td>\n<td>to weaken gradually, often from below<\/td>\n<td>contradict: to state the opposite<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>unprecedented<\/td>\n<td>never done or known before<\/td>\n<td>unusual: rare<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>viable<\/td>\n<td>capable of working or surviving<\/td>\n<td>valuable: worth a lot<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>warrant<\/td>\n<td>to justify or call for; also, the reasoning that links evidence to a claim<\/td>\n<td>guarantee: a promise<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>ACT<\/strong> (46). The ACT tests vocabulary almost entirely in context: common words with a precise meaning in the sentence. These are the ones that decide close questions.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word<\/th>\n<th>Means<\/th>\n<th>Not the same as<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>abate<\/td>\n<td>to become less intense or widespread<\/td>\n<td>end: to stop completely<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>accommodate<\/td>\n<td>to provide room for; to adapt to<\/td>\n<td>tolerate: to put up with<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>acute<\/td>\n<td>sharp, severe, or sudden; also, keen in perception<\/td>\n<td>chronic: long-lasting<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>adverse<\/td>\n<td>harmful or unfavorable<\/td>\n<td>averse: having a strong dislike (describes a person)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>advocate<\/td>\n<td>to publicly support; a person who does so<\/td>\n<td>defend: to protect from attack<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ambiguous<\/td>\n<td>open to more than one interpretation<\/td>\n<td>vague: unclear or imprecise; an ambiguous sentence has two clear meanings<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>anticipate<\/td>\n<td>to expect, and often to prepare for<\/td>\n<td>hope: to want something to happen<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>assert<\/td>\n<td>to state firmly, with or without evidence<\/td>\n<td>argue: to give reasons for<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>attribute<\/td>\n<td>to regard as caused by<\/td>\n<td>blame: to attribute something bad<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>bias<\/td>\n<td>a tendency that prevents fair judgment<\/td>\n<td>opinion: a view, which may be fair<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>characterize<\/td>\n<td>to describe the distinctive nature of<\/td>\n<td>criticize: to point out faults<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>cite<\/td>\n<td>to quote or refer to as evidence<\/td>\n<td>mention: to refer to in passing<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>coherent<\/td>\n<td>logical and consistent; holding together<\/td>\n<td>clear: easy to understand<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>compel<\/td>\n<td>to force, or to be irresistibly convincing<\/td>\n<td>persuade: to convince by argument<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>conform<\/td>\n<td>to comply with rules or standards; to be similar<\/td>\n<td>obey: to follow a command<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>contend<\/td>\n<td>to argue or assert; also, to struggle<\/td>\n<td>contest: to dispute<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>context<\/td>\n<td>the circumstances that surround an event or a word<\/td>\n<td>background: prior history, one kind of context<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>convey<\/td>\n<td>to communicate or carry<\/td>\n<td>imply: to suggest without stating<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>criteria<\/td>\n<td>standards by which something is judged<\/td>\n<td>goals: outcomes aimed at<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>deliberate<\/td>\n<td>done on purpose; or, to consider carefully<\/td>\n<td>careful: cautious<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>derive<\/td>\n<td>to obtain from a source; to reason out<\/td>\n<td>invent: to create new<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>devise<\/td>\n<td>to plan or invent by thinking<\/td>\n<td>discover: to find something already there<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>discern<\/td>\n<td>to perceive or recognize something not obvious<\/td>\n<td>notice: to see something plain<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>distinct<\/td>\n<td>clearly different; recognizable<\/td>\n<td>distinctive: characteristic, identifying<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>elicit<\/td>\n<td>to draw out a response or reaction<\/td>\n<td>illicit: illegal (a different word)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>emphasize<\/td>\n<td>to give special importance to<\/td>\n<td>exaggerate: to overstate<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>evoke<\/td>\n<td>to bring a feeling, memory, or image to mind<\/td>\n<td>describe: to state in words<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>explicit<\/td>\n<td>stated clearly and in detail, leaving nothing implied<\/td>\n<td>obvious: easily seen, stated or not<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>fundamental<\/td>\n<td>forming a necessary base or core<\/td>\n<td>important: mattering a great deal, basic or not<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>imply<\/td>\n<td>to suggest without stating (the writer does this)<\/td>\n<td>infer: to conclude from evidence (the reader does this)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>inadequate<\/td>\n<td>not enough or not good enough<\/td>\n<td>absent: not present at all<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>indicate<\/td>\n<td>to point out or show; to be a sign of<\/td>\n<td>prove: to establish<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>infer<\/td>\n<td>to conclude from evidence and reasoning<\/td>\n<td>assume: to take as true without evidence<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>integrate<\/td>\n<td>to combine parts into a whole<\/td>\n<td>include: to make part of a group<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>interpret<\/td>\n<td>to explain the meaning of<\/td>\n<td>translate: to change into another language<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>justify<\/td>\n<td>to show to be right or reasonable<\/td>\n<td>explain: to make clear, without defending<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>modify<\/td>\n<td>to make partial changes to<\/td>\n<td>replace: to put something else in its place<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>notion<\/td>\n<td>an idea or belief, often vague<\/td>\n<td>theory: a tested explanation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>persist<\/td>\n<td>to continue firmly despite difficulty<\/td>\n<td>remain: to stay<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>perspective<\/td>\n<td>a particular point of view<\/td>\n<td>opinion: a judgment; a perspective is where the judgment is made from<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>presume<\/td>\n<td>to suppose to be true without proof<\/td>\n<td>assume: to take for granted; presume implies some basis<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>prominent<\/td>\n<td>important, famous, or standing out<\/td>\n<td>dominant: controlling<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>qualify<\/td>\n<td>to limit or modify a statement; also, to meet a standard<\/td>\n<td>weaken: to make less strong<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>relevant<\/td>\n<td>closely connected to the matter at hand<\/td>\n<td>important: mattering, whether connected or not<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>subtle<\/td>\n<td>so delicate or precise as to be hard to notice<\/td>\n<td>minor: small in importance<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>valid<\/td>\n<td>well founded; logically sound<\/td>\n<td>true: in accordance with fact; a valid argument can have false premises<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>AP Language<\/strong> (46). The terms the AP English Language exam uses for how writers argue. Knowing the term is worth little; naming what it does in a specific sentence is the skill.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word<\/th>\n<th>Means<\/th>\n<th>Not the same as<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>allusion<\/td>\n<td>an indirect reference to something the audience knows<\/td>\n<td>reference: a direct citation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>analogy<\/td>\n<td>an extended comparison used to explain or argue<\/td>\n<td>metaphor: a compressed comparison<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>anaphora<\/td>\n<td>repeating a word or phrase at the start of successive clauses<\/td>\n<td>repetition: any repeating<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>antithesis<\/td>\n<td>contrasting ideas in balanced, parallel phrasing<\/td>\n<td>contrast: any difference<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>appeal to authority<\/td>\n<td>supporting a claim by citing an expert or institution<\/td>\n<td>ethos: the speaker's own credibility<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>appeal to emotion<\/td>\n<td>persuasion that works on the audience's feelings rather than reasons<\/td>\n<td>manipulation: emotional appeal used to bypass judgment<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>audience<\/td>\n<td>the readers or listeners the writer is trying to move<\/td>\n<td>public: everyone<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>begging the question<\/td>\n<td>assuming the conclusion in a premise<\/td>\n<td>raising the question: prompting a question, which is a different thing<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>claim<\/td>\n<td>a statement the writer wants the audience to accept<\/td>\n<td>fact: a verifiable statement<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>commentary<\/td>\n<td>the writer's own explanation of how evidence supports the claim<\/td>\n<td>evidence: the quoted support<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>concession<\/td>\n<td>acknowledging a point on the other side<\/td>\n<td>surrender: giving up the argument<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>connotation<\/td>\n<td>the feeling or association a word carries beyond its definition<\/td>\n<td>denotation: the literal meaning<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>counterargument<\/td>\n<td>the opposing position a writer must address<\/td>\n<td>objection: one specific point against<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>deductive reasoning<\/td>\n<td>reasoning from general principles to a particular conclusion<\/td>\n<td>inductive reasoning: from particulars to a general claim<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>diction<\/td>\n<td>the writer's choice of words<\/td>\n<td>vocabulary: the words available<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ethos<\/td>\n<td>an appeal based on the speaker's credibility or character<\/td>\n<td>authority: being in charge<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>euphemism<\/td>\n<td>a mild or indirect word used in place of a blunt one<\/td>\n<td>understatement: deliberately saying less, for effect<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>exigence<\/td>\n<td>the situation or need that prompts a piece of writing<\/td>\n<td>topic: what it is about<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>false dilemma<\/td>\n<td>presenting two options as the only ones when others exist<\/td>\n<td>dichotomy: a real division into two<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>hedging<\/td>\n<td>language that limits the strength of a claim<\/td>\n<td>equivocating: using ambiguity to avoid commitment<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>hyperbole<\/td>\n<td>deliberate exaggeration for effect<\/td>\n<td>lie: a false statement meant to deceive<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>inductive reasoning<\/td>\n<td>reasoning from particular observations to a general conclusion<\/td>\n<td>deductive reasoning: from general rule to particular case<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>irony<\/td>\n<td>a gap between what is said and what is meant, or expected and what occurs<\/td>\n<td>sarcasm: irony meant to wound<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>juxtaposition<\/td>\n<td>placing two things side by side to highlight contrast<\/td>\n<td>comparison: noting likeness and difference<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>kairos<\/td>\n<td>the opportune moment for an argument; timing<\/td>\n<td>exigence: the problem that prompts the argument<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>line of reasoning<\/td>\n<td>the sequence of claims and evidence that leads to the conclusion<\/td>\n<td>outline: the structure of sections<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>loaded language<\/td>\n<td>words chosen for their emotional charge<\/td>\n<td>strong language: forceful, which may be fair<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>logos<\/td>\n<td>an appeal to logic, evidence, and reasoning<\/td>\n<td>data: the evidence itself<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>non sequitur<\/td>\n<td>a conclusion that does not follow from what preceded it<\/td>\n<td>contradiction: a conclusion that opposes the premise<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>parallelism<\/td>\n<td>using the same grammatical structure for related ideas<\/td>\n<td>balance: a general evenness<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>pathos<\/td>\n<td>an appeal to the audience's emotions<\/td>\n<td>sympathy: feeling for someone<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>premise<\/td>\n<td>a statement from which a conclusion is drawn<\/td>\n<td>evidence: facts offered in support; a premise may be unsupported<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>purpose<\/td>\n<td>what the writer wants the audience to think, feel, or do<\/td>\n<td>message: the main idea<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>qualifier<\/td>\n<td>a word that limits the strength or scope of a claim<\/td>\n<td>hedge: a vague softening<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>rebuttal<\/td>\n<td>a response that counters an argument<\/td>\n<td>refutation: a rebuttal that succeeds<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>refutation<\/td>\n<td>showing an opposing argument to be wrong or weak<\/td>\n<td>rebuttal: a reply, strong or weak<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>rhetoric<\/td>\n<td>the art of using language to persuade a particular audience for a particular purpose<\/td>\n<td>style: the manner of writing, apart from its aim<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>rhetorical question<\/td>\n<td>a question asked for effect, not for an answer<\/td>\n<td>inquiry: a real question<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>straw man<\/td>\n<td>a distorted version of an opponent's argument, set up to be knocked down<\/td>\n<td>counterargument: the opponent's real position<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>syntax<\/td>\n<td>the arrangement of words into sentences<\/td>\n<td>grammar: the rules<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>synthesis<\/td>\n<td>combining several sources into one coherent argument<\/td>\n<td>summary: restating one source<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>thesis<\/td>\n<td>the central claim an argument defends<\/td>\n<td>topic: the subject, without a claim<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>tone<\/td>\n<td>the writer's attitude toward the subject or audience<\/td>\n<td>mood: the feeling produced in the reader<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>tone shift<\/td>\n<td>a change in the writer's attitude within a text<\/td>\n<td>turn: a change in direction of argument<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>understatement<\/td>\n<td>presenting something as less significant than it is<\/td>\n<td>modesty: a personal quality<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>warrant<\/td>\n<td>the reasoning that connects evidence to a claim<\/td>\n<td>evidence: the support itself<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>AP Literature<\/strong> (46). Terms for how literature works, each defined by what it lets you say about a passage. A term you cannot attach to a line is a term you do not have yet.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word<\/th>\n<th>Means<\/th>\n<th>Not the same as<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>allegory<\/td>\n<td>a narrative whose characters and events stand for ideas<\/td>\n<td>fable: a short tale with a moral<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>allusion<\/td>\n<td>an indirect reference to another text or event<\/td>\n<td>quotation: a direct reproduction<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ambiguity<\/td>\n<td>openness to more than one valid reading<\/td>\n<td>vagueness: lack of clarity<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>apostrophe<\/td>\n<td>an address to an absent person, an object, or an idea<\/td>\n<td>personification: giving a thing human qualities; apostrophe speaks to it<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>blank verse<\/td>\n<td>unrhymed iambic pentameter<\/td>\n<td>free verse: no fixed meter or rhyme<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>caesura<\/td>\n<td>a pause within a line of verse<\/td>\n<td>stanza break: a pause between groups of lines<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>characterization<\/td>\n<td>how a writer reveals a character<\/td>\n<td>description: appearance only<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>connotation<\/td>\n<td>the associations a word carries beyond its definition<\/td>\n<td>denotation: the dictionary meaning<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>couplet<\/td>\n<td>two consecutive rhymed lines<\/td>\n<td>stanza: any grouped lines<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>diction<\/td>\n<td>word choice<\/td>\n<td>vocabulary: available words<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>dramatic irony<\/td>\n<td>the audience knows something a character does not<\/td>\n<td>situational irony: the outcome is the opposite of what was expected<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>elegy<\/td>\n<td>a poem of mourning or serious reflection on loss<\/td>\n<td>eulogy: a speech of praise for the dead<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>enjambment<\/td>\n<td>a line of verse running on into the next without a pause<\/td>\n<td>end-stop: a line that pauses at its end<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>epiphany<\/td>\n<td>a sudden realization by a character or reader<\/td>\n<td>surprise: an unexpected event<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>foil<\/td>\n<td>a character whose contrast highlights another<\/td>\n<td>rival: an opponent<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>foreshadowing<\/td>\n<td>hints about what will happen later<\/td>\n<td>prediction: an explicit forecast<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>free indirect discourse<\/td>\n<td>a character's thoughts rendered in the narrator's voice, without quotation marks<\/td>\n<td>dialogue: speech in quotation marks<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>free verse<\/td>\n<td>poetry without regular meter or rhyme<\/td>\n<td>prose: ordinary writing<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>imagery<\/td>\n<td>language that appeals to the senses<\/td>\n<td>description: any account of appearance<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>in medias res<\/td>\n<td>beginning a story in the middle of the action<\/td>\n<td>flashback: a return to earlier events<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>irony<\/td>\n<td>a gap between appearance and reality, words and meaning, or expectation and outcome<\/td>\n<td>coincidence: chance alignment<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>lyric<\/td>\n<td>a short poem expressing a speaker's feelings<\/td>\n<td>narrative poem: a poem that tells a story<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>metaphor<\/td>\n<td>a comparison that says one thing is another<\/td>\n<td>simile: a comparison using like or as<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>meter<\/td>\n<td>the pattern of stressed and unstressed syllables<\/td>\n<td>rhythm: the felt flow<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>mood<\/td>\n<td>the atmosphere or feeling a passage creates in the reader<\/td>\n<td>tone: the speaker's attitude<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>motif<\/td>\n<td>a recurring element that develops a theme<\/td>\n<td>theme: the idea itself<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>narrator<\/td>\n<td>the voice telling a story<\/td>\n<td>author: the writer<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>octave and sestet<\/td>\n<td>the eight-line and six-line parts of a Petrarchan sonnet<\/td>\n<td>quatrain: a four-line group, as in the Shakespearean sonnet<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>paradox<\/td>\n<td>a statement that seems contradictory but reveals a truth<\/td>\n<td>contradiction: a plain conflict<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>pastoral<\/td>\n<td>literature idealizing rural life<\/td>\n<td>nature writing: close observation of the natural world, idealized or not<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>personification<\/td>\n<td>giving human qualities to nonhuman things<\/td>\n<td>anthropomorphism: treating something as fully human<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>point of view<\/td>\n<td>the perspective from which a story is told<\/td>\n<td>opinion: a judgment<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>prosody<\/td>\n<td>the patterns of rhythm and sound in verse; also, in reading, phrasing aloud<\/td>\n<td>meter: the fixed pattern; prosody is the whole sound of the lines<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>quatrain<\/td>\n<td>a four-line stanza or group<\/td>\n<td>couplet: two lines<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>simile<\/td>\n<td>a comparison using like or as<\/td>\n<td>metaphor: a direct comparison<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>soliloquy<\/td>\n<td>a speech in which a character, alone, voices thoughts aloud<\/td>\n<td>monologue: a long speech, possibly to others<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sonnet<\/td>\n<td>a fourteen-line poem with a turn<\/td>\n<td>stanza: any group of lines<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>speaker<\/td>\n<td>the voice of a poem, not necessarily the poet<\/td>\n<td>poet: the author<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>stanza<\/td>\n<td>a grouped set of lines in a poem<\/td>\n<td>paragraph: a grouped set of prose sentences<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>symbol<\/td>\n<td>an object or action that stands for something larger<\/td>\n<td>sign: a direct indicator<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>synecdoche<\/td>\n<td>a part standing for the whole, or the whole for a part<\/td>\n<td>metonymy: an associated thing standing for the thing itself<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>theme<\/td>\n<td>a central idea the work explores, stated as a claim<\/td>\n<td>topic: the subject in one word<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>tone<\/td>\n<td>the attitude of the speaker or narrator toward the subject<\/td>\n<td>mood: the atmosphere the reader feels<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>unreliable narrator<\/td>\n<td>a narrator whose account the story's details do not fully support<\/td>\n<td>first-person narrator: any <em>I<\/em> narrator, reliable or not<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>verisimilitude<\/td>\n<td>the appearance of being true or real<\/td>\n<td>realism: a literary movement; verisimilitude is a quality any work may have<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>volta<\/td>\n<td>the turn in a poem's argument or feeling<\/td>\n<td>climax: the point of highest tension<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>GRE<\/strong> (59). Harder academic vocabulary for text completion and sentence equivalence, where the sentence's logic, not the word's rarity, decides the answer.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word<\/th>\n<th>Means<\/th>\n<th>Not the same as<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>abrogate<\/td>\n<td>to repeal or do away with formally<\/td>\n<td>abdicate: to give up a position<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>abstruse<\/td>\n<td>hard to understand; obscure<\/td>\n<td>complex: having many parts<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>acerbic<\/td>\n<td>sharp and forthright in tone<\/td>\n<td>caustic: sharp enough to wound<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>alacrity<\/td>\n<td>brisk and cheerful readiness<\/td>\n<td>haste: speed, with or without willingness<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ameliorate<\/td>\n<td>to make better or more tolerable<\/td>\n<td>cure: to fix completely<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>anachronism<\/td>\n<td>something out of its proper time<\/td>\n<td>archaism: a deliberately old-fashioned usage<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>anomaly<\/td>\n<td>something that deviates from the norm<\/td>\n<td>error: a mistake<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>apathy<\/td>\n<td>lack of interest or concern<\/td>\n<td>calm: a settled state<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>arcane<\/td>\n<td>understood by few; secret or obscure<\/td>\n<td>ancient: very old<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>assuage<\/td>\n<td>to make an unpleasant feeling less intense<\/td>\n<td>satisfy: to fulfill fully<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>austere<\/td>\n<td>severe, plain, without comfort or decoration<\/td>\n<td>strict: enforcing rules<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>aver<\/td>\n<td>to state or assert as fact<\/td>\n<td>allege: to assert without proof, often of wrongdoing<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>belie<\/td>\n<td>to give a false impression of; to contradict<\/td>\n<td>reveal: to show<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>bombastic<\/td>\n<td>high-sounding but with little meaning; inflated<\/td>\n<td>grandiose: impressively large or ambitious<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>capricious<\/td>\n<td>given to sudden, unpredictable changes<\/td>\n<td>flexible: able to adapt<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>censure<\/td>\n<td>to express strong disapproval formally<\/td>\n<td>censor: to suppress<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>circumspect<\/td>\n<td>wary; considering all circumstances before acting<\/td>\n<td>timid: fearful; the circumspect are careful, not afraid<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>cogent<\/td>\n<td>clear, logical, and convincing<\/td>\n<td>loud: forceful in volume<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>didactic<\/td>\n<td>intended to teach, often too obviously<\/td>\n<td>pedantic: showing off learning; didactic writing wants to improve you<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>disparate<\/td>\n<td>essentially different; not comparable<\/td>\n<td>diverse: varied, which may still be comparable<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>dogmatic<\/td>\n<td>asserting opinions as if they were facts<\/td>\n<td>confident: sure, with reason<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ebullient<\/td>\n<td>overflowing with enthusiasm<\/td>\n<td>optimistic: expecting good outcomes, quietly or not<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>eclectic<\/td>\n<td>drawing from a wide range of sources or styles<\/td>\n<td>random: without principle of selection<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>enervate<\/td>\n<td>to weaken or drain of energy<\/td>\n<td>energize: to strengthen (its opposite)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ephemeral<\/td>\n<td>lasting a very short time<\/td>\n<td>brief: short<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>equanimity<\/td>\n<td>calm and composure under stress<\/td>\n<td>indifference: not caring<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>equivocate<\/td>\n<td>to use ambiguous language to avoid commitment<\/td>\n<td>hedge: to soften a claim<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>esoteric<\/td>\n<td>intended for or understood by a small group<\/td>\n<td>obscure: hard to find<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>extol<\/td>\n<td>to praise highly<\/td>\n<td>approve: to accept<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>fastidious<\/td>\n<td>very attentive to detail; hard to please<\/td>\n<td>meticulous: careful in detail, without the distaste<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>garrulous<\/td>\n<td>excessively talkative<\/td>\n<td>eloquent: speaking well<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>hackneyed<\/td>\n<td>overused and therefore lacking force<\/td>\n<td>traditional: long established, which may still have force<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>iconoclast<\/td>\n<td>a person who attacks cherished beliefs or institutions<\/td>\n<td>critic: anyone who finds fault<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>impartial<\/td>\n<td>treating all sides equally; unbiased<\/td>\n<td>indifferent: not caring about the outcome<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>impetuous<\/td>\n<td>acting quickly without thought<\/td>\n<td>decisive: acting firmly after thought<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ineffable<\/td>\n<td>too great or extreme to be expressed in words<\/td>\n<td>indescribable: hard to describe; ineffable is beyond words in kind<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>intransigent<\/td>\n<td>refusing to compromise<\/td>\n<td>principled: acting from principle, which may compromise<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>laconic<\/td>\n<td>using very few words<\/td>\n<td>terse: short to the point of rudeness<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>mercurial<\/td>\n<td>subject to sudden changes of mood<\/td>\n<td>volatile: liable to change rapidly, of things as well as moods<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>obdurate<\/td>\n<td>stubbornly refusing to change<\/td>\n<td>firm: steady<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>obfuscate<\/td>\n<td>to make obscure or unclear, usually on purpose<\/td>\n<td>confuse: to make someone unable to think clearly<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ostensible<\/td>\n<td>stated or appearing to be true, but perhaps not<\/td>\n<td>obvious: plainly true<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>paucity<\/td>\n<td>scarcity; smallness of amount<\/td>\n<td>absence: none at all<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>pedantic<\/td>\n<td>excessively concerned with minor details or rules<\/td>\n<td>precise: accurate<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>pellucid<\/td>\n<td>transparently clear in style or meaning<\/td>\n<td>simple: easy, which may be unclear<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>perfunctory<\/td>\n<td>done with minimal effort or interest<\/td>\n<td>efficient: done well and quickly<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>polemic<\/td>\n<td>a strong verbal or written attack on a position<\/td>\n<td>critique: a reasoned evaluation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>prevaricate<\/td>\n<td>to speak evasively, avoiding the truth<\/td>\n<td>procrastinate: to delay acting<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>prosaic<\/td>\n<td>dull, ordinary, lacking imagination<\/td>\n<td>practical: useful<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>quixotic<\/td>\n<td>idealistic to an impractical degree<\/td>\n<td>ambitious: aiming high, practically or not<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>recalcitrant<\/td>\n<td>stubbornly resisting authority or control<\/td>\n<td>independent: self-directed<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sanguine<\/td>\n<td>optimistic, especially in a difficult situation<\/td>\n<td>cheerful: in a good mood<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>specious<\/td>\n<td>seeming plausible but actually wrong<\/td>\n<td>false: untrue; specious arguments look true<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>tacit<\/td>\n<td>understood or implied without being stated<\/td>\n<td>explicit: stated plainly<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>tenuous<\/td>\n<td>very weak or slight<\/td>\n<td>tentative: not yet fixed<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>trenchant<\/td>\n<td>vigorous and incisive in expression<\/td>\n<td>harsh: severe; trenchant criticism cuts cleanly<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>vacillate<\/td>\n<td>to waver between options<\/td>\n<td>deliberate: to weigh carefully<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>venerate<\/td>\n<td>to regard with deep respect<\/td>\n<td>admire: to regard with approval<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>verbose<\/td>\n<td>using more words than needed<\/td>\n<td>eloquent: fluent and persuasive, at any length<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>Test verbs<\/strong> (24). The verbs that question stems and answer choices are built from. Each one sits on a rung: suggest is weaker than show, concede is not surrender, imply is what the writer does and infer is what you do. Misreading the verb is the most common way to miss an item whose passage you understood.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word<\/th>\n<th>Means<\/th>\n<th>Not the same as<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>acknowledge<\/td>\n<td>to accept the truth or existence of, often a point against one's own side<\/td>\n<td>admit: to confess, usually to fault<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>advance<\/td>\n<td>to put forward a claim or proposal<\/td>\n<td>prove: to establish<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>assert<\/td>\n<td>to state firmly, as fact, without necessarily offering proof<\/td>\n<td>prove: to establish with evidence<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>attribute<\/td>\n<td>to credit something to a cause or source<\/td>\n<td>blame: to attribute fault<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>caution<\/td>\n<td>to warn against reading too much into something<\/td>\n<td>deny: to say it is false<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>challenge<\/td>\n<td>to call into question<\/td>\n<td>reject: to refuse<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>characterize<\/td>\n<td>to describe the nature of<\/td>\n<td>evaluate: to judge the worth of<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>cite<\/td>\n<td>to refer to a source as evidence<\/td>\n<td>quote: to repeat the exact words<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>concede<\/td>\n<td>to admit that a point is true, usually one that weighs against you<\/td>\n<td>surrender: to give up the whole position<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>contend<\/td>\n<td>to argue or assert, in a dispute<\/td>\n<td>agree: to share a position<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>corroborate<\/td>\n<td>to support with additional, independent evidence<\/td>\n<td>repeat: to say again; a second survey by the same team corroborates less<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>counter<\/td>\n<td>to respond with an opposing argument<\/td>\n<td>contradict: to assert the opposite<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>dismiss<\/td>\n<td>to reject as unworthy of consideration<\/td>\n<td>refute: to prove wrong<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>imply<\/td>\n<td>to suggest without stating<\/td>\n<td>infer: what the reader does with what the writer implies<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>infer<\/td>\n<td>to conclude from evidence and reasoning<\/td>\n<td>imply: what the writer does<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>posit<\/td>\n<td>to put forward as a basis for argument<\/td>\n<td>prove: to establish<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>qualify<\/td>\n<td>to limit the force or scope of a claim<\/td>\n<td>contradict: to deny<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>refute<\/td>\n<td>to prove a claim wrong<\/td>\n<td>rebut: to argue against, successfully or not<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>speculate<\/td>\n<td>to form a view without firm evidence<\/td>\n<td>hypothesize: to propose for testing<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>substantiate<\/td>\n<td>to provide evidence that supports a claim<\/td>\n<td>assert: to claim without support<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>suggest<\/td>\n<td>to indicate or point toward, weakly<\/td>\n<td>demonstrate: to show clearly<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>temper<\/td>\n<td>to moderate or soften a claim<\/td>\n<td>withdraw: to take back<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>undermine<\/td>\n<td>to weaken the basis of a claim<\/td>\n<td>refute: to prove wrong outright<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>underscore<\/td>\n<td>to emphasize<\/td>\n<td>introduce: to bring in for the first time<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>"},
{"title": "Glossary", "part": "Appendix", "html": "<p>Every term this book uses with a specific meaning, with the chapter where it is taught.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term<\/th>\n<th>Meaning<\/th>\n<th>Chapter<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>After-action review<\/td>\n<td>A short, blameless review after any timed section: what was supposed to happen, what happened, why, what to keep and change<\/td>\n<td>19, 31<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alternative cause<\/td>\n<td>Another difference between groups, or another event, that could explain the result<\/td>\n<td>4, 26<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Attitude verb<\/td>\n<td>A purpose verb that carries a feeling (<em>mock, praise, lament<\/em>); needs a word in the passage that carries the feeling<\/td>\n<td>24<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Automaticity<\/td>\n<td>Reading words accurately without conscious effort, which frees attention for meaning<\/td>\n<td>13, 16<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Base rate<\/td>\n<td>The size of the group a percentage is taken from; a large share of a small group can be a small effect<\/td>\n<td>27<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Blank verse<\/td>\n<td>Unrhymed iambic pentameter<\/td>\n<td>29<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Calibration<\/td>\n<td>How well a reader's confidence matches their accuracy<\/td>\n<td>18<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Certain-and-wrong<\/td>\n<td>A missed item the reader rated as certain; the most useful error to study<\/td>\n<td>18, 19<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Claim<\/td>\n<td>The statement a passage, or a reader, is trying to establish<\/td>\n<td>7<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Closed-book summary<\/td>\n<td>Two sentences written with the passage covered: what it showed, what it could not show<\/td>\n<td>17<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Comma splice<\/td>\n<td>Two independent clauses joined by a comma alone; an error<\/td>\n<td>35<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Comparison group<\/td>\n<td>A group that did not get the change; what turns a sequence into evidence of a cause<\/td>\n<td>4, 20, 26<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Concession<\/td>\n<td>Granting a point to the other side before answering it<\/td>\n<td>8<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Consistent with<\/td>\n<td>Does not contradict; weaker than <em>supports<\/em><\/td>\n<td>20, 26<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Decoding<\/td>\n<td>Turning print into words; one of the two terms of the Simple View<\/td>\n<td>13, 16<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Delete test<\/td>\n<td>Covering a detail and asking what the paragraph lost, to find its purpose<\/td>\n<td>24<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Distractor<\/td>\n<td>A wrong answer choice built to be tempting<\/td>\n<td>11<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enjambment<\/td>\n<td>A sentence running past the end of a line without a pause<\/td>\n<td>29<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evidence rate<\/td>\n<td>The share of answers whose cited sentence actually supports them<\/td>\n<td>30<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Free indirect discourse<\/td>\n<td>A third-person narrator using a character's words or judgments without quotation marks<\/td>\n<td>25<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fused sentence<\/td>\n<td>Two independent clauses run together with no mark at all; an error<\/td>\n<td>35<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Function<\/td>\n<td>What a paragraph or detail does for the passage, as opposed to what it says<\/td>\n<td>23, 24<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hedge<\/td>\n<td>A word that limits a claim's reach: <em>may, often, suggests<\/em><\/td>\n<td>3, 8<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hint ladder<\/td>\n<td>Help in rising order, from where to look toward the answer, given only as far as needed<\/td>\n<td>13, 19, 31<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Iambic pentameter<\/td>\n<td>A line of five unstressed-stressed pairs, about ten syllables<\/td>\n<td>29<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Independent clause<\/td>\n<td>A subject and verb that can stand alone as a sentence; the test is a period after it, read aloud<\/td>\n<td>35<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inference<\/td>\n<td>A claim the passage supports but does not state; the weakest such claim is usually the answer<\/td>\n<td>4, 7<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interested source<\/td>\n<td>A speaker with a stake in the conclusion<\/td>\n<td>5, 27<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Irony<\/td>\n<td>A gap between what is said and what is meant or shown<\/td>\n<td>6<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Language comprehension<\/td>\n<td>Understanding spoken language; the other term of the Simple View<\/td>\n<td>13, 15<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Long read<\/td>\n<td>A whole book at the edge of what you can read, 150 minutes a week, logged and closed out after each session<\/td>\n<td>36, 37<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Margin translation<\/td>\n<td>A six-to-fifteen-word modern paraphrase of a long sentence<\/td>\n<td>28<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Module<\/td>\n<td>One of the two timed halves of an SAT section; the second is harder or easier depending on the first<\/td>\n<td>32<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nonessential element<\/td>\n<td>A phrase that can be lifted out without changing the claim; set off by a matching pair of commas, dashes, or parentheses<\/td>\n<td>35<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>One-line map<\/td>\n<td>A verb-first note of six words or fewer naming each paragraph's job<\/td>\n<td>23<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Oral reading fluency<\/td>\n<td>Reading aloud with speed, accuracy, and phrasing; usually measured in words correct per minute<\/td>\n<td>16<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Overclaim<\/td>\n<td>A statement that reaches further than its evidence<\/td>\n<td>3, 7, 18<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Periodic sentence<\/td>\n<td>A sentence that holds its main verb until late<\/td>\n<td>28<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Periodization<\/td>\n<td>Planning training in phases: base, build, peak, taper<\/td>\n<td>13, 19<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Point of view<\/td>\n<td>The position a story is told from: first, second, or third person, limited or omniscient<\/td>\n<td>25<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prosody<\/td>\n<td>Reading aloud with the phrasing and stress that show understanding<\/td>\n<td>16<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Qualifier<\/td>\n<td>A small word that sets how far a claim goes: <em>nearly, most, only, unless<\/em><\/td>\n<td>2<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rebuttal<\/td>\n<td>The answer to a concession or objection<\/td>\n<td>8<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Retrieval practice<\/td>\n<td>Recalling something before looking it up; stronger for memory than rereading<\/td>\n<td>17<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rhetorical synthesis<\/td>\n<td>An SAT item that gives notes and a goal; the right sentence is the one that does the goal, not the one with the most facts<\/td>\n<td>34<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Root and affix<\/td>\n<td>The meaning-bearing parts of a word; the base and the prefixes or suffixes around it<\/td>\n<td>14<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rung<\/td>\n<td>A position on a calibration ladder, from weakest to strongest<\/td>\n<td>7, 18<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Seam<\/td>\n<td>The specific way a wrong answer departs from the passage<\/td>\n<td>11<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Selection<\/td>\n<td>Who ends up in a sample, and how that differs from the group the claim is about<\/td>\n<td>27<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Self-report<\/td>\n<td>What people say they did or felt, as opposed to what records show<\/td>\n<td>27<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Simple View of Reading<\/td>\n<td>Reading comprehension is the product of decoding and language comprehension<\/td>\n<td>Introduction, 13<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spaced review<\/td>\n<td>Revisiting material at growing intervals<\/td>\n<td>13, 17<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Speaker<\/td>\n<td>The voice of a poem or argument, which is not the same as the author<\/td>\n<td>5, 9, 29<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transition<\/td>\n<td>A word that names how a sentence stands to the one before it: contrast, concession, cause, example, and the rest<\/td>\n<td>33<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Turn<\/td>\n<td>The point where a passage or poem changes direction<\/td>\n<td>23, 29<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unreliable narrator<\/td>\n<td>A narrator whose account the details of the story do not fully support<\/td>\n<td>25<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Warrant<\/td>\n<td>The unstated link that makes evidence support a claim<\/td>\n<td>7, 21<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>"},
{"title": "Sources and further reading", "part": "Appendix", "html": "<p>Part Two states findings from reading and learning research in plain language without footnotes. The studies and summaries behind each claim are listed here by author and year so that a reader, a parent, or a reviewer can check them. Authors, titles, venues, and years have been checked against the published records; confirm page numbers and exact figures against the originals before print.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tabwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Claim in the book<\/th>\n<th>Source to check<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Reading comprehension is decoding times language comprehension (the Simple View)<\/td>\n<td>Gough and Tunmer, 1986, \"Decoding, reading, and reading disability,\" <em>Remedial and Special Education<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Knowledge of the subject outweighs general reading skill (the baseball study)<\/td>\n<td>Recht and Leslie, 1988, \"Effect of prior knowledge on good and poor readers' memory of text,\" <em>Journal of Educational Psychology<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The gap in words read per year between strong and weak readers<\/td>\n<td>Anderson, Wilson, and Fielding, 1988, \"Growth in reading and how children spend their time outside of school,\" <em>Reading Research Quarterly<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Comprehension strategies help and then saturate<\/td>\n<td>Willingham, 2006, \"The usefulness of brief instruction in reading comprehension strategies,\" <em>American Educator<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Eye movements cap reading speed; skimming is a different skill<\/td>\n<td>Rayner, 1998, \"Eye movements in reading and information processing: 20 years of research,\" <em>Psychological Bulletin<\/em>; Rayner and colleagues, 2016, \"So much to read, so little time,\" <em>Psychological Science in the Public Interest<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Writing about reading improves comprehension<\/td>\n<td>Graham and Hebert, 2010, <em>Writing to Read<\/em>, Carnegie Corporation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Repeated oral reading builds fluency<\/td>\n<td>National Reading Panel, 2000, <em>Teaching Children to Read<\/em>, fluency chapter<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Oral reading fluency norms by grade<\/td>\n<td>Hasbrouck and Tindal, 2017, <em>An Update to Compiled ORF Norms<\/em>, Behavioral Research and Teaching, University of Oregon<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Retrieval beats rereading (the testing effect)<\/td>\n<td>Roediger and Karpicke, 2006, \"Test-enhanced learning,\" <em>Psychological Science<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spaced practice beats massed practice<\/td>\n<td>Cepeda, Pashler, Vul, Wixted, and Rohrer, 2006, \"Distributed practice in verbal recall tasks,\" <em>Psychological Bulletin<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interleaved practice beats blocked practice<\/td>\n<td>Rohrer and Taylor, 2007, \"The shuffling of mathematics problems improves learning,\" <em>Instructional Science<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Which study techniques work, and which only feel like they do<\/td>\n<td>Dunlosky, Rawson, Marsh, Nathan, and Willingham, 2013, \"Improving students' learning with effective learning techniques,\" <em>Psychological Science in the Public Interest<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A small set of prefixes covers most prefixed words<\/td>\n<td>White, Sowell, and Yanagihara, 1989, \"Teaching elementary students to use word-part clues,\" <em>The Reading Teacher<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The share of English academic vocabulary built from Latin and Greek parts<\/td>\n<td>Nagy and Anderson, 1984, \"How many words are there in printed school English?\" <em>Reading Research Quarterly<\/em>; Nagy and Townsend, 2012, \"Words as tools,\" <em>Reading Research Quarterly<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sleep consolidates memory<\/td>\n<td>Walker, 2017, <em>Why We Sleep<\/em>, for the general reader; Rasch and Born, 2013, \"About sleep's role in memory,\" <em>Physiological Reviews<\/em>, for the research<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Calibration and confidence judgments in learning<\/td>\n<td>Koriat, 1997, \"Monitoring one's own knowledge during study,\" <em>Journal of Experimental Psychology: General<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reading on screens costs some comprehension on informational text, more under time pressure<\/td>\n<td>Delgado, Vargas, Ackerman, and Salmerón, 2018, \"Don't throw away your printed books: A meta-analysis on the effects of reading media on reading comprehension,\" <em>Educational Research Review<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The six long reads in chapter 37<\/td>\n<td>Douglass, 1845; Washington, 1901; Du Bois, 1903; Madison, 1787 and 1788; Thoreau, 1854; Austen, 1813. All public domain; full texts at Project Gutenberg, and the Federalist papers at the Library of Congress<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Test formats stated in Parts Three and Eight (GMAT Focus verbal: 23 questions in 45 minutes, with up to three answers changed at the end; GRE verbal: sections of 12 and 15 questions; SAT Reading and Writing: two adaptive modules of 27 questions in 32 minutes each; ACT reading: 36 questions in 40 minutes) are as published by the test makers at the time of writing and change; confirm against GMAC, ETS, ACT, and the College Board before relying on them.<\/p>"}
]};
